      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>t.,;',</p>
        <p>COLONEL PILKINGTON, .M.P., HECKLED.</p>
        <p>Earlestown Electors and Chinese Labour.</p>
        <p>On Monday night a meeting was held at the Earles- town Town Hall under the auspices of the Newton Division Conservative Association, when Mr. C. B. F. Borron presided, and was supported by Colonel R. Pilkington, M.P. for the division, Mr. Courthope Wilson (Liverpool), and a number of local gentlemen. Mr. W. Collingwood proposed a resolution approving of the reconsideration of the fiscal system, and to bring the Empire more closely together. Mr. G. Goff seconded. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Colonel IL Pilkiniiton, in supporting referred to the foreign policy of this Government, and said it stood for the liberties of the people and against the greatest tyrant Power that existed in the world. Colonel Pilkington next dealt with the question of Chinese labour in South Africa, during which he was heckled by a section of the audience. He urged that it was on the strength of the representations of Chambers of Commerce trade councils Free Church councils, whose repr4entations he closely examined, that induced him to vote in favour of the introduction of Chinese labour, and so to help to save South Africa from bankruptcy. He subsequently touched upon the fiscal question, and said that, whilst fifty years ago England was regarded as the workshop of the world, she was now importing £149,000,000 of manufactured and semi- manufactured articles. Were that work done in England it would represent a working population of a million or 'telt* f some flvelmillions,and the wage-earning capacity of the same would be an enormous factor in averting present industrial condiiions. Mr. Courthope Wilson asserted that Colonel Pilking- ton's'interests were bound up with Lancashire,and that he could not run away from the constituency like a man who could carry all his belongings in a handbag. The resolution was passed without dissent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BLOOD AND SKIN DISEASES.</head>
        <p>BLOOD AND SKIN DISEASES.</p>
        <p>BLOOD IS LIFE, LET IT BE PURE.</p>
        <p>Blotches, pimples on the face and body, scrofula, sores, itching and redness of the skin, tettez and eczema are symptoms which indicate impure blood and skin diseases. If your blood is pure - your skin will be clear and healthy looking ; the direct cause of skin eruption is found in the blood. Purify the blood and you purify and clear the skin. The purifying and healing properties of VKlo's SEA- WEED Tome do this admirably. Treatment for a week or two will be so convincing as to place a cure even in the worst cases beyond doubt. The old way' was to take sulphur, sarsaparilla, etc., the new way is to take SEAWEED, which is infinitely superior. VENO'S SEA WEED TONIC not only purifies but enriches the blood. It acts constitutionably by strengthening the indigestion and increasing assimilation and. nutrition. Ask for VEND'S SEAWEED TONIC. Price Is. 11d. and 2s. 9d.,at Chemists everywhere.</p>
        <p>Feodora Machnow, a Russian giant who stands 9ft. Sin. in his socks and weighs 32 stone, has arrived in London for exhibition at the hippodrome. His hand is 19 inches long, his foot 2.1 'inches long and his head three-quarters of a yard round. He is 23 years old, and was of normal size till he was seven. He sleeps a great deal and eats largely. In a case before Mr. Justice Swiufen &amp;tap a medical witness stated, as the outcome of his experience in agricultural districts, that softening of the brain was caused by the lack of brain exercise. The unusal incident of a lady performing a marriage ceremony occurred at Blackburn on Tuesday,when Mrs. Lewis, the well-known Temperance advocate, married a young couple from her Temperance mission in the Congregational Church. The pastor, Rev. Joseph IDugdale, assisted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>r 3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>St. Paul's and All Saints; Church House.</head>
        <p>St. Paul's and All Saints; Church House.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Rinks and Robinson as secretaries of this institution, have just issued their annual report. It states that the main object in a Church House is to create a spirit of fellowship among its members so that they may join in their games with good temper and earnestness. This object has been fully attained, and there has been no occasion to rebuke any member for . the use of obscene language, and never in their games has there been any sign of gambling, a charge which is so often hurled against clubs of a similar nature. The I members are benefited, as they have the p:ivilege of social intercourse with one another, and the secretaries congratulate themselves that the parish of St. Paul possesses such a Church House, the importance of which is being felt more and more in a working parish. The club is increasing its members, having enrolled 80 during the past year. There have been several billiard handi- caps, which have been very interesting to the members and also profitable to the club. There have also been matches with other clubs in the town and district, and these have been the means of bringing Churchmen in touch with each other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>St. Luke's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Luke's Church.</p>
        <p>Success attended the annual congregational tea party in connection with this church which took place in the schoolroom on Monday evening. The entertainment which followed wis presided over by the Rev. E.C.E. Carleton, who said that on previous occasions they had generally had with them Mr. John Peers and Mr. Percy Randall, who brightened the proceedings by taking part in them. They were very sorry to lose such good friends He thanked those who had contributed trays, and those who had helped at the tea and entertainment. Songs were given during the evening by the choir, the Misses Longdin and Ball and Mr. Barnes; recitations by Miss A. Partington and Mrs. Riley; A fancy dance by Miss Partington's pupils; a tambourine dance by Miss A. Kenyon ; an instrumental quartet by the Misses Pooley and Warren; and a humorous song by Mr. Forshaw ; the concluding item being an amusing dialogue by several members of the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CHASE FOR A BRIDE. A Reporter's Success.</head>
        <p>CHASE FOR A BRIDE. A Reporter's Success.</p>
        <p>NOT often (says a Manchester evening paper) has a reporter to interview a Bride on her Wedding morning ' but a Manchester pressman recently attempted this delicate task. He reached his destination only in time to examine the confetti which had greeted the depart- ing couple. But a week later the persistent reporter found the newly-wedded pair, Mr. and Mrs. Ridgard, at their home, 29, Walton-street, Miles Platting, His errand was to obtain from the Bride some parti- culars of a matter which had reached the Editor's ears. In reply to questions Mrs. Ridgard said: "I shall be twenty years of age next week. Three or four years ago I suffered terribly from a weak heart and anxruia. Various remedies were prescribed for me, but I only grew weaker and weaker. I had no appetite. Life was a burden. I was so weak and ill that if I only walked across the garden I was exhausted. I was little more than skin and bone."</p>
        <p>"There has evidently been a great change !" The blushing bride rejoined : "About six months afo I was induced by friends to try Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. I had often heard of their cures, and have a friend who suffered, like me, from anmmia, and was cured after two boxes of these pills. "After the second box, I, too, began to feel an improvement, and this increased with each succeeding box. My appetite became stronger; I could isleep better ; I felt fresher in the morning. In short, new life and new strength had been given me. My recovery was steady and gradual. I have taken nine boxes alto- gether, and now I am quite well and strong again." This bride of a week looked as happy as she is healthy; and Mrs. Willans, her mother, gladly added her own testimony to the value of Dr. Williams' pink pills, which had cured her, she said, of bronchitis and biliousness.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Winans, a healthy and buxom widow, was, like her daughter, full of praise for the cures which had been effected in her own household by this medicine. It is by making new blood that these pills are able to cure so many different ailments—anzemia in the case of Mrs. Ridgard and her friend, bronchitis as well as bile in the case of Mrs. Willans. Dr. Williams' medicine co., Hol- born-viaduct, London, send a box of the genuine pills post free for two and ninepence ; but they can be had of all medicine dealers, provided care is taken to see that the package bears the full name, Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people—seven words. It is well known that substitutes never cure, and they should be firmly refused. Consumption, decline, eczema, fits, gout, heart disease, rheumatism, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, St. Vitus' dance, are among the other ailments the genuine pills have cured. Ladies, who know why they need new blood oftener than their brothers and husbands, will find this medicine a revelation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>YOUNG MEN'S BIBLE CLASS SOCIAL.—On the invita- tion of Mr. Councillor Brotigh, the members of the Padgate Wesleyan Young Men's Bible Class together with their lady h friends spent on Saturday a very pleasant social evening. At 5.30 about 50 ladies and gentlemen assembled in the Padgate schoolroom for tea. The: tables were very prettily arranged and the tea was thoroughly enjoyed and proved a successful commencement to the evening's amusements. It was decided that the evening should be divided into two portions, a concert being arranged for the first part and the second to he spent in games. A splendid programme was soon arranged for the concert which was opened by Mrs. Cotterill who sang "Just s song at twilight "with very deep feeling. Later in the eveninr, Mrs. Cotterill sang" Sleep, my love, sleep" with beautiful effect, for which she was recalled. Miss Gandy also rendered two songs, which were eueedingly well received, her second effort gaining an encore. Mr. B. L. Pearce contributed two welcome items to the programme, the second time singing "I am the Bandolero " by request. Mr. H. i Todd, a young member of the Bit&amp; Class, gave two violin solos which were rendered very skilfully and with good effect. The humorous portion of the pro- gramme was left entirely in the hands of Mr. Cornelius Furness, who was most enthusiastically received. His name appeared three times on the short programme but he had to appear six. Mr. Furness kept his audience in ' one continual roar of laughter with his really funny 'sketches, and his visit to Padgate will not be easily forgotten. The second portion of the evening was spent in a very sociable manner, young and old entering into the games with equal vigour. Game after game was commenced and the evening which everyone present enjoyed all too' soon passedjaway. During the evening Mr. Ernest Wilkes acted as an efficient accompaniest both for the concert items and for the games. Mr. Brough at the close was heartily thanked for his kind- ness in providing the pleasant evening and in his reply he expressed the hope that his class would continue to prosper in the way it was at present doing. The meet- ing broke up about 10.15 and many pleasant memories f will be retained of the evening's enjoyments.</p>
        <p>:</p>
        <p>,t -CU EZ D BY zrim_ IBUFC.</p>
        <p>:'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>AN ITALIAN SUBMARINE.</head>
        <p>AN ITALIAN SUBMARINE.</p>
        <p>Theqtalian submarine Delfino is built of steel plates over an inch thick. Of cigar-shape, her length is about 78ft. and her beam about 9ft. Her displacement varies according to the extent of submersion from 95 to 107 tons. Her engines work by electricity from 300 accumulators. She has three screw propellers, one aft for going ahead or astern, and two above for submersion and emersion. The turret allows of a look-out when the boat is sunk. She has two torpedo tubes forward. Her oxygen supply only serves for twelve hours in summer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>GROS L FE-BELT.</head>
        <p>THE GROS L FE-BELT.</p>
        <p>The Gros life-belt, whic ;I; attracting notice in France, consists, sa: s the Globe, of four small at pockets or gas-bags, connected by a tube and enclosed in a vest of gauze, which is put on under the ordinary vest. Connected with the tube are two metal cylinders holding carbide of calcium, which generates acetylene gas in contact with water. When a person wearing the belt falls overboard the sea water acting on the carbide generates enough gas to inflate the pockets or gas-bags and buoy him up. • Experiments have shewn that the life-belt, which can be worn with an ordinary dress without being noticed, supports and saves persons who cannot swim when they plunge over- board. It only weighs a few ounces,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>RADIO-ACTIVE MINERALS.</head>
        <p>RADIO-ACTIVE MINERALS.</p>
        <p>Among the principal radio-active minerals may be mentioned thorite and orangite. Both of them have been examined by M. Curie. These two minerals are analogous as regards their chemical composition, but they are distinguished from each other, says the Scientific American, by their exterior aspect and the different amounts of thorium which they contain. As to thorite, it is a hydrated sili- cate of thorium which contains about 60 per cent. of oxide accompanied by a great number of bodies, among which are oxides of iron, manganese, calcium, uranium, magnesium and lead, with potassium and sodium compounds and stannic acid. This mineral is obtained principally in the neigh- bourhood of Brevig, Norway. In the natural state the thorite is found in the form of amorphous masses Whose colour varies from chestnut brown to blackish brown. It is found but rarely in the crystallised state; in this case it occurs in • dode- cahedral crystals. In general the thorite which occurs in Norway has a resinous lustre and a conehoidal fracture. When reduced to thin plates it is translucent, and sometimes even transparent. It is in one of the specimens found at Brevig that Berzelius discovered thorium in 1828. Madame Curie examined a great number of specimens of thorite. The following figures shew the radio. activities of these different specimens, taking metallic uranium as unity : Uranium, 10; thorite from Love, Sweden 0.58; different thorites, 0.04; 0.13, 0•57, 0.62. These determinations were made with an electrometer method which is very precise. It consists in measuring the current which passes in a condenser formed of two plates, on the lower of which is placed the test substance. . -</p>
        <p>The second mineral, orangite, is a variety of thorite. It always accompanies the latter, and it is also found at Brevig, Norway. However, its colour is different. It is either orange-yellow or orange- brown. Its proportion of oxide of thorium va!les from 70 to 75 per cent. The distinctive character- istics of this mineral are the same as for thorite. As the mineral is richer in thoridin it is also more active, and some samples which were found shewed a relatively high activity. The result of a certain number of measurements, taking uranium as unity, gives the values 0437, 0.68, 0.99, and 1.10.</p>
        <p>LOW TEMPERATURE PHENOMENA. ' The "new low temperature phenomena,* on which Sir James Dewar recently addressed a brilliant audience at the Royal Institution, were chiefly those that are displayed by charsoal when it is subjected to the temperature, of liquid air or liquid hydrogen. Sir James first shewed that at low temperatures there were four kinds of carbon —graphite, cocoanut carbon, alder carbon, and sugar carbon—which were capable of</p>
        <p>ABSORBING ENORMOUS QUANTITIES 0! OASES. Oxygen, nitrogen, and coal gas were absorbed in this way. Similarly he made by these means a large carbon vacuum tube. He shewed that if some cocosalut charcoal were' placed in a closed tube, and this tube then • immersed in liquid air, the charcoal absorbed the air in the tube with such avidity that an almost perfect mercury vacuum was obtained. This experiment he repeated in a different Storm with a number of small vacuum tubes, in which there were residues of oxygen, nitrogen, hydrogen, argon and helium. In connec- tion with these vacuum tubes was a small quantity of charcoal. The nitrogen tube, for example, exhibited the usual violet glow when under ordinary conditions an elearic current was passed through it. When, however, the tube with its charcoal attachment was</p>
        <p>DIPPED IN LIQUID ATP, the absorptive power of the charcoal became so much increased that it began to absorb the minute trace of nitrogen still remaining in the vacuum tube; and consequently the glow in the tube passed through various stages of attenuated brilliance, till ultimately the vacuum became so nearly perfect that the electric spark could not pass through at all.- When the vacuum tube was removed from contact with the cooling influence the changes made themselves visible in the reverse order. The oxygen vacuum tube exhibited a similar series of trans- formations; but in the case of the hydrogen tube the absorptive power of the charcoal, though greatly increased, did not become high enough to make the tube</p>
        <p>• SO PERFECT A VACUUM that it would refuse to conduct the spark. In the argon tube the brightness diminished, but there Iv as a residue of brilliancy due to the presence of helium in combination with the argon. The helium tuhe proved, of course, of as great an obstinacy— imbed, of greater than that exhibited by the ydrogen vacuum tube. Charcoal could be made t v absorb hydrogen completely at the temperature Ci liquid hydrogell, remarked the lecturer; but in ( :our to ilce it completely absorb helium gas in ;: vacuum tube one would have to go down td the</p>
        <p>Tl•z:P:thcAli.:l;l,. OF F.ItCZEN 11YDROOEN, I'l ,i hydrogen. 'nut temperature would be only lo-c 1;. trot:: aLsolate zero—that is to .•, it ‘‘....x1i0 15(!eg. above the lowest degree of Li I.AL ii:.•za;bLa LoneVe to be possible in the Abother t-x:relnelv interesting series of ,I;e%%eu how 111 -the little instruments et itutioinctCrs the vanes could be made to go iotnd by d:pping them, when their interior was coo:let:Ltd Nvitli a tune cootaining charcoal, into liquid air. The vanes, which were quite still at ordinary temperature, began to go round as soon as the chilled charcoal made the TACl4llll3ia Whial they revolved more complete.</p>
        <p>embroidered in black and green silks and fine cord. The belt is of drawn silk, like the bodice, and is finished with slide and buckle of gun metal and green enamel. The short sleeves are of silk like the bodice, and are rucked up the inside seam, and caught again on the outside with two rows of gathering threads; the drooping cuffs are overlaid with cloth, carrying out the yoke design, and are lined with accordion-pleated green chiffon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>COOKERY INSTRUCTION FOR NURSES.</head>
        <p>COOKERY INSTRUCTION FOR NURSES.</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of the Bethnal Green Guardians a report was presented from the examiners of the London County Council, which stated that sixteen of the nurses at the infirmary entered for the practical cookery examination, and that of these fourteen gained first-class and two second-class marks. The examiner added that the nurses worked neatly and well, and appeared to be keenly interested in what they were doing. Several of the guardians expressed their satisfaction with the report, but a doctor protested against the lessons being given, and said that a nurse's duty was "to tend the sick and dying, and not to cook daintily." As a portion of the duty of tending the sick is to see that the food prepared for them is of an appetising and suitable kind, the Hospital thinks that cookery instruction should be given wherever it can be. In private nursing it is almost indispensable, and as so many probationer d in ' hospitals and workhouse infirmaries contemplate I taking up private nursing, the inclusion of cookery lessons in the curriculum is obviously an advantage likely to be appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>AN UP-TO-DATE GOWN.</head>
        <p>AN UP-TO-DATE GOWN.</p>
        <p>In our illustration we have a glimpse of the style of gown we are likely to see in the near future. Our model may be realised in taffetas tourle in a faint shade of mauve. The bodice, which is gathered up towards the bend of the figure, opens over a vest of tucked Hese. The corsage belt is also of rucked material, edged with drappings of mauve panne, the upper straps being</p>
        <p>carried right up to meet the opening of the vest. The lace which it used as a central panel on the skirt is carried up under the straps as far as the high point of the belt. The full skirt is finished with three flounces headed by a panne strap. The sleeves are quite new and distinctive. The silk part reaches to the elbow, and is very full round the shoulder, the rest being composed of lace closely fitting the arm from elbow to wrist, and edged with panne straps. WHAT IS ENGLISH BEAUTY' ?</p>
        <p>What is a beautiful woman? In England, says the Lady, the question is more controversial than in any other country, for there is no regular type of English beauty.,/One gathers from mid-Victorian novels and poetry that fair hair and blue eyes were once considered indispensable. Nowadays a woman's hair may be of what, colour it—or her hairdresser—pleases.</p>
