        <p>BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME.</p>
        <p>MMICIIO XAMNTX.</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>xxiv. RUN-THROUGHS.—Continued.</p>
        <p>The ordinary player when playing a run- through stroke almost invariably strikes his ball high up irrespective of its distance from the ,object ball. As already shown, in an earlier article, this high hitting of the cue ball is un- necessary when the object ball is some distance • away, and in long-distance run-through strokes the cue ball should always be struck low down. When, however, the object ball is very near, the cue ball must be hit high up or at least above the centre), especially if it has any distance to travel after running through , the object ball. Quite an ordinary player can play run-through cannons when the eue, and object balls are 5 or 6 inches apart, but as the distance between the balls is reduced the ordinary player finds the run-through increasingly difficult, and when one ball is only two inches from the other.he finds it impossible to play the run-through without a • push. When the balls are not. more than one inch apart only a very capable player can play a fair stroke. The difficulty in svell -stroke is to get enough run on to the cue 1.);;Il whilst still avoiding any push. Often and often in praying such a stroke the push is avolded, but the cue ball only travels a few inches after contact, and fails to reach the second object ball, or perhaps the cannon may be niakle. but the -cue ball little more than reaches the second ball, and a still more difficult position is left for the next stroke. There can be no better practice for these close run-through strokes than to begin with a position where the run-through is easy -enough, and to gradually reduce the distanc3 'between the cue ball and first object ball.</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM EP</p>
        <p>Diagram 59 shows a series of such positions. in each case the object balls are the same dis- -tance apart, but the respective distances of the •cue ball from the red are 7,6, 5,4, 3, and 2 inches. Many players when quite close to an 4object ball (say, only half an inch away) play, an undoubtedly foul stroke, and yet maintain, and in all sincerity, too, that the ,stroke was not a push, because they did not play, to pilsh the balls. If a good player inadvertently plays a push stroke, he knows it at Once, for he feels the weight of two balls for one fleeting moment -at the end of his cue. A poorer player with a less sensitive touch can, however, easily distin- guish between a fair stroke and a push in all -close run-through positions in the following manner: When the stroke is a fair one the cue ball will almost momentarily stop at the instant of contact, and then run on, and it will -always travel much more slowly than the object ball. When,, however, the stroke is a push, the two balls will travel at nearly the same speed. In order to avoid a push when playing a very Close run-through (cue ball and object ball two inches, or less apart) the forward movement of the cue must be arrested at almost the very moment it strikes the-ball, yet at the same time a smart little blow must be given the ball in order that it may carry sufficient forward rota- tion to enable it to run on some distance after its full collision with the object ball. This arrest- ing of the cue's forward movement at just the</p>
        <p>With the cue ball so close to the 'object ball it would, be impossible for any player to hit the cue ball smartly enough to give it plenty of f or- ;ward rotation, and at the same time arrest the cue in time to prevent the stroke being a push. Bateman; )however, plays this particular - run- through in a manner quite different frolic any other run-through stroke. He does not arrest or try to arrest the cue at the moment of con- tact. Instead he depresses the butt of the cue and strikes the ball as high up as possible and lets the cue go forward, but high up over the balls. In fact, immediately the cue touches the ball it describes a curve in the air very much after the style shown on diagram 61. If the stroke be played in this manner it is guile possible to get sufficient run on to the cue ball to make the cannon without the slightest semblance of a push, for the cue curving up- wards can be made to leave the object ball prac- tically at the very instant that it strikes it. The stroke is, however, an exceedingly difficult one, as really good amateuss will find if they attempt it. In trying this stroke a player will find that often in his anxiety to avoid a push he will strike the cue ball so high up that hie cue will glance off the ball, thus making the stroke a mise-caie, or in his eagerrneesto give the cue the neeessary upward curve he will mit hit the object hall at aIL When the one and object balls are not too far away from the cushion a. rousse-stroke-will gener- ally be far easier than this most dilfisolt run- through strake, and. it is pretty sate 430.saY that any player who is not sufficiently accomplished ta 'Way msassit.ixtrukamwiU _Etna +...1%1.-.eirstaa. =UN.</p>
