      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>CONSTIPATION AND PILES.</head>
        <p>CONSTIPATION AND PILES.</p>
        <p>Cured by Bile Beans</p>
        <p>WHEN HOSPITAL TREATMENT FAILED.</p>
        <p>When doctors' medicine and hospital treatment have failed, Bile Beans in scores of places have succeeded in effecting a cure. This is because they are entirely different and superior to ordinary medicine. Absolute proof of this statement is reported by the "British Times and Mirror." A reporter of that paper recently interviewed Mrs. Ellen Kate Friend, of 32, Stephen-street, Whitehall,, Bristol, who described her remarkable cure of constipation and bleeding piles by Bile Beans. She said:—" After an irregularity of -the system, constipation and bleeding piles commenced, and as the doctor was unable to cure them, I grew very weak. After two years of this I became too feeble to continue my work, so I gave up my situation. I was so weak I had to use a stick when walking. My weakness continued month after month and year after year. Sometimes, of course, I was a little better, and then I should be worse again. Eventually I came to be married, but my parents took a doctor's opinion specially as to whether I was strong enough, before they would give consent. The doctor was in favour of my marrying. My health was no better after I married I then went through the hands of several doctors with- out success, and at last became an out-patient at the hospital—all to no purpose. I continued a sufferer from the painful ailments, with the irritating addition of neuralgia tormenting me, nntil a year ago, when through reading about Bile Beans I was induced to try them. The effect was marvellous ! The doctor, too, was astonished at the change that gradually came over me. My illness seemed to gradually and surely die as I continued taking the Beans. Then my strength increased and everything went for the better. Bile Beans eventu- ally drove the ailments completely away, and set me up in the healthy, hearty condition I am now enjoying. "My mother and husband have both taken Bile Beans frequently since, and derived great benefit from them." Bile Beans are a certain cure for constipation, piles, female ailments and irregularities, indigestion, dizziness, wind, heartburn, headache, anemia' liver complaint, biliousness, sickness, and feelings of fulness after food, sleeplessness, influenza, eruptions, and all blood impuri- ties. Of all chemists, at is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box (2s. 9d. box contains 3 times is. *I. size.) Avoid all substitutes. Bile Beans are not sold loose--only in sealed boxes.</p>
        <p>Newsagents " The Hardest Worked People in the Universe." "The hardest worked and the least paid people in the universe " was the description applied to news- agents at a meeting of the Warrington and district newsagents' association which was held at nrmber 3 Scotland road, on Monday evening. There was a large attendance induding Mr. B. Burrows, hon. secretary of tne National Newsagents' Association, and of Mr. D. H. Astley, hon. treasurer. Mr. Burrows pointed out that the ,National Association had not been founded with any intention of being arbitrary, its sole aim being to better the position of newsagents, and to prove to tho public that the business of news- agents was a trade, in every respect equal to any other trade, and not, as a great many people seemed to think, one of those businesses that every " Jack, Tom or Harry "could earn an easy living at. News- agents, as a body, were the hardest worked people and the least paid in the universe, but he hoped as time went on the whole trade would be in a far better position, by reason of the founding of branch associa- tions and ultimately these branches coming under one banner, viz. the National Association. (Applause). —Mr. Astley said he was proud the newsagents had at last decided to make a stand to better their position in Warrington, by forming an association. He hoped he would have the pleasure of seeing them thrive. Why should the newsagent, who paid rates and taxes (heavy at that), be hampered and deprived of a fair living by urchins, who in a great many cases were only sent out to earn money for their parents to indulge in drink? Some people said that these boys were a public necessity ; others that they were a public nuisance. The latter was the correct term to be applied to them ; and the sooner that portion of the British public who declared them to be a want, looked at the other side of the question the better. not only for the newsagent, but also for themselves.— Mr. Dickinson, secretary cf the Warrington and Dis- trict Newsagents' Association, proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. R. Burrows and Mr. D. H. Astlev, which was seconded by Mr. J. Kay and carried.</p>
        <p>As a memorial of the late Miss Frances Power Cobbe it is proposed to establish an annual prize for students in women's colleges connected with the Universities of Oxford, Cambridge, and Dublin. • The prize is to be awarded for en essay on some subject bearing upon natural religion. Lady Batteries is receiving donations for the fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 11, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 11, 1905.</p>
        <p>are too far away—much too far away—like the stars." But shc took his }mud.</p>
        <p>" Yes, it is I. Will yen not believn " No," he said, " I can't. I can't. It is t. picture—a picture," uid his eyes turned to picture on the wall— a picture covered by a light veil.</p>
        <p>She crossed the apartment, and drew the ing, and then started back. It was her portrair. There could be no mistake. • Yet how did it come there ?</p>
        <p>" Yes," he sithl, as though answering her mute interrogation. " I had it painted froin graipbs. It is very like—very like. You know, yon are the picture come to life." Per answer slic lii r 1 into tears.</p>
        <p>Towards i•ight the Captain WaA less well. The doctor feared brain fever. But on the following morning there was more coherency, yet there was lesspning strength. . . . .</p>
        <p>For a 0111" en t the girl had picked up a book, when p•lie heard her name math " Stern.. r'</p>
        <p>She turned " Yes, Reg," she said. He seemed to find speaking more and more difficult, Lilt at length he said, " I am dying." " Oh, new: ! No, no, no! You are better— You Will soon be well." He shook his head. " No," hc said mournfully , "it is no use. I sliould like to deceive myself, but I can't. I have been given a clear sight of the truth. God al- lows that before one goes. Stella, I want you to marry me before I go. The doctor will help. Will you—will you ?" " Yes, she said, ''if you wish."</p>
        <p>, "It will make me happy," be said. "It will only be for a few days—a few boars even, and then I shall be gone."</p>
        <p>She turned ortickly away. Au old college friend was called in, and in the presence of the doctor and the man-servant the simple rites were performed. The doctor turned to Stella at the olose, and took her band. . .</p>
        <p>" My poor, brave girl 1" he said, and then he broke off, for even he seemed to be struggling for once with an emotion which it wan difficult tt, control.</p>
        <p>"Doctor,'' she said piteously, " there is shil hope ?" " There is always that," he said gravely, " while there is life."</p>
        <p>" But you think that there is none ?" The physician did not reply, but turned to leave, and, the clergyman who had performed the brief service came up to Stella. "rrs. Vartrey," lie said, " you ought to have help here ; you are worn out. Don't deny it. I can see it. May I send on a nurse ?" " No, please not," she said. "I am equal to anything—anything." The boyish-looking clergyman looked at her sadly. Then be shook his bead and said goodbye. The Captain's term of lucidity had vanished, and the brain fever which the doctor had feared supervened ; and all through that night he talked incoherently of what had been, of things he had seen during his campaigns ; he was fighting again down in the South ; he was thinking of his hopeless love; and +'en once he looked hard at her. " Stella !" • She bent over him.</p>
        <p>" Goodbye," he said ; "goodbye !" " Pearest," she said, clasping him in ner arms, "it ii't be goodbye, for I love you—l love you so ! You will stop with me—" and then as her tears came thick and fast she continued, with that wonderful tenderness of womanhood which atones for all the world, " You who are noble' and good and great, you must not go—you shall not ! There are spring; and summers which we will see. We have the world, and God is good." But he did not understand.</p>
        <p>Then towards morning he fell back, motion. less, and she knew that that was the end. The sunshine of the very early morning was being winnowed through the blinds, and there came up to her the sounds of the morning—the talk, the cries, the rolling of traffic; the watered streets glistctxd ; there was the scent of flowers—all that brilliant life after the tourbillon of the mys- terious night which had wrapped up the cities, the countrysides, and the seas. The after-effect of grief was too much for her, and she seemed to lose knowledge of what was passing, and not to be aware when the doctor ap reared. " Mrs. Vartrey," be said, putting his band gently on her arm, " how is our patient this fine morning P Better, I hope." " Doctor," she murmured, " he is dead." She was past feeling beyond the terror of grief. The doctor went to the bedside and looked, and then turned to Stella.</p>
        <p>"The bad l:nie i passed," be said; " you were mistaken : your husband is not dead." " And Ile will live ?" she cried excitedly. "Yes, he will liv3 ; your prayers have been heard," and the old man touched her fair hair.</p>
        <p>" hoetar," she cried,- seizing his hand una breaking down with the tears of happiness, " what have I done that Heaven should give him book to me? Ah, lam happy—happy." "Yon have been good,' he said " you have been good and kind, and with those two things our hearts can always pass into Paradise."</p>
