        <p>From Mr. BALFourt THE LIBERAL downwards it has become PROGRAMME. an accepted fact that a change in the Govern- ment of the country is inevitable, and cannot be long delayed. Parliament opens on Tuesday, and it is quite possible that the policy of the Govern- ment will be challenged at the outset. In that event an early appeal to the country may be forced from the present incompetent and widely-dis- trusted holders of office. Naturally under the cir- cumstances, there have been many queries within and without the camp as to the lines of the Liberal policy. A definite programme is hardly yet called for, but the nation will be grateful to Earl SPENCER for his important and illuminating manifesto issued yesterday in a letter to Mr. CORRIE GRANT. The noble Earl,—than whom there is no more staunch nor trusted upholder of Liberal principles—has sketched the course of the prospec- tive Government with a firm hand. The question of fiscal reform, he says, "dominates the situation," and his lordship then goes on to devote the larger part of his letter to a searching analysis of what Mr. CHAMBERLAIN and Mr. BALFOTJR would bring us to were they accepted, jointly or separ- ately, as the nation's "guide, philosopher, and friend." He asks pertinently, " Will this country agree to protective duties for the benefit of home producers and manufacturers? Protection may increase the wealth of individuals among the classes and employers in the trades pro- tected, but only by the impoverishment and loss of all the rest of the community. . . . I feel that it is the duty of everyone who wishes for the prosperity, comfort, and happiness of the nation strenuously to resist such a change of fiscal policy, as fraught with certain disaster." There could, of course, be no doubt as to the EARL'S views:on the dominating political question, but his serious affirmation of personal duty will be wel- comed by all sections of the Progressive party. The EARL is equally clear,precise,and intelligible in his forecast of prospective Liberal legislation and re- form. The land question has,of late,occupied a fore. most position in the minds of all Progressives. It cannot be shelvedffiuch longer, and we are glad to see that EARL SPENCER fully apprccrates the grow- ng public desire to make a breach in the monopoly of large land-holding in the country and to redres the unequal incidence of land taxation in the towns. He boldly forecasts the introduction of a broad and comprehensive measure "tc deal with the whole basis and inci- dence of taxation and rating which, both in town rid country, now are antiquated and need drast reform." Anything in the nature of a pledge b</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 11, 1905.</p>
        <p>a Liberal administration to legislate on these lines will cement a large section of their own followers with the bulk of the knights of Labour. The democracy demands a re-adjustment of the rating of land values and it will look to a Liberal administration to supply the demand. With regard to the precedence of measures of reform, Earl SPENCER is naturally silent, but he gives ample ground for believing that the repairing of some of the errors of its predecessor will early occupy the attention of the new Government. Full public control of schools paid for by public money and the removal of all sectarian tests for teachers, will be a welcome forecast for educationists, while with regard to licensing matters we are pro- mised the limitation of vested interests, which Mr. MonLEv voiced as one resolve of the Opposition. In South Africa the fullest measure of representative and responsible government will be considered, while Chinese labour may have reason to tremble for its existence once the obliga- tions of existing contracts are at an end. Trade Unionists will be gratified by the EARL'S allusion to the "stability of social and trade conditions " as affected by the Taff Vale and other decisions in the courts of law. There is a promise of protection and the restoration of just rights and privileges in his words, which accords well with the prospective return to a method of government which looks to the well-being of the nation rather than to the endowing of favoured classes and vested interests.</p>
        <p>THE tactical PROTECTIONIST dilemma in which TACTICS. Mr. BALFoua has</p>
        <p>placed his supporters is well illustrated by the Ministerial comments on Mr. MORLEY'S speech at Newbury. Quite naturally, Mr. Moranv pointed out that Mr BALFOUR'S half-sheet of notepaper had not clearea up anything. For instance, a statesman who pro- ' poses to levy retaliatory duties without raising home prices must expect to be asked how this is to be done. This question goes to the very root of the principle involved, and cannot be put off as a mere detail which can wait until Mr. Balfour introduces a Bill. But what says the" Times ? " " Means cannot be dismssed to any advantage until the country has said what it wants to find means of attaining." This luminous attitude is made absurd by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S campaign. Mr. Morley pointed very forcibly the contrast we have repeatedly made between the absolute lucidity of Mr. CHAM- BERLAIN'S proposals and the calculated obscurity of Mr. BALFOUR'S. It is ludicrous to say, as the " Times " says, that " means cannot be discussed to any advantage," when Mr. CHAMBERLAIN has been discussing them for eighteen montlis. It is ludicrous to pretead that the Fiscal question is not yet before the country, when Mr. CHAMBERLAIN has made it clear that nothing else is before the country. It was the " Times " that likened Mr. BALFOUR and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN to partners at whist, emphasizing by this significant image the identity of their aims. What is the good of suggesting now that their aims are quite separate and distinct, and that while one of them in.folds his plan in every speech the other is entitled to hide behind the plea that the question is not ripe for settlement? To raise a problem which has divided the Unionist party, and driven some of the most prominent Unionists into the ranks of the Opposition, and then to maintain that the problem cannot be discussed to any advantage until the country had said what it wants, is probably the most singular exhibition of trimming in our political history. However, the "Times" finds Mr. BALFOUR "per- fectly clear and explicit," and adds, with fine complacency: "When as much can be said for Mr. MORLEY and his:friends, it will be time enough to listen to their random and speculative objec- tions to what Mr. BALFOUR has not yet pro- posed." Well, Mr. BALFOUR has certainly proposed to levy retaliatory duties, and the random and speculative objection is that you can- not impose such duties without raising home prices. The " Times "tries to evade this point by accusing Mr. MORLEY of shirking important issues. "What this stickler for lucidity on the part of other people owes to the country is a clear state-' rent of his agreement or disagreement with the object of lessening the obstacles to British trade. What Mr. MORLEY and the other Liberal leaders have said over and over again with the utmost lucidity is that British trade, on the whole, is doing very well and that no departure from the principle of our fiscal system can be of any avail in dealing with the obstacles set up by foreign competition. As for the Colonies, a Colonial Conference is due next year; and if the Colonial delegates should formu- late proposals for a closer commercial union they will certainly be discussed. But the delegates will I not be left in any doubt as to the rejection by the British electorate of the entire policy submitted by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN and Mr. BALFOUR. The advantages of preferential trade to the Colonies are obvious ; but public opinion here can see no advantages to this country ; and there is no reason to suppose for one moment that Colonial opinion will quarrel with this conclusion.</p>
        <p>The Progressive Labour League is only in its infancy, bLit it is already giving evidence that the title chosen by the promoters is no misnomer. " Progress " has been the main characteristic of its short spell of work, and it is only necessary to mention that this week's meeting was the most largely attended of the series to show that the organization is in a healthy state and promises to become a power to be reckoned with in the politi- cal affairs of Warrington. We are pleased to announce that the league has arranged for the visit to Warrington of a well known representative of Labour' in Parliament in the person of Mr. Fenwick, M.P. for the Wansbeck Division of Northumberland. This energetic politician who achieved the distinction of going straight "from the pit to the House of Commons" will address a meeting in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on February 21st. Trio chair will bo taken by Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield, and the other speakers will include Mr. Joseph Howes, president of the League, and Mr. H. Samuelson, of Liverpool. There will doubtless be a tare muster of War • rington workingman on this interesting occasion,</p>
        <p>The sitting of the Town Council on Tuesday was protracted in length and stormy at intervals. Physical and mental waariness were everywhere apparent long before the end of the proceedings and some of the later items on the agenda hardly received the attention which, perhaps,they merited. The annual increases of salaries caused the greatest amount of argument, and not one was granted without considerable opposition. Those which came up on the business of the earlier Committees were fully debated, but later on there was scant ceremony shown the applicants for "a rise." A determination to be very economical seemed to come over the Council, and the modest £lO or £l3 per year extra was in many cases refused. The only large increases—£2s to Mr. Woodcock, and</p>
      </div>
