      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>50 to Mr. Mathias—were granted and probably deserved, but to exercise strict economy at the expense of the smaller men seemed a little bit farcical. What the Council might have more reasonably done would have been to decide at the outset not to grant any increases this year owing to the outcry against high rates. We hope the spirit of economy shown on Tuesday evening will be made manifest in other directions.</p>
        <p>Some amusement wes caused by the attitude of Councillor Wright. He supported every amend- ment against the increases on the ground that until the" poor workingman " was better paid he would not sanction an advance for the higher officials. Apparently, from his point of view it was a fair comparison to point out that Mr. So-and-So wa§ receiving £6 14s. per week while the Corpora- tion dustmen and labourers were only getting 20s. or 255. Presumably Councillor Wright would have them paid equally, the nature of their respec- tive service being unworthy of consideration. It was a peculiar attitude to take and emphasises the danger of having too many men in the Council who look at things from a narrow and unenlightened standpoint. Councillor Wright ought to know that there is a premium on brains nowadays, and that a man's worth is not decided by the sweat on his brow or the hardness of his palm.</p>
        <p>The lack of candour shown by the Corporation in regard to the George Hotel licence is both unjustifiable and unwise. At the Council meet- ing the phrase " municipal doging" was applied to the matter, while at the Licensing Sessions,yester- day, the words "Corporation jobbory " were used in a similar sense. All this evidence of personal feeling and public dissatisfaction is a testimony to the folly and the lack of straightforwardness on the part of the Corporation. Information with regard to the position of affairs concerning the disposal of the licence is positively refused. It ought not to be. We do not suggest that the Corporation should make public the negotiations which may be going on, but everybody is entitled to know exactly how matters stand. Last week there was a heated debate in committee on the George licence, but all reference to it was excluded from the minutes of the committee in question—the General Purposes Committee. This gave rise to an unpleasant scene in the Council on Tuesday, when Councillors Maginn and Pemberton defied the Mayor and insisted on their right to discuss the matter. There was obviously a considerable amount of sympathy with them in their protest, and we are convinced that an entirely wrong course is being tazen in the cloaking of all information concerning a licence which is unquestionably a public asset. The Mayor, as one of the parties interested in the sale of the licence, ought to make it a personal responsibility to see that nothing is kept back from the public which it is entitled to know. We appeal to the Mayor and Corporation, for the sake of dignity and fair-dealing, for more candour !</p>
        <p>Free breakfasts for poor children were the subject of a motion by Councillor Skelland, that read as follows :—" That a committee be appointed to consider what steps can be taken for the feeding of poor children during the depression of trade." The resolution, which is on reasonable lines, was carried unanimously. We hear a great deal nowadays about the question of feeding the poor— and it is not one to be lightly considered. It is every parent's duty to provide for his own offspring, and not only municipalities but the State must be careful not to relieve him of the duty, or lighten it unnecessarily. Children cannot be allowed to starve, of course, and in periods of great depression relief measures would certainly be imperative. But it is questionable whether there is much need for "free breakfasts "in War- rington to-day, and the committee to be appointed will doubtless satisfy themselves on that point. We know of a large elementary school in the centre of a poor district of Warrington where a careful inquiry has been made into the breakfast question. Out of 800 scholars— typical children of the poor—only one had come to school without breakfast. This is a remarkable fact, and we see no reason to suppose that a similarly satisfactory ',condition of things does not obtaii, generally. Some rather interest- ing particulars were obtained by the headmaster of the school in the course of his inquiries. For instance porridge,and bread and milk,the best form of child's breakfast, were rarities, whereas bacon and even meat were common articles of early morning diet. We should suggest that the special committee take steps to have a " census" of the breakfastless children attending the elementary schools before devising means to relieve distress which may,possibly, be more imaginary than real.</p>
        <p>As the result of a motion by Councillor Stott,the responsible committees ,of the Corporation are to put in hand as much cleaning,paving,and repairing work as possible in order to find work for the un- employed. Much may be done in this direction,or to enable the willing workers to earn an honest wage, but we fear that many of the men walking about the streets are not of the type who will take work wherever offered. Councillor Stott also sug- gested the opening of a bureau which would obtain definite information as to the number of unem- ployed, and the suggestion was approved. An ounce or two of fact and figures will be worth pounds of theory and imagination. It is time we knew, by some means or other, what the state of employment in the town really is!</p>
        <p>Contract questions have cropped up with such unpleasant persistence in Warrington during the past few years, that the desire to leave "no reasonable grounds for suspicion " has led the Board of Guardians to take the important step of revising its regulations affecting the method of dealing with tenders. There appears to be a con- sensus of opinion that the existing arrangements are unsatisfactory and have a tendency to restrict competition. It is of the first importance that persons tendering for public work shall have every confidence that their claims will be considered quite irrespective of "personal influence." To that end a committee of the Board has made inquiries as to the systems obtaining in other unions and the result is a scheme which it is hoped will be a decided improvement. The chief object—and a very desirable one— is that the name of the tenderer shall not be known to the committee until the tender has been approved for acceptance. In regard to coal contracts there certainly is a diffi- culty, but it should not be insurmountable and we hope a method satisfactory to all concerned will be devised.</p>
        <p>' The Court of Appeal will have the support of most sensible people in the action they have taken in reversing the judgment in which a child was awarded damages against his schoolmaster for informing his schoolfellows that he was "a bad boy," and warning them against holding com- munication with him. Times have changed since Scrooge and Dotheboys Hall; and had this decision stood the N.U.T. might well have called for a Dickens to rouse the public to the grievances of teachers. The Court of Appeal held that the words used by the schoolmaster in this case were not actionable ; and the decision will have the effect of checking the growing tendency to invoke the law in trivial cases of this character. The award of damages in such a case was a direct encourage- ment to elevate the schoolboy to a position of dictatorship over the unfortunate master, whose control under such circumstances would speedily disappear. Certain limitations which have been placed in recent years on the power of schoolmasters over the children under their charge have been dicated ny public opinion, and have undoubtedly done away with the many abuses of authority which occurred formerly; but public opinion re3ognises that a line must be drawn, lest the authority of those responsible for education be destroyed in the anxiety to protect the child. Were the decision still unreversed, the Board of Education would have been under the necessity of issuing a special instruction to teachers on the terms in which without fear of the law of libel, theylmight address the young ladies and gentlemen to whose intel- lectual development • a merciful Providence had permitted them the privilege of ministering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
