      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <p>It is an item of news generally overlooked by the English Press that mornings are now beginning so much earlier than formerly that there is an element of sensation in the circumstance for the alert observer. For him the winter is done. It may still be true that" If Candlernas Day be dry and fair, the half of winter's to come and mair' ' but the man who has a nodding acquaintance with the sunrise is already emancipated from the year's prison. There is a new and ideal life in the hours before breakfast. Candlemas Day this year was both fair and dry; but ic is gloom that makes winter. Toe thrushes in the last few days have tuned a fuller song, and the young trees are all breaking into bud. For the man who possesses a spade, and does not regard it as a piece of orna- mental furniture, there is the regular turn in the garden, and the invitation again to outdoor activities; for the student, "by eastern windows," there are the generous and luminous hours before the world and his wif 3 answer the fragrant call of eggs and bacon. But the light, music land ideality of early morning are only for the few. Nature turns her sibylline leaves when the activi- ties of the world are stilled. The poet in his glade, as the year progresses, the sentinel at his post, and the sufferer on his pallet have respite then from the perplexities, the clamours and the ' pains of night and noonday. The lot of the gardener is best of all ! He alone looks upon orderly processes; to him the spring has already come frankly with her promises, and he hears again the music of asolian harps in the tree tops, which is played etarnally to the spirit of labour, hope, content, and patience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>2. Women's Chat and Fashions. Runcorn Rural Council. Science Notes. Local Churches. 3. " After the Dance," by Clive R. Fenn. Billiards. 6. Town Council: Increase of Salaries. "The Romance of Land Movements," by Dr. Andrew Wilson. Police News. 7. Football. Licensing Point at Warrington. Runcorn Board of Guardians. Coming Events. In connection with the Wesley Guild the Rev. George Harbottle will preach to-morrow,morning and evening, at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, and the subject for the evening's discourse will be "The Welsh Revival." He has recently paid a visit to South Wales to see the revival for himself, and will thus be able to say something of the wonderful move- ment which is at work throughout the .greater part of Wales. On Monday the Rev. George Harbottle will deliver a lecture in the church on " Peter Mackenzie." The Silver-street Juvenile Prize Choir (conductor, Mr. Wadsworth) will give three selections including the test piece at the recent Eisteddfod - " Don't forget the Old Folks." Chair to be taken at 7.30 by Mr. John Taylor, L.D.S. ' This evening a concert in connection with the Warrington Male Voice Choir will be held in the Parr Hall. An exceptionally attractive programme has been arranged and the Manchester Orpheus Choir and well known artistes will take part. A special people's service will be held at Wycliffe Church to-morrow evening, when the Rev. John Yonge will deliver an address. At an open meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. Society to-morrow afternoon the speaker will be the ' Rev. J. F. Matthews. Miss Gosney will speak at the Spiritualist Society's meeting in the Druids' Hall to-morrow. On Monday evening Mrs. Hazlehurst will give clairvoyance. " The Welsh and other revivals" will be the Rev. H. Dunnico's subject at the Parr Hall to-morrow evening. Another of the series of University extension lectures will be delivered in the Co-operative Hall on Friday evening by Prof. S. J. Chapman, M.A. "Methods of settling trade disputes " will be the subject. The third subscription concert of the season in con- nection with the Musical Society will be held in the Parr Hall on Tuesday, February 28th. Social Service League. At the weekly meeting of this league held last evening an interesting °debate on the Education question was opened by the Rev. H. Dunnico. Ellesmere Street Mission. A successful concert in aid of the new building fund was held in Ellesmere-street Wesleym School, on Thursday evening. An exceptionally interesting programme included a pianoforte duet by the Misses Carnson,songs by Miss Emily Taylor, Miss Mabel Hen- , shall, Mr. Poole and Mr. Griffiths, violin solos by Miss Bouchier, and humorous readings by Mr. H. M. Ashton. All the items were splendidly received, and encores were frequent. Miss Carnson discharged the duties of accompanist. Workingmen's Mission. In connection with the above the second Band of Hope entertainment of the season was given in the schoolroom on Tuesday evening by the members of the Ellesmere-street Band of Hope, when there was a large audience, over which Mr. Barlow presided. The programme consisted of songs, recitations, piano- forte and mandoline solos, and dialogues, and an enjoyable evening was spent. UNDER. THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. Mr. William Croskill presided at a well attended' meeting of this society on Sunday, and referred to the labours of the late John Ashworth, author of" Strange Tales." Sergeant-Major Fogg, of Manchester, spoke of the disciples'iwords to the Master, "We have toiled all night, and caught nothing." He said be once had a trying time on the sea. He went with the high sea fishermen. While he lay sick all night,the fishermen toiled hard, but caught nothing. Time was lost and energy wasted. Men, tired oil/ after their labours, often felt they had toiled to no purpose. Why? Fishermen were experienced. They were triers, but still had not succeeded. Had they done their best ? Had they put themselves to any inconvenience or sell-sacrifice? Often they had neglected duty and yet they wondered why they had failed. It was because they had not been triers. Mr. C. Cotton read the lesson, and Mr. A. Cotterill sang effectively " There is a green hill far away." Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, Limited.</p>
        <p>The general office staff of Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, Limited, held their annual dinner at the Lion Hotel on Wednesday evening of last week. An excellent repast was served by Mr. John Lee, an assembly of over 100 sitting down. Mr. Wardle pre- sided over the after-proceedings and an interesting musical programme was submitted. The accompanists were Messrs. IL Clayton, W. Brown and G. Hall. An excellent rendering was given of the quartet, "Allan water," by Messrs. Kinsey, Grooms, Gillin and Swift, and the Male Voice Choir (office members) was heard to advantage in the glee, "Men of Harlech." Songs were given by the Misses M. Dixon and A. L. Brundrett, and Messrs. W. Dennett, H. Griffiths. Cunningham, Wardle, Lomas, Morris, Swift, and J. P. Hignett. Various toasts were honoured, Mr, Lomas in proposi g that of "The firm" giving interesting reminiscenes of his earlier connection with the concern. It was a curious coincidence that at the present time they had three brothers in the firm, Mr. Arthur Crosfield, Mr. J. J. Crosfield and Mr. George Crosfield, and when he came there first there were also three brothers, Mr. George Crosfield, the late Mr. John Crosfield, and Mr. Morland Crosfield. In some firms, after a lapse of years, the original founder was forgotten, and very frequently his name was not even retained. He was glad to say that that was not the case with their firm, the link not yet having been broken--(applause)--and he trusted that aichange of that character mitt not occur for many, many years. (Hear, hear an applause.) H. M. ASHTON'S LITE TONIC is the Rum or TONICS. I 63, SANRICY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>L.</p>
      </div>
