      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>_Peculiar Accident to a Boy. A peculiar accident occurred at Howley on Mon- day afternoon, Herbert Hughes, three years of age,son of Mr. Herbert Hughes, engine driver, Holmesfielcl house, Howley, was playing near the back gate of Howley Tannery, when the gate, which weighs seven or eight cwt., fell on the boy's head inflicting a severe scalp wound several inches long. The police ambu- lance was at once telephoned for, and the child was removed to the Infirmary. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF. TONICS.-63, SANKEY ST., WARRINGTON.—Advt. Fire Brigade Again at Work. The members of the Borough Fire Brigade had another call to duty on Saturday evening. At half- past ten information was received of an outbreak in a corrugated iron shed on the premises of the Longford Wire Co., Ltd. The bell was rung, causing about 3,000 people to assemble, and the brigade promptly turned out under the direction of the Superintendent and Deputy-Superintendent (Ur. Frank Turner, senr., and Mr. Frank Turner, jun..) with the steam engine " Major," reels and ladders. At the works it was found that the shed (in which were stored waste of all kinds, valuable machinery and straw used for packing purposes) was well ablaze, but as a good supply of water was obtained from the works reservoir the members of the fire brigade with other assistance succeeded in about two hours in mastering the flames and preventing them spreading to the adjoining premises. The shed was, however, gutted, and damage done amounting to about £3OO, which is fully covered by insurance. The origin of the fire, which was discovered by a night workman named Joseph Fellows, is unknown. _ .</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Wakefield. . . Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Wakefield. wife of Mr. Samuel Wakefield, tenant of the Midland Hotel, Wintvick-street, who passed away on Saturday afternoon. The deceased lady, who was 56 years of age, had been in a delicate state of health for some months. She, took an active part in the arranging of the treat given by herself and her husband to the newsboys of Warrington at the latter end of December, and since that time she had been confined to her room. She was at first attended by Dr. Stewart, of Liverpool; and latterly by Drs. Joseph and Bowden, of Warrington. The cause of death was dropsy and heart trouble. Mrs. Wakefield was held in respect by many townspeople and much - sympathy is expressed for her husband in his bereave- ment. The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Tuesday afternoon, a large assembly witnessing the last rites. The following were the chief mourners :—Mr. S Wakefield (husband), Messrs. John and George Horn (brothers), Alfred Wakefield (brother-in-law), Samuel and George Horn arid George Smith (nephews). There were also present :—.1.1r. Charles Bunch and Mr. MacMillan, Messrs. Gough, Allen, and Johnson (West Bromwich), Messrs. Thomas Poffinore (Manchester), George Sturgess (Ashton-in- . Makerfield), Harry Hibbert and Charles Owen (Earies- town), J. Rose (Wigan). J. Sampson, J. Potter, James Payton, Joseph Allison, Thomas Mather, Boothroyd, Joseph, Roberts, John Horton, John Wareing, J. Griffiths, George Beard, W. J. Shaw, B. Young, Cherry, Bailey, Ashton, Rigby, Day Sergeant- Major Dalmage, and Dr. Bowen. Floral tributes were sent by Mr. S. Wakefield, Mr. and Mrs. G. Horn, Mr. A. Wakefield Mr. and Mrs H. Brooks (Blackpool), Dr. Stewart, Mr- and Mrs. Waters, and Mr. and Mrs. Stevenson (Liverpool), Mr. and Mrs. Owen and Mr. and Mrs. Thorpe (Earlestown), Mr. and Mrs. J. Rose (Wigan), Mr. J. Gough, Mr. and Mrs. Allen, Mr. and Mrs. Hunt, and Mr. William Johnson (West Bromwich), Warrington newsboys, newsagents, Central Station staff, Mr. and Mrs. Archer, Mr. C. E. Bunch, Mr. and Mrs. John Clarke, Mr. W. J. Shaw, Miss Edith Jones, Messrs. Eddowes and Co., Mr. Wakefield's workmen Sergeant-Major Dalmage, W.O. staff, Sergeant H. L. It., Colour-Sergeant Sinclair, Sergeants D Company and others. The bearers were SIX non-commissioned officers, Colour-Sergeant Hampton (V.C.), Wilson, Clarke, Mahoney, Hardie and Wainwright. The Rev. C. L. Stowe, of Legh-street Primitive Methodist Chapel officiated. ..,.,_' PURLICjBENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST LOOTS ra LascAsHrite.—Advt. I P.1101.0S 12 for 6d.—MAIIDEEN, 37, Sankey-st.—Adyt Independent Order of Rechabltes. The annual meeting of the Warrington District No 4 was held in the Congregational schoolroom, Ashton, near Newton-le-Willows, on Saturday. The Council' was opened about 3 p.m., by the D.C.R. Bro. B. Walker, supported by the whole of therlistrict officers and also the representatives from the 18 Tents forming the District.—The report of Bro, T. Winstanley, D.S., Which contained a most interesting summary of the work done during the year, both of a numerical and financial character, and especially his remarks on the excessive sick pay experienced by several of the mining tents for the last lea- years, was adopted. The balance sheet was presented, which showed that through the excessive sick pay during the past Year only £lO had been placed to the credit of the sick fund. In the funeral fund £75 had been paid in funeral claims on the death of members and Members' wives, there being a net gain on the funeral ; 'tad of £224. The, District funds are as follows : Telt fund, £1,972 3s. 5W.; financial fund, £1,681 ,a• gd. : special fund, £Sg 12s. 2d.; management fund, '195 19s. sd. ; total fund, £3,933 4s. 9d. The rnirnerical statement showed that the District com- rsed 18 Tents with a membership of 1237, this being an decrease of 11 members during the year. Tt_ ne auditors' report of the books and investments of _h,e District presented by Brethren B. Warburton and B i 'lark° were considered very satisfactory and adopted. The recommendation to alter Rule 19 Clause No. 6 to i read from one to two years was lost. The recom- mendation from Purity tent to amend Rule No. 15 to reduce the amount of extra fees was lost. . The report of Bro. Wright D.S.J.T. was given, ; a and was of a most satisfactory character, 20 r, ?nembers having been transferred from the a Juvenile tents to the adult branches, and leaving 00 ..., - a net gain of 95 juvenile members during the year. 'she , total membership was 875. The funds of the ~ ~,- ' Julrenile district continued to increase there , being a gain of £7O 19s. 11d. during the C Lear the total funds amounting to £316 2s. 9d. i, The Hope of Sankey juvenile tent were competing for the Gavel and Block presented by the order, being able to show an increase of 150 per cent. during the year. The election of officers took place and resulted as follows: Bro. W. Antrobus, D.C.R., Bro. It Hamson, D.D.K.; Bro. J. Hill Levite, Bro. S. Long- slimy, guardians, Bro. B. Walker P.D.C.R., Bro. T. Massey, treasurer, T. Winstaniey, DG., Bro. H. Blacker D.S.J.T.,.trustees Brethern T. Hall, J. Shaw f, P and E. Walkden, auditors, Bro. B. Warburton and 13'. Clarke. Refiresett tatives to the High Movable Con- ference to be held at Aberdeen in August next, Brethern J. Horne, and T. Massey. The meeting Was closed at 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>Cartons New and Improved Breeds of Farm Seeds. The large illustrated and descriptive catalogue just issued bY Messrs. Gartons Liinited,of Warrington and Newton:le-Willows, reveals many interesting facts re- garding the valuable new breeds of seeds which they have been so instrumental in placing before agricul- turists during recent years. In this catalogue many evidences are given of the superior properties of these new breeds both by prizes that have been awarded to them in open competition, as well as by striking testimonials from experienced growers in all parts of the country. Beautiful reproductions of many of the firm's specialities such as" Model" Swedes," Improved Large Yellow Globe " Mengel, &amp;c., &amp;c., give an artistic appearance to the book which is well worthy of perusal by all who are interested in agriculture. ,BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Street Fatality. Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at the Police Station on Wednesday evening touching the death of Arthur Lawrinson, aged one year and seven months, son of Thomas Lawrinson, forge labourer, 40, Haydock- street.—The evidence showed that on Monday morning deceased was run over by a lorry driven by Robert Seed, in the employ of Messrs. W. and J. Richard- son. He was severely injured internally and died a few minutes after admission to the Infirms. 7— Seed stated that he was sitting on the front a the lurry when he felt one of the back wheels jolt over something. On pulling up he found the deceased had been run over. The street was very narrow and the child had run into the roadway without witness seeing him—Mr. Burdett, manager for Messrs. Richardson, said Seed was a very steady driver —The jury re. FIRE GRATES Of every description.—J. S. GAR turned a verdict of "Accidental death," exonerating LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, &amp; 55, Bridge- the driver from all blame. street, Warrington.—Advt. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE 1.0.G.T. South West Lancashire Juvenile CLOCK, rt —RIDGE-STREET.—Advt. District Council. Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club. The annual session as held on Saturday, in the On Tuesday evening the distribution of prizes to Mansfield Mission-room, Liverpool, Mr. E. Newman, the winners of the billiard handicap promoted by this D.5.1.T., of Earlestown, presiding over a good attend- club took place at the Cemetery Hotel. There was a since of representatives from all parts ei the district, large attendance, and in the absence of the president Mr. R. D. Petterson, D.C., Mr. M. Jones D.Secretary, (gr. A. H. Crosfield) the chair was occupied by Mr. G. B. Walker,D.Treasurer, were also present as Captain G. R. Crosfield. Mr. Samuel Taylor was in distinguished visitors. The annual report of the the vice-chair. Captain Crosfield distributed the D.S.I.T. was very encouraging and especially the prizes as follows :-1, Mr. A. J. Jones: 2, reference to those who taken part in the National Mr. Joseph Forster ; 3, Mr. A. E. 'Boardman ; examination. Quite a number had been successful 4, Mr. R. Young; 5, Mr. T. Bolton ; 6, Mr. and one, J.W. Woods of the Pride of Earlestown had J. Wood ; 7, Mr. Win. Povey ; 8, Mr. S. gained honours. An interesting discussion took place Davies ; 0, Mr S. Taylor ; 10, Mr. H. Mather' in reference to the report and it was decided that all 11, Mr. E. Taylor; 12, Mr. T. Reddish; 13, Mr. J. the certificates be framed and presented at some Berry; 14, Mr. T. Humphreys ; 15, Mr. F. Miller ; public meetings arranged. A telegram of sympathy 16, Mr. J. Bramhall. During the evening songs were was ordered to be sent to Mr. David Reid,D.Treasurer, given by Messrs. J. Willett, jun., T. Humphreys, J. who was very ill ; also one received from Newton and Princep. The health of " the President" Ur. Hopkins, Ci-.5.1.T. Gloucester, wishing success, was honoured on the proposition of Mr. Sansuel and also stating that the Juvenile Council was to be Taylor,ancl a similar compliment was paid to the vice- held in Liverpool in the autumn. The election of president on the proposition of Mr. J. Hollins. officers then took place. Bro. E. Newman was Asx for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL recommended the D.S,I.T. for the coming year; Mrs WATERS.—Advt. Woods (Earlestown), D.C. Councillor; Mr. George Girls' Club Silver-street. Clarks (Earlestown) D.C. Secretary, the rest of the The above club was visited on Monday evening by officers being in Liverpool. Various matters were veveral members and friends of the British Women's then discussed for the interest of the Juvenile Order. Temperance Association. Short addresses were given A new branch is to be opened at Wigan in a week's by Mrs. Simpson, Mrs. Milne, and Mrs. Norman. Miss time. After tea, the installation of the officers took Cox in a very pleasing manner Sang "The Happy place, Mr. G. B. Walker, D. Treasurer, presiding. Nigger" and later she gave "The Flight of Ages," He also gave a report of what had been done in the she was accompanied each time by Miss Bertha forthcoming arrangements for the National Juvenile Norman. Mrs. Whitmore and Miss Wilson blended Conference. A vote of sympathy was tendered to the their voices very effectively in "Linger with me Rev. J. A. Lee, D.C.T., on his serious illness. Thanks Precious Saviour 1" Miss Wilson also sang" Dad dy." tendered to the Wesley Hall friends for the arrange- They were accompanied by Miss Osborne. Mrs. ments made for the comfort of the visitors. Davies gave a recitation in the Lancashire dialect. FOR GLASS AND CHINA of every description try Mrs. Davenport with much feeling recited "The J., S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and Voiceless chimes." The girls' enjoyed the meeting 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. very much and were each presented with a pretty pledv</p>
        <p>motto card, 32 of them signed the temperance pledge Many of the girls are very poor and any old garments would be gladly received for their use. PHOTOS 12 for 6d.--INIAIIDEEN, 37, Sankey-st.--Advt, British Women's Temperance Association. A meeting of the above society was held on Tues- day afternoon in the Church Parlour Bold-street. There was a good attendance over which Miss Bessie Dutton presided. Mrs. Whitmore engaged in prayer. The Rev. J. S. Hughes who had very kindly come instead of the Rev. H. Dunnico who had been called away, gave an interesting address on the Welsh Revival. The rev, gentleman said that the revival had worked wonders in Wales, it had brought in great temperance reform for if the people were con- verted they would leave the drink alone and if we could only get this movement in our town what a benefit it would be to all classes. He spoke of the improvidence of the poor and suggested that a good work could be done by trying to teach these people how to cook and make the best use of things. Miss Bessie Dutton thanked Mr. Hughes for his very interesting address. Mrs. Garnett with a few instruc tive words seconded the motion.i PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BBST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Cairo-street Literary and Debating Society. On Tuesday evening, instead of the usual debate, the members of the above Society and a large gather- ing of their friends had the pleasure of listening to a lecture by Mr. J. S. Hill, entitled "Recollections of a holiday in St. Petersburg," illustrated with lime-light views. In the course of his lecture, Mr. Hill gave an account of a visit paid by him last summer to this famous city. After recounting the main incidents of the voyage, he related his experiences of Russian officialism at Cronstadt,and on arriving at the Port of St. Petersburg. He then proceeded to give an account of the principal places of interest in the Russian Capital, specially describing some of the scenes of the recent riots. He also expounded some of the native customs, notably the worshipping of icons. An interesting collection of views was thrown upon the screen, a number of them, including one of the Baltic Fleet, having been reproduced from photographs taken by Ur. Hill himself. The lecture throughout was listened to with very close attention, and many of the incidents narrated by the lecturer highly amused the audience. The chair was occupied by the Rev. C. Harvey Cook, and, before the close of the proceedings, the best thanks of the meeting were tendered Mr. Hill for his kindness in giving the lecture. Sacred Concert at the Theatre. A highly deserving object and a very interesting prograrLme were responsible for a crowded attend- ance at the sacred concert held at the Royal Court Theatre on Sunday afternoon in aid of the widow and children of the late Mr. Harry Whitehouse, who for 29 years was in the employ of Messrs: W. H. Smith and Son, and for nine years was stationed at the Warrington Central Station. With such enthusiastic support did the effort meet that the amount realized, including subscriptions, is expected to reach about £5O. The arrangements had been admirably carried out by a committee under the chairmanship of Mr. W. J. Shaw. Many local artistes willingly gave their services and the result was an excellent concert which was much appreciated. Songs were most effectively rendered by Mrs. Arthur Booth, Miss Polly Houghton, Miss Mabel Henshall, Mr. Peter Bate, Mr. Peter Berry, Mr. W. H. Ireland, and Mr. Fred Warburton who were all heard to the best advantage. The Lyric Quartette party gave an artistic and feeling interpretation of "God is a Spirit" and the Warrington United Choir, under the leadership of Mr. Percy George, rendered "The Hallelujah chorus "-and " Worthy is the lamb" with fine power and expression. Master Tom Barton's juvenile orchestra was in attendance and added much to the pleasure of the afternoon, while Master Barton very skilfully played a violin solo. Miss Turner's mandoline band also played in creditable style. Miss Maude Sloane contributed a pleasing pianoforte solo and Mr. Clement George recited well. A series of pictures, including "The passion play," were exhibited by means of the bioscope,manipulated by Mr. C. Wilkes. The duties of accompanists were discharged by Mr. W. H, Payton, Mus. Bac., and Mr. Frank Hickman.</p>
