      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>JUST INTRODUCED</head>
        <p>JUST INTRODUCED</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>GOLDEN BUTTER BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 11, 1905</p>
        <p>Billiards at the Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>The final for the Christmas billiard handicap was played at the Liberal club on Tuesday evening, between Dr. R. Smith, and Mr. R. Woods. The two men were on practically level terms and after a fine game the doctor won by two points in 200 up.</p>
        <p>St. Johns Literary and Social Society. At the usual fortnightly meeting of this society on Tuesday evening, the Rev. J. L. Milne, 8.D., being in the chair, Mr. J. Watson, Warrington, gave an interesting and instructive limelight lecture on " Our neighbours in space." At the close, on the motion of Mr. Milne, the Lecturer was accorded a hearty vote of thanks for his entertaining lecture.</p>
        <p>The Lloyd-George Meeting. Those who attended the great Liberal meeting held in the Parr Hall last Friday evening will no doubt be pleased to possess themselves of a moment° of the occasion in the shape of a booklet which has been printed at the " Examiner " office containing a full report of the meeting together with reports of the speeches of Mr. Asquith. at Heywood and Mr. John Morley at Newbury. The booklet is embellished with portraits of Mr. Lloyd-George and Mr. A. H. Crosfield, and copies may be obtained on application at the Liberal Club. Market Tenants' _Ball.</p>
        <p>_ A highly successful function was the eighth annual ball promoted by the Warrington Market Tenants' Association which was held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. Admirable arrangements had been made by a committee consisting of the following : Councillor Arthur Peacock (chairman), Messrs. W. Hewitt, J. Balliwell, A. Roberts, H. Turner, H. J. Pearse, A. T. Pemberton, Albert Peacock, I. Bower, and George Atkinson, the last named gentleman ably discharging the duties of hon. secretary. Mr. J. T. Barton's band gave an excellent selection of music and the assembly, numbering about 300, inEulged in dancing with zest until the early hours of the morning. The M.C.'s were Messrs. W. Hewitt and James Halliwell, while the catering was in the capable hands of Mr. John Atkinson, Golborne-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>PROGRESS OF CONGREGATIONALISM.</head>
        <p>THE PROGRESS OF CONGREGATIONALISM.</p>
        <p>Another Church Needed In Warrington.</p>
        <p>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD OPENS A SALE OF WORK AT COCKHEDGE.</p>
        <p>With the advancement of Congregationalism in War- rington the need for a second church has been increasingly felt. At present the small mission at Cockhedge serves to supplement the good work carried on at Wycliffe Church, but the building is not by any means adequate to meet the demands upon it and owing to its situation an extension could not be carried out. It is therefore proposed to erect a new and commodi- ous church, and with the object of forming the nucleus of a building fund a sale of work was opened in the mission onfThursday afternoon. The room had been very tastef ally decorated, the following ladies presiding at the stabs :—Congregational stall, Mrs. North, Mrs. Reid, and Mrs. McClure; Refreshment stall. Misses V. M. Taylor, F. Ashton, E. Joynson, M. Jovnson, N. Senior, Jessie Matlier, and F. Briggs, Mrs. Hackett and Mrs. Phcenix ; plant and flower stall, Mrs. Holland, Mrs. Pearson, Miss Wallington; Sunday school stall, Mrs. Clayton, Miss Alice Clayton, Miss Gertrude Clayton, Mrs. Morrall, Misses Alice Pearson, Nellie Johnson, Lucy Senior, M. Senior, and Emily Mather •, sewing class stall, Mesdames Ashton, Daniels, Shaw, Wallington, Warharst, and Williams. The sale was opened by Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield, the chair being taken by Mr. Charles E. Parker. Others present included Mrs. Parker' the Rev. John Yonge and ' Mrs. Yonge and Rev. Harry Wall. The Rev. JOHN YONGE said the sale had been arranged by the energy,enthusiasm and enterprise of their friends who were accustomed to worship in that room under the leadership of their minister, the Rev. Harry Wall. The purpose of it was to help to form the nucleus of a fund which should, at no very distant date they hoped, be successfully raised to provide the friends of the mission with a more commodious and comely sanctuary than that which they at present possessed. He would not say that the new sanctuary was as yet in the clouds, but certainly it had not got an inch of ground to build upon, for they had not found a site. They had, however, im idea of one or two sites and, concerning the location of them, except that they would not be immediately contiguous to the present plot, it was perhaps not expedient that he should give any specific directions. Until they were perfectly sure of their ground they had better not try to stand upon it or they might find themselves disappointed. They hoped before very long to secure a site, but even that would mean money so that the sale was an immediate necessity. He believed the mission workers had a comparatively modest idea as to the financial outcome of the effort, and he would be delighted if these expectations were exceeded. All the accommodation at the mission was fully utilized and they had no room for expansion. There were parts of the town not far away where there appeared to be a very great necessity for additioaal means of grace. They thought as Congregationalists that it was more than time that there was an extension of Congregationalism in the Borough of Warrington. (Hear, hear.) For all these years there had simply been the one church to which the friends at Cockhedge were attached in membership, and it was time there was a second. The town was growing somewhat rapidly, and the least Congrega- tionalists could do was to take their full share in making provision for the religious accommodation which was needed. (Applause.) Mr. PARKER then took the chair. He was sure he was speaking for Mr. Crosfield and himself when he said what a pleasure it was for them to be present. He scarcely thought the great men of the country recog- nized what an important work such mission halls as that with which they were-associated were accomplishing. In the various educational institutions there was a reat organization for looking after the intellectual interests of the children of the town. The work carried on in missions was even more important beoause he put moral culture a great deal higher than intellectual culture. At that mission they raised a standard which would ever stand for national righteousness. Their ideal was a high one—the moral uplifting of the people —and as such it deserved the hearty support of all who loved their country. (Hear, hear.)• At such a function as that Mr. Crosfield and himself felt very much like Square men in round holes—(laughter)—for although they might in general terms admire the work displayed, they would be very far at sea if they attempted to offer any intelli- gent detailed criticism. (Laughter.) Nevertheless it was a pleasure to them to assist the work in any way. Mr. Parker concluded with remarks in a humorous strain, indulging in chaff at the expense of Mr. Crosfield, who, he said, as an unmarried man would be obliged to make a tour of the stalls and purchase a variety of wares which he did not really require—(laughter)—whereas he (Mr. Parker) could delegate that little duty to his wife. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was warmly received, said he was not quite prepared for the amount of banter and good-humoured raillery they had had from Mr. Parker, There was an old saying about aßoland for'an Oliver, but he was afraid he could not on the spur iof the moment quite pay Mr. Parker back in his own coin. He was sure he would be treading on very dangerous ground if he entered upon the question of matrimony (Laughter.) Apparently his friend in the chair thought there was a sad lack of enterprise in his (Mr. Cros- field's) composition. (Laughter.) He had alluded to the example he (Mr. Parker) had set, and possibly some of those present might take advantage and courage by that example and follow in the footsteps of their chair- man. (Laughter). Mr. Parker had given a most charming address. He (Mr. Crosfield) was afraid he could not offer any remarks that would be so apropos and so very pleas- ant to listen to, but he wished to thank them sincerely for their kindness in asking him there. What added to the interest of the visit from his point of view was the fact that that mission was started by an old friend of his and a still longer friend of his father's, Mr. Samuel Rigby. (Applause.) He dare say a good many of them could recall as he could, the familiar figure of Mr. Rigby, with his old-fashioned white top hat and quaint, somewhat old-fashioned manner, and the old world style he had in the days when things were quieter than they were to-day. No doubt many could recall the genial and pleasant way Mr. Rigby bad. When he (Mr. Cros- field) heard that Mr. Rigby started the mission in 1872 he wondered whether it was founded just before or after his father started the mission at Bank Quay. It, would be justabont the same time because he remem- bered his mother laying the foundation ston&amp;of the latter He had often heard his father say—and they knew he was a broad-minded man that never in his life had he spent any money that brought him more permanent satisfaction that the money he spent upon the mission at Bank Quay. (Laughter.) He did not doubt that their dear old friend Mr. Rigby had the same feeling of satisfaction in starting the mission at that end of the tovvn. He hoped the twc missions and other missions in Warrington also might continue and, if he might so express it, run neck and I neck in their endeavour to do good work in raising the inora/eand well-being of the people in the neighbour- hoods in which they were situated. He would like to refer to a very important speech, to his mind one of the most striking speeches that has been made for many , a long day, It was addressed to the Church Congress in Liverpool not long since by Professor Oliver Lodge' and in it he spoke of the mistake that people made nowadays in fearing that as science opened up new fields of wonder to us and modified the more ancient forms of religious belief it would have the effect of cutting the ground from beneath the fundamental basis of religion. He (Mr. Crosfield) was very glad to see Professor Oliver Lodge tackle that supremely important question in the very noble way in which he did. He was sure they must all feel that the work for the moral and intellectua</p>
        <p>welfare of the people that missions of that kind carried on was as necessary now as ever it was. The Chairman had told them that on such an occasion they on the platform were like square men in round holes. That did not seem to him (Mr. Crosfield) to be anything very ornamental—(laughter)—but if they could not be ornamental they might at least strike at the useful. Whether they were successful in that or not, he Was delighted to see on every side such evidences of both the ornamental and useful, to that they would have plenty of opportunity of doing what little they could to help the sale. He hcped the result would be such n success as would encourage the good cause in that part of the town, and encourage the friends of the mission who had the work chiefly at heart. (Applause.) The Rev. H. WALL moved a vote of thanks to Mr Crosfleld and Mr. Parker. They were convinced that as the outcome of their efforts they would take one great step towards the completion of the work they had set out to do. He believed it was twenty-one years this month since there was a sale of work in the mission. He hoped it would not be so long before they had another, but that it in future they would have a more commodi- ous room for the purpose. The work had for some time been making gradual progress and he thought the future was hopeful. (Applause.) Mr. HOLLAND seconded the motion which was heartily agreed to. Mr. CROSFIELD briefly replied, thanking the meeting for the kindness and friendliness with which they had received Mr. Parker and himself, and had borne the ineffectual attempts they had made to discharge the duties which devolved upon them. (Applause.) The proceeds on the day amounted to £4l. YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the sale was re-opened by Mrs. Henry Roberts, Mr. A. H. Lee presiding. Prayer having been offered by the Rev. J. L. Milne, The CHAIRMAN expressed his full concurrence in the object of the sale. He had long felt that something more ought to be done by Congregationalism in War- rington and the denomination ought certainly to be represented by two churches in a town of this size. (Applause,) Mrs. ROBERTS. in an interesting little speech,said the congregation had far outgrown the missionand she was in great sympatny, with their desire to enlarge their premises that their work might not be crippled. Their effort was worthy of all praise and she hoped God would bless the mission and that when the new church was built it would be in every sense a church to the honour and glory of God. The Rev. H. WALL proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Roberts and Mr. Lee, and pointed out that Mr. Lee had been connected with the mission for many years as ' a worker. They were pleased also that Mr. Henry Roberts, who was at one time. superintendent of the Sunday School, was present. Mr. W. H. WALLINGTON seconded the vote which was heartily carried. In acknowledging the compliment Mr. HENRY ROBERTS said he had since entering the room been told of an occasion thirty-six years ago when his father preached at the mission. Previously he did not know that his father had ever preached in public and it was only an addition, if possible, tr, the affectionate memories he had of his work. Mr. Roberts alluded to the interesting fact that in 1880 when Mr. MeMinnies took the chair at a meeting in the mission he (Mr. Roberts) brought him word during the evening that the dissolution had been announced. In conclusion Mr. Roberts said he would bn pleased to give £5O towards the purchase of the new site, in memory of his father. (Applause.) The Rev. JOIN YONGE observed that he hoped before the sale ended they might receive the news of another dissolution. (Laughter.) The sooner it came the better. (Hear, hear.) Yesterday's proceeds were £3B making a total of £79.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Revised Contract Arrangements.</p>
        <p>TO LEAVE NO "GROUND FOR SUSPICION."</p>
        <p>The Warrington Beard of Guardians met at the Work- house under the presidency, of Mr. A. Brooks. There were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Revs. Dr. Mercer and T. R. Matthews, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. Hickman, G. Thompson, S. Davies, G. Mather, R. Forshaw, J. Billinge, T. H. Ash- croft, J. Turton W. Flannagan, M. W. Seton, 3. Back- house, W. Houghton. J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools). REVISED CONTRACT ARRANGEMENTS. IMPORTANT RECOMMENDATION 3. The following report of a special committee ap- pointed to consider the contract arrangements was submitted :—Your Committee have given long, frequent and careful consideration to the matters referred to them, have patiently investigated the methods of obtain- ing and dealing with tenders in other Unions and now submit to you certain proposals which they believe would ensure competition, a probably more econbmical and certainly more satisfactory supply to your institu- tions, and which would eliminate, as far as possible, any personal influences during the selection of tenders. In view of the adverse criticisms of existing methods which were one of the reasons of its appointment, and of the necessity for leaving no reasonable ground for any suspicion of unfairness if tenders are to be obtained from firms of repute, your committee attach special importance to this last point. Your Committee are unanimously of opinion that separate tenders should be obtained for the Workhouse and the Cottage Homes, I that samples should be kept at each institution, that the tenders should be distinctly endorsed, sent to each I institution and the power of selection and acceptance delegated to the respective committees ;that the tenders be opened by the Acting Chairman and that arrange- ments be made by means of numbers and duplicates whereby, until a tender has been approved for amen- I ance, the name of the tenderer shall not be known to the Committee, but that the final acceptance shall not be made until the name of the tenderer has been sub- mitted to and approved by the Committee. Your Com- mittee are farther of opinion that the period of each contract should be,aa heretofore, six months, except for milk, which should be for a year, and that the existing custom of each committee selecting for quarterly periods, tradesmen for its minor requirements outside the contracts, should be definitely recognised by the Board. Your committee also recommend that your standing orders be modified so far as may be necessary to meet these proposals and in order that this revised system may be brought into operation for the next half year's contracts, the Committee should be empowered to forthwith arrange the details of such modifications of the existing forms of tender as may be necessary to meet the needs of the Workhouse and Cottage Homes respectively' and to provide suitable samples to be kept at each institution. Your Committee desire to express their appreciation of the valuable assistance rendered in respect of many matters of detail by Messrs. Awbery and Wright.—ALFRED BROOKS, Chairman. The antic said there should be very little difficulty in carrying out the report, which contained some very good suggestions with regard to the receipt and open- ing of tenders. It was laid down clearly by the law that tenders must come before the Board in the first instance. That could be done in a formal way. Instead of being opened in committee as at present, the tenders could come before the Board and be opened, and the committee could then proceed teJ deal with them. The question of coal contracts had been a sore point for several years past. He did not know how the Board 'proposed to get over that difficulty. The objection really arose through the idea—which might be a mis- taken one, but still it obtained—that there was a prejudice on the part of some of the Guardians favour- able to Messrs. Evans's coal. He did not see, if the matter came before a committee, how they could avoid that committee knowing,although the names might not be gigen to them, whose tenders they were considering. The description of the coal was gi,ren in the tender and any person familiar with the collieries could tell who the tendering parties were.</p>
        <p>Mr. IftclntAN asked whether the Board intended to adopt a new form of contract. The old form was so worded that it had caused a great deal of friction. The description of the various goods required was not clear and precise. The CHAIRMAN said the Committee suggested a new form. 11/1 Mr. BELLI-NEE said the object of the committee was to ensure more competition. Alluding to the coal con- tract, he remarked that if the Board would see its way to send an order to his district where work was slack, it would be appreciated. Mr. THomesoN : I thought canvassing was pro- hibited. (Laughter). rhe report was adopted. On the motion of Mr. BILLINGE seconded by Mr. THOMPSON, the committee was empowered to carry out the suggestions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DEPUTATION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>A DEPUTATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The CLERK announced that the Central Poor Law Conference would be held in London on February 21st and 22nd under the presidency of the Right Hon. Sir Edward Fry. Mr. BILLINGE moved that the Board be represented by the Chairman and vice-chairman (Mr. J. W.Knowles) together with the Clerk. Mr. THOMPsON proposed as an amendment that the Chairman and Clerk should be the deputation. He I considered it was a waste of money for three to go, and the Board could ill-afford it. Mr. ASHCROFT seconded. The CLERK: It is an old custom to send three. Mr. THOMPSoN : Let us break through it then, and the sooner the better. Mr. BILLHIGE: If other Unions took the same atti- tude there would be no conference at all.</p>
        <p>Mr. ASHCROFT : If no one went we should get a full report of the meetings. The CLERK: There would be no meetings. (Laughter) Mr. DAVIES was of the opinion that the honour of attending. the conference should be shared by all the members In turn, taking them in order of seniority. The CLERK: That would affect the ladies.</p>
        <p>Mr. FORSHAW supported Mr. Billinge's motion, although he admitted that he had never seen any results from the attendance of Warrington representatives. The amendment was defeated by nine votes to seven. THE DUTIES OF RELIEVING OFFICERS. SUGGESTED RE-ORGANIZATION.</p>
        <p>The Board then considered an application by Mr.F.H. Aukland, relieving officer, for an increase of his salary which now stands at .£ll5 per annum. The CHAIRMAN said the matter had been under the consideration of the number two (country) district relief committee. They all knew that the work of the relieving officers had greatly increased during the last two years. Mr. Garnett's district in particular was a very heavy one to work and at the present time, to put it plainly, Mr. Garnett was overworked. The Relief Committee bad an idea that Mr. Aukland—to put it mildly—was not overworked and they suggested the formation of a small committee to go into the matter of r3lief districts and see if any allocation between the two relieving officers could be made in order to relieve IMr. Garnett of the pressure and give Mr.Aukland some- thing more to do before they began to talk about raising his salary. He moved that such a committee be appointed. Mr. MATHER seconded. It was the unanimous opinion of the relief committee that it was the best step to take in the interests of the Board, the poor people, and the relieving officers themselves. Mr. HOUGHTON supported. The REV. DR. MEncEtt agreed with what had been said but he would ask if the time had not come for another point to be submitted from the considera- tion of the committee. In other unions the principle of having a cross-visitor or inquiry officer had worked well. He thought the Union would benefit if they had an officer who was free to do thatiwork.D uring the week he could visit the districts unexpectedly and see how the relief granted by the committees was being used in the various homes. He moved that the committee should take that side of the question into considera Mr. THOMPsON seconded. Mr. HICKMAN said that if the Board appointed such an officer they would be carrying out the suggestion made by the master in his recent report. The members of the committee were agreed as to the advisability of having an officer to see that the relief' was being administered wisely and well. Mr. FORSHAW suggested if Mr. Shearer, who was one of the best relieving officers, was relieved of a great deal of offico work he might undertake the cross-visit- ing. If a new official would mean a further expendi- ture of £2OO a year he would be loth to agree to the change. Mr. ASHCROFT thought the committee might try to do something in the way of re-organization without increasing the staff. He did not think they needed a kind of detective inspector. Mr. JOLLEY did not see that they experienced the need which was felt in some unions for a cross-visitor. If they were going to have the work of such an officer carried out on good and systematic lines, they must not suppose they were going to get a competent man for less than £2OO a year. He sympa- thised with the suggsstion to re-arrange the districts, but he did not think an additional officer was necessary or even desirable. The Committee were not unanimous in approving the suggestion,as a discussion on the point was withheld. _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. CLAY suggested that a lady would make a very efficient visitor. . .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said his impression was that Warring- toil was far too small to support a cross-visitor. Their present officers could do their own visiting very well indeed.</p>
        <p>Eventually the Board appointed the following om- mittea to take the whole subject into consideration The Chairman, Mrs. Clay, Miss Broadbent, Mr. G. Mather, Mr. A. .1. Jolley, and Mr. S. Babies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>ODDFELLOWS AT DINNER.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS AT DINNER.</p>
        <p>A Successful Warrington Lodge. The Prince of Wales Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Mannhester Unity) enjoys a high repu- tation among the friendly society organizations of the district, and striking evidence of its continued progress was afforded at the fourth annual dinner which was held in the well-appolnted club room at the Pelican Hotel on Thursday evening. There was a large attend- ance and the gathering was in every way a pronounced success. An excellent dinner was served by the host and hostess, Mr. and Mrs. W. Chalmers. At the after' proceedings the chair was taken by Bro. T. P. Bradshaw Prov. who was supported by Prov, G.M.Joseph Taylor, P James Massey, R. H. Holford and F. Webster ( C.S.), P.G.'s T. Wadsworth (Lodge Secretary), a F. Freeman, E. A. Sanders, and T. Reddish (N G. of the Lodge). The C rntttxsaid the Manchester Unity had gained the highest position in the return prepared by the registrar, and they had every reason to feel proud of the fact. The Order had a capital of £12,000,000 and a membership of over 1,000,000 ; the increase in the capital during the year having been half a million. He was pleased the Prince of %Vales Lodge continued to hold such a a orthy place as the premier friendly society lodge in Warrington. They had paid out over £325 in sick benefits and had placed £156 to the credit of their funds. They had a membership of 464 and I with a little exertion on the part of the ()facers and members they hepPd to increase that figure to 500 during the year. The lodg,e funds totalled £3,000. Bro. J. MASSEY, P.P.U.NI., proposed the toast of "The Manchester Unity Board of Directors" and briefly traced the progress Of the order. The members of the I Board were men of Intelligence, zeal, and perseverance, I who had had to prove their fitness for the positions through many years of trial and experience. 13r0. J. TAYLOR, Pow. G.M. responded. He hoped the time was not far distant when Warrington might be directly represented on the Board of Directors. The Prince of Wales lodge had in its midst professional men quite capable undertaking duties oa the Board which would call forth the energies they possessed (Applause.) Bro. R. H. HALFORD, P.P.G.M. progosed " The Wat- rington District "which, he said was doing a very good work.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN moved that the report of the sub- committee be adopted as far as possible. The coal contract would be difficult to deal with, whatever regu- lations were enforced. The committee had done their best to clear away any cause for the complaint that personal influence was used either by the Board or the committees for the sake of individual tenderers. With the exception perhaps of coal he thought the subject of contracts should be dealt with in accordance with the committee's suggestions,. and the new arrangement would remove any suspicion there might be in the minds of individual guardians or the outside public as to undue influence being brought to bear. Mr. JOLLEY seconded. He said the committee had given a great Mal of thought to the matter and had tried to meet the wishes which had been voiced on several occasions at Board meetings and to amend the method hitherto adopted and which was felt in Some ways to be unsatisfactory. The clerk had raised n technical point in connection with the consolidated orders which had the force of law. It was purely a technical point and there was a way round. As a matter of fact, if the consolidated orders were observed in the most rigid and literal manner, the Board did not carry them out now. The new regulations were drafted for the purpose of achieving what the spiritlof the consoli- dated orders was. It was quite possible, if the Board wished it. to carry out the strict letter of the law and act on the spirit which underlay the law, and secure that the contracts should be dealt with in an absolutely fair and important way. If the Board adopted the report and acted on the suggestion embodied in the , InSt paragraph, the committee would be able to bring PENKETH the revised system into operation for the next half- rft, n, year. He ventured to think that if the Board approved ESENTATLON.—On Saturday last a number of friends of Mr. Joseph Foden met in the Wesleyan the action of the committee, considerable reform would • Schoolroom to present him with a token of their regret be affected. He did not say they were going lot of money by the change—he did not think it was to save saa.venat at his severing his connection with the Wesleyan I Chapel. Mr. Foden is leaving the village to reside in all probable that they would—but he believed they Liverpool but has for many years been an earnest and would get much more satisfaction, valued worker in the cause of Wesleyanism. The gift Some discussion took place with regard to the coal selected was a secretaire and silver inkstand.subscribet2l contract. should he for by the congregation and Sunday school. Mr. Mr. THOMPSON suggested that tenders Parker made the presentatioa, and thanked Mr. Foden • invited for fuel suitable for the relnirements of the for his work among them. Mr. Foden feelingly House. If the coal that was being used at the time a responded. On Sunday afternoon, Mr. Foden took fresh supply was needed was suitable, the fuel tendered leave of the scholars end teachers of the Sunday school, for must be equal to that sample. If the coal did not where he has worked for so long. come up to the requirements of the House, they could se to take it in.</p>
        <p>„...... In responding Bro. Frank Webster, Prov. C.S. said the gain to the distrit lodges sick and funeral fund dur- ing the year was £a9o 15s. Bd. while on the district funeral fund there was anladvance of .£226 Cs. 10d..mak- inga total gain of £914 2s. 6d. They had met death claims of £435 which was £95 above the previous year. There had been a slight decrease in membership but he attributed it not so much to tontine clubs as to bad trade. The toasts of "The Prince of \Vales Lodge ” was entrusted to Bro. W. Holbrook, P. 0., who paid a t ibute to the work of the lodge officers. Bro. T. WAnswourit, P.G. (lodge secretary) res- ponded. The lodge, he said, was an important factor in the life of Warrington, and the amount of good it was doing was almost impossible to reelize. The total receipts for one year alone amounted to £BOO. Their position had not been attained by leaps and bounds, but by steady progress and hard work. (Applause.) The health of The host and hostess" was drunk on the proposition of Bro. T. REDDISH, P.G. During the evening songs were well rendered by Bro. J. Willett, Bro. T. Reddish, and Bro. Alban Boyle, while ' recitations by Bro. E. A. Sanders created considerable amusement. P.G. Fred Webster acted as accompanist. The artistes and committee which had charge of the , arrangements were heartily thanked at the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>1 LICENSING SESSIONS. IINCREASE IN CASES OF DRUNKENNESS AT WARRINGTON.; –</head>
        <p>1 LICENSING SESSIONS. IINCREASE IN CASES OF DRUNKEN- NESS AT WARRINGTON.; -</p>
        <p>The George Rote! Licence.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED "CORPORATION JOBBERY."</p>
        <p>There was a dearth of important business at the annual licensing sessions for the Borough of War- rington which were held at the Police Court yesterday morning. Only two new applications—and those for billiard licences—came up for decision. The interest- ing question of the future of the George Hotel licence , was mentioned and the Bench decided to defer the , consideration of its renewal until the adjourned sessions on March 7th. The members of the licensing committee present were :—Mr. John Fairclough (presiding), Alderman Francomb, Ur. C. White, and Mr. H. Roberts, while the other magistrates on the Bench were :—Tne Mayor,Alderman Smethurst,Coun- cillors Roberts, Wilkinson, and Tinnion, Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberts, L. W. Reynolds, H. Thornton, A.J. Jolley, C. Jackson Holmes, and G. Pilling. The solicitors appearing were Mr. Arthur Browne (on , behalf of Messrs. (Imenall, Whitley and Co. and Mr. , Benjamin Kenyon), Mr. T. S. Steel (for the Off-licence Holders' Association and others), Mr. Fenna (for the London and North-Western Railway Co.) and Mr. Stanley Wilson, deputy town clerk (for the Corporation.) The Chairman said he wished it to be understood that no magistrates who were not members of the licensing committee would have a voice in what was going on. THE CHIEF CONSTABLE'S REPORT,</p>
        <p>SLIGHT INCRE XSE IN CASES OF DRUNKENNESS. The Chief Constable (Mr. Luke Talbot) presented his annual report as follows :—Gentlemen, I have the honour to present to you my ninth annual report relative to the business of the Brewster Sessions, together with the detailed information and statistics in connection therewith, for the year ended December 31,st. 1904. The population of the Borough, according to census of 1901, was (14,242; the presentestimated popu- lation is 68,000. There are in the Borough 86 alehouses, 36 on-beerhouses (the license of the " Star and Garter," Friars'-green, having lapsed by arrangement), 1 refresh- ment house, with on-and-off wine license. and 84 off- licensed houses (including wine, spirits, &amp;c.), making a total of 207. The proportion of inhabitants to each license is 310.34. In Table 3 of this report are shown corresponding figures from 15 Northern towns, most of them approximating in size to ,Warrington the average in these being one license to each 33286 inhabitants. During the past year the licenses transferred were as follows :-12 alehouses, 3 beerhouses, and 8 off-licenses, making a total of 23 as against 28 in 1903 332 oersons were charged with drunkenness during the year, an increase of 24 compared with 1903. It must be borne in mind, however, that 70 of the charges repre- sented only 30 persons proceeded against, 2 having been convicted five times, 4 persons 3 times, and 24 twice each. I can assign no special reason for the slight increase in these prosecutions which fluctuate from year to year from a variety of causes. The average number of charges of drunkenness in Warrington during the past decade is 309.8, for details of which see Table 1. The average number of charges of drunkenness in the 15 towns before mentioned is 5'650 each 1,000 inhabitants the corresponding figures for Warrington being 5'16. The following is the classification of the 332 persons disposed of for drunkenness in Warrington during 1914, viz. :-240 were males and 92 females; of these 318 were convicted, 14 being discharged ; 237 were residents of the Borough and 95 strangers.</p>
        <p>CONDUCT OF LICENSED HOWES. During the year two alehouse keepers were prosecuted one for allowing gaming upon his licensed premises, and the other for permitting drunkenness ; they were both convicted and fined. Generally speaking, the licensed houses have been conducted in a satisfactory manner. 803 visits of inspection were made to licensed houses at irregular intervals by inspectors and sergeants during 1904 ; as a result 33 licensees were formally cautioned by these officers for trifling irregularities. A careful record of all these visits is kept for future reference. As in former years, the members of the Force have greatly assisted licensees by calling their attention to drunken persons and women of bad charac- ter who may have,been.seeti.”tering licensed premises.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MUSIC LICENCES.</head>
        <p>MUSIC LICENCES.</p>
        <p>Two alehouses—the "Lion" and "Patten Arms" Hotels—are licensed for public music and singing (the licensed justices last year deciding to renew only these two.) The "Lion Hotel." in addition, has a dancing licence. Temporary (14 days) music licenses were granted in the cases of 21 alehouses during the year. The applications were made for the Walking Day, Mid- summer, and Christmas holidays. lam glad to report that in no case was the privilege abused. ALTERATIONS TO LICENSED HOUSES.</p>
        <p>The following houses have been completed in accord- ance with directions of the Licensing Bench of 1903, viz.: The "Red Lion" alehouse. Winwick road, and the " Ring's Head " alehouse, Winwick-street, " Old House at Home" beerhouse, Brick-street, and the "Railway Inn" beerhouse, Knutsford-road. The re- building of the " Brickmakers' Arms" beerhouse, School-brow, is nearly completed. These alterations and rebuildings have been strictly in 'accordance with the approved plans.—l have the honour to be, gentle- men, your obedient servant, LUKE TALBOT (Chief Con- stable).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>GEORGE ?HOTEL LICENCE. AN OFFER OF £l,OOO. MR. BROWNE SUSPECTS " CORPORATION JOBBERY."</head>
        <p>THE GEORGE ?HOTEL LICENCE. AN OFFER OF £l,OOO. MR. BROWNE SUSPECTS " CORPORATION JOBBERY."</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Browns said he was instructed by Mr. Benjamin Kenyon, of the Green Afan,to ask the Deputy Town Clerk, who was present, what was proposed to be done with the George Hotel licence. He had given an offer to the Town Clerk of £l,OOO for the town if the removal of the George was granted to Mr. Kenyon,and he received a courteons reply from the Town Clerk stating that Messrs. Walker's offer was not yet disposed of. He would like the deputy town clerk to state pub- licly what was the position. Supposing Messrs' Walker's offer was "off" and Mr. Kenyon made an offer of RD 00 on condition that the George licence was transferredto the Green Man, it would, in the event of the magis- trates agreeing, mean a decrease of one beerhouse without .any addition to other premises.</p>
        <p>The Charrman said the licensing committee wss a new one and the members had not held meetings of any description. Whatever decisions they came to that day would be simply on the applications brought before them. He did not know that they could give Mr. Browne any promise. Mr. Browne: I don't ask for any promise. The Chairman t I can give no information because we have not met before, _</p>
        <p>Mr. Stanley Wilson said he was not in a position to give the information which Mr. Browne asked for. He appeared to ask the Bench to renew the licence, and the reeson he was there was because Mr. Steel had informed him that he was instructed to apply to their wotships for an adjournment of the case to the adjourned licens- ing sessions. If Mr. Steel would state the reasons for his appearance, he would be glad. Mr. Steel said he was instructed to ask the Bench to allow the matter to stand over until the adjourned licensing sessions in order that proper notice of objec- tion to the renewal might be given. He appeared on behalf of certain residents in the town and their names would be stated in the notice of objection together with the grounds on which they objected. He asked the Bench not to renew the licence that morning but to go into the whole matter at the adjourned sessions. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr: Browne: In other words, what is it—the Cor- poration? It is perfectly plain what the object is. They are going to apply to you to refuse the licence of the George and come upon the compensation fund. It sounds something like Corporation jobbery if you ask me. The Chairman : We ha co nothing to do with that in any shape. Mr. Browne : I represent Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. Mr. Steel : I shall take exception to any question of Corporation jobbery. I am instructed by bona Wcie persons to raise the objection. That is why r appear this morning. Mr. Wilson: I was just going to get up and object to his appearing. He was no locus standl. Mr. Roberts: lam sure you did not use the expres- sion in the sense it was perhaps understood. Mr. Browne: I intended nothing offensive. It may be a bit out of the way, but one has one's own opinion after all. Mr. Wilson: No one knows better than Mr. Browne the negotiations which have been going on in connec- tion with the licence. Under the circumstances I do not object to the adjournment. Mr. Browne: lam instructed by Messrs. Greensll Whitley &amp; Co., to represent them. They own by far the larger number of houses in Warrington and they object to this compensation being put upon them to pay. Mr. Wilson : That is not before the court this morn- ing. Mr. Browne : That is the question. After the magistrates had consulted in private the Chairman said they had decided to renew the whole of alehouse licences with the exception of that of the George Hotel which would be considered at the :W- I joured licensing sessions. OTHER RENEWALS.</p>
        <p>I The magistrates renewed the whole of the beerhouse and off licences, the music licences of the Lion Hotel and Patten Arms, and the hillier,: licence of the ' Winmarleigh Oal.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>Notice had been given of an application by Adolphus Sheffield, secretary of IMessrs.Daniel Melia and Co.. for</p>
        <p>n off-wine licence for the premises in Horsemarket- street. It was, however, announced that the applica- cation had been withdrawn.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR BILLIARD LICENCES. Mr. Browne applied on behalf of William Edward Longmore, Railway. Inn, Factory-lane. for a billiard licence. He pointed out that whereas a licensed victualler was authorized to have a billiard table, a beerhouse keeper must make special application to the Bench for the same privilege. The table owned by Mr. Longmore was only a semi-billiard table. It had been used for about nine years and it was not known that the licence° was doing wrong until April last when the Chief Constable very properly told Mr. Longmore that he ought to have a licence. He understood Mr. Talbot said a few months ago that the room was a very good one.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable : Oh, no. I am opposing it this morning.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne : I am instructed that you told Mr. Long- more it was a very good room, and most suitable for a billiard table.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable : Mr. Longmore has misin- formed you. Mr. Browne went on to submit that a billiard table ate _public-house " did no harm." Workingmen could not have tables in their homes, and they were entitled to have a game. There were no other billiard tables in that neighbourhood. _</p>
        <p>The Ciiief Constable said he did not object on the ground of the harmful nature of the game of billiards, but on the ground that the room was unfitted by its situation in the house, and the table was not a billiard table at all, but only a semi- billiard table. A short time ago the owners of the ' house came to the Bench and said the residential quarters in the house were too small and they obtained permission to extend them. Now it was propased to take a portion of the residential quarters for the billiard room. It was situated right among the bedrooms and next to the sitting-room. It was at present used • to store lumber in, and it VMS so far removed from the area of supervision of the licencee that the customers had to communicate with the bar by metaphone. There would not be any supervision to prevent betting or any other malpractices. The customers would be taken into the heart of the private dwelling, and that, he con- tended. was altogether contrary to the policy of the Bench. Sergeant Williamson corroborated this statement. He added that the measurements of the room were 141 t. byl2ft. 6in. and it was M. 51n. high The table was a three-quarter size one. The Chief Constable said he did not suggest that the applicant or his predecessor had the slighest knowledge that there was any necessity for the licence. Sergeant Williamson said he had ascertained that no marker would be employed. No provision was made for the continual oversight of the room, the licence° only going up occasionally. Mr. Browne then applied for a billiard licence on behalf of James Payton, Blueback Inn, Allen-street. He said this was also a semi-billiard table and Mr. Payton bought it for his own use. He subsequently found that ass. beerhouse keeper he nad no right to use it without a licence. He had used the table principally for himself and friends. .</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said the room in this case was a good one, and was well under the supervision of the licencee. Tne only objection he had was the objection he took generally to the games on licensed premises— that they were simply an inducement to visit the houses. Mr. Browne said the room would not be used on Wed- nesdays or Saturdays, as it would be required for market purposes. The Bench decided to refuse the application in respect of the Railway Inn, but to grant Mr. Payton's, subject to the table not being used on Wednesday or Saturday. Mr. Browne: It is to my client's advantage not to use it.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable : I know that if Mr. Payton gives an undertaking it will be kept. ADJOURNED SESSIONS. The adjourned sessions were flied for Tuesday, March 7th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE. "A National System of Education"</head>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE. "A National System of Education"</p>
        <p>Discussed.</p>
        <p>The weekly meet:rig of the above League was held in the large room of the Liberal Club on Monday evening last. Mr. Joseph Howes, took the chair and was supported by Messrs. Joseph Elwell, E. Osborne, T. Morton, Wilkinson Hague, T. Barnes, E. W. Walker S. Wright, J.P., and many others. The subject down for discussion was "A National System of Education," and was introduced by the Rev. H. Dillanico. In the course of his address Mr. Dunnico said that one of the most distressing features of English Educe- tionalism was the apathy and unconcern of the very persons who ought to be most concerned, viz., the parents of. the children who used the schools. In Germany the meanest day labourer was keenly alive not only to the broad features of his common school system but also about the details of the work within. The same thing held good in almost every other civi- lized country save our own. The result was that whilst in those countries National Education flourished man atmosphere of national sympathy, in ours it languished in the shade of public apathy, disregard, and indiffer- ence, Directly any Parliamentary proposal was made to deal with education in this country the people who took the field *ere not the persons who ought to take it, namely the parents of the children, but the parsons, theologians and professional politicians. If only the parents of the children realised their responsibilities and the momentous issues at stake, they would ruthlessly .sweep aside all intreders and settle the question on wise and liberal lines. rt ought to be made possible for every child -no matter how humble his extraction—to have opportunities of a moral intellectual and physical education limited only by the limits it had pleased God to put upon his capacities. It was with tho hope that the members of that league would be induced to put forth every effort to secure a national system of education, adequate representation, equal free and freed from the fatal interference of parish parson and pastor that he had con- sented to be with them that evening. Mr. Dunnico then gave a short survey of the history of elementary education in this land and to simplify matters divided same into three periods. The first period prior to 1870, second from 1870 to the passing of the Act 1902, third period under the present Act. Deal- ing with the first period Mr. Dunnico said that it VMS frequently asserted that we were indebted to the Church of England for commencing the work of edu- cating the children. He wished to give one emphatic denial to that statement and said that it was Noncon- formists and not Churchmen who were entitled to that proud distinction. The Anglican Church resisted edu- cation as long as she could and when she did take iit up it was n a spirit of hostility and rivalry. Dealing with the act of 1370 Mr. Dunnico said that there was only one point upon which the Act was clearland that was that somehow and somewhere every child must go to school and if sufficient schools did not exist they must be provide. Upon every other part as far as he could see there was either vagueness or illogical com- promise. It might be treason to say what he was going to say but he would say it nevertheless that the Non- conformists were betrayed by the Liberals of 1868. The Radical and Democratic element in the House of Com- mons and the Nonconformists outside opposed the com- promise and it \tag only carried with the aid of Tory votes. The Leading Radicals and Nonconformists thought then as many of them thought now, that the sole business of the , state was to educate and not to teach religion That was a sound intelligible principle and if that had been adhered to then wllk should have saved ourselves thirty years of confusion and this present regrettable struggle. Dealing with the act of 1902 Mr. Dunnico subjected the same to a severe criticism. It had placed a premium on inefficiency by destroying Board Schools. It placed denominational schools on the rates without giving any sort of public control. It closed the doors of 16,410 sedmo/s so far a. the heed teacherships were concerned to all who would not subscribe to the dogmas of the established church. It compelled people to pay for the teaching of dorm: is which in their opinious were pernicious. It prevented the erection of public schools in any• parish if the priest, parson or other persons volunteered to provide them —ln conclusion Mr. Dunnico stated that it was one of ' the gravest misfortunes that Education had been made a party question for it was a national questien. In 1870 there was a compromise, and though neither side was satisfied still attempts were made to conciliate both sides. The Nonconformists had loyally kept the com- promise until it was broken by the present Act, but now th -t it had been broken the way was clear for the nation to take it up and settle it on the broad principle of simple justice of freedom for all classes. An animated discussion followed, in which Messrs. Hague, Cotterell, Osborne, England, Ellwell, Wright, and many others took part. Mr. Dim-Nrco replied, ana the meeting closed with a vote of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>School Attendance Officers' National Association. ._..</head>
        <p>School Attendance Officers' National Associa- tion. ._..</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington and dis- trict branch of the above association was held in the Education Office, Sankey-street, Warrington, on Satur• day, Mr. Thomas Martindale (Earlestown) president- in the chair. There was a fair attendance of members, The question of continuing membership of tht Lanca- shire, Cheshire, and North Wales Federation XV3i dis. cussed, and it was eventually decided to bring the matter forward at the next meeting for further con- sideration.—A letter was read from Mr. William Rutter resigning the position of secretary and treasurer, but as it was the unanimous wish of the members present that he should continue in office until the next annual meeting he consented to do so. Mr. T. E. Clough (Earlestown), was elected a mem- ber of the Association.—A Vote of thanks to the Chair- man concluded the business.</p>
        <p>II</p>
        <p>• _ •. - , ' .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>INFLUENZA INCREASING NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the country. The visitation of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly on the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring it about is as much a mysterj as ever. You cannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than almost any other disease and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he has only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung Complaints must be ascribed to the baneful influence of Influenza, whose after-effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has never since failed to revive at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages, and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will, regain their former health. As a Preventativeove know of nothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in time have often proved effectual in warding off attacks of this Malady. and always succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes. Severe Head- ache, accompanied by difficulty in breathing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest, under and between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symptoms. know that they are the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Bitters. By common consent Patients every- where admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an attack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best, way to banish these miserable feelings is to take Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confidently recommend this Preparation, as it has treated severe cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds,Extreme Weakness, and Langour. with greater success than any other known remedy. We may also add that as a remedy for Indigestion,Sleeplessness,Lovv Spirits, Nervousness, and Loss of Appetite. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters stands unevalled. It is sold in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. Pd., by all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors, or sent direct, carriage free, for the above prices, by the Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters Nlanufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Avoid Imitations. See the name" Gwilym Evans" on Label Stamp and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SURPRISED THE M.AGISTRATF.S.—VOLUBLE PRISONER DISCHARGED. ThOMRS Troy, who u-as charged with drunkenness, suprised the Warrington Bench on Monday by deliver- ing a long and apparently carefully-rehearsed speech.— " Gentlemen of this court of jurisdiction," he commenced "there is no doubt that much of the suffering of this life is self-affficted."__" Give us reasons why you should not be sent to gaol " interrupted the Mayor sternly. —Troy was undaunted and went on "Apart from this there are poor helpless sufferers who, thr augh a combination of circumstances, are daily surrounded with misery." Next the prisoner invited the Bench. whom he addressed as "dear gentlemen "to take a walk in the back streets and slums of any large manufacturing town where they might cast their eyes on miserable,wretched surroundings. General Booth came in for a good word, Troy remarking that be "does more good for the poor than all the others who are a lot of utter imposters and won't do anything for yon."—Evidently impressed by the stream of eloquence the Chief Constable said that if prisoner would go away, and take his tongue with him, he would be glad if the Bench would discharge him.—Troy readily con- sented to take his departure and he was discharged. ALLEGED SAVAGE ASSAULT BY COLLIERS. Before Mr. F. A. Frost at Warrington Occasional Court on Monday, William Lee and Joseph Davies, colliers, Hazel-grove, Earlestown, were charged with assaulting and inflicting grievous bodily harm on John Prescott on January 2.l at 13,Hazel-grove, by kicking and striking him. Both pleaded not guiltv.—The evi- dence of the complainant was to the effect that at 3 a.m. on the day in question the prisoner Lee, who lodged at the same house, struck him violent blows on the nose, bursting it. He also struck him in the month,splitting both lips. Davies then attacked him, and struck him a violent blow over the left eve, causing a wound. Both prisoners then threw him on the floor, and kicked him severely, in consequence of which he had suffered great pain and was unable to work.—Dr. Valentine stated that he was summoned to attend Prescott. He found him suffering from shock and a lacerated and contused wound over the left eyebrow, and his face was covered with blood. There were bruises over the left side of the nose and face, and on the lower part of the spine, in the right loin he found a great amount of confusion and swelling. For the first twenty-four hours Prescott was in a critical con- dition. Severe direct violence would have been necessary to cause the injuries. —Both prisoners were ommitted to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions. CAUGHT IN THE ACT. • .</p>
        <p>Henry Billings, a notorious character, was charged at the Warrington Police Court on Saturday, with having heels found on the enclosed premises of Mr. Peter Leigh,grocer and wine and spirit merchant,Horsemarket- street, with intent to commit a felony. He pleady guilty.—Superintendent Monaghan stated that the shop was left secure at eight o'clock on Friday night, and as six o'clock that morning some workmen passing noticed the prisoner concealed behind the sign over the window which be was trying to open with some fitters' tools. Police-constables Dawson and Tolly were summoned, and the prisoner then slid down the spout, and was taken into custody. He said he was going to break into the shop as he wanted something to eat. He was a well-known character, having been sentenced to penal serviture for hones-breaking, and served several other terms of imprisonment for various thefts. He never worked.—He was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Befnre the Mayor (presiding), Aldermen Francomb, Erutchingon and Smethurst, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. H. Thornton, J. Geddes, S. Wright. L. NV. Reynolds, G. Pilling, IV. Roberts. and Edwin Woods.</p>
        <p>M A INTENANC F..—Stephen Collins, 4, Stoney-yard, was summoned to show cause why an order should not be made upon him to contribute to his child's mainten- ance in a Reformatory School.—He was ordered to con- tribute Is. a week. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>HER 68Th l APPEARANCE.--Jane Johnson, 5, Rodney; street, made her 68th appearance, Charged with obscene language in Rodney-street. onJanuary 26th. The Chief Constable said he hoped the magistrates would make an example of her. She was fined ss. and costs.—For a similar offence in Dannett-street on January 29th, John Rutter, 26, Dannett-street, was fined ss. and Costs. THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. John Fairclough (in the chair), J. W. Hayward, W. Roberts, andJ...R. Young.</p>
        <p>MAINTENANCE CASE.—James Shaw, 9, Howley -lane, was summoned to show cause why he should not con- tribute to the maintenance of his daughter in the deaf and dumb school, Preston—Mr. Stanley Wilson,deputy town clerk, asked for an order for payment of at least 58. per week.' The case was brought under the notice of the School Attendance Committee in 'Tilly and when the child was sent to the institution it was arranged that de- fendant should pay2s .6d.oe r week as a temporary measure. Defenpant had absolutely refused to pay even to pay even that low amount anp the actual cost to the rate- payers wos .£2.4 per annum. The w ages coming into the house amounted to £2 14s. per week. The Bench made an nrder for defendant to pay is. per week. ASSAULT.—Thomns Wright was charged with assault- ing his wife Jane \Wig' t on January 14th, and was bound over to Keep the peaee for six months.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Nf.ayor (presiding) and other</p>
        <p>magistrates.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND AND WlFE.—Thomas Rowlands was charged with assaulting nis wife Mary Rowlands on February 4th.—Defendant alleged that his wife commenced the quarrel, and the case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>An invalid child named Violet Victoria Ve'den, th daughter of a Camberwell working man, wrote a touch- ing little letter of sympathy to Princess Victoria, and received in answer some beautiful flowers and a charm- ingly kind joint note from the Queen and the Princess. I will pray for you," wrote the little girl. The Princess in reply Hopes and prays that God may soon make them both well again."</p>
