      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay.</head>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>The special services in connection with the opening of the new organ were continued in the above chapel on Sunday, Mr. Henry Bury, BA., (student from the Baptist College, Manchester, being the preacher morn- ing and evening. His remarks in the morning were addressed to the children (of whom there were a large number present) from the words "Is not this the carpenter's Son? " In the evening the chapel was crowded, the preacher delivering an eloquent sermon from the words " Whosoever drinketh of this water .shall thirst again," &amp;c. The choir, under the conductor- ship of Mr. S. Hassan. rendered an anthem in a praise- worthy manner, and a solo by Miss Dunn was very effective. Mr. F. Atkinson presided at the organ. At the close of the service an organ recital was given by Master Clatworthy, pupil of Mr. Podrnore, the various items being cleverly rendered, as was also a solo by Mr. J. Willett. The collections for the day amounted to £9 13s. G, this sum including a donation of 2Fis. from the Young Men's Class.</p>
        <p>In the Divorce Court, on Thursday,Mr. Justice Deane granted a decree nisi to Alfred George Roden,a Birm- ingham traveller, on the ground of his wife's miscon- duct with William Henry Cox, a picture dealer. Petitioner said that on one occasion he found Cox who is an old man hanging about his garden gate and he gave him a thrashing which laid him up for weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>1904.</head>
        <p>1904.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 11, 1905.</p>
        <p>also advised the overseers in questions of valuations, and his efforts to equalize the rating of the borough so that no ratep ger might pay an unfair share of the rates had been most praisworthy. He had done the work with great skill, and the results had been most satisfac- tory to them. But for the re-valuation of the borough four years ago, whieh was entirely his work, the rate at the present time would have been 41d. in the pound more. Re thought he need hardly remind them again that by the Corporation taking over the work of the overseers, they were saving about £7OO perannum. They would remember that Mr. Tunstall was compensated on the basis of a gross income from the rates of £873. The present charge for salaries was £332, a difference of £5ll. By a re-arrangement of bank charges, the use of joint books, and other matters, a further saving of £l5O was made. Alderman BENNETT seconded. He did not wish to be invidious, but he thought that if they had a faithful and devoted servant it was Ar. ‘Voodcock. (Hear, haar). The town had reap3d benefit from his financial ability. He had never seen books more accurately or more sys- tematically kept, and the rates were got in remarkably well • the amount outstanding being ridiculously</p>
        <p>Councillor P. PEACOCK said he felt compelled to oppose the payment of £25. He admitted that Mr. Woodcock was one of the nnst valuable servants of the Corporation but he objected to the automatic increase. He should rather have been prep tred to have proposed £5O without the annual increamt. He moved that the matter be referred back.</p>
        <p>Councillc r PENIIPERTON seconded, and also admitted that Mr. Woodcock WM one of the most valuable ser- vants the Corporation had. He did his work in a very conscientious manner, but he (the speaker) objected to the mode of settling salaries for thre:3 or four years to come.</p>
        <p>Councillor WRMIIT said that Mr. Woodcock might be a genius, but the question was one of principle so far as he (the speaker) was concerned. Mr. Woodcock was receiving 14s. per weak from various depart- ments. A good many of the workmen of the Corporation would be glad to handle such a sum. It would be a perfect "heaven on earth" for them. Councillor WILKINSON thought it would be better to delete the words "annual increments," thus doing away with the necessity of discussing the matter each time an official's salary came up for consideration. Councillor P. PEACOCK said he was prepared to with- draw his amendment if the words "annual increment " were deleted. Alderman PIERPOINT and Councillor JAMES EVANS also supported the suggestion. The latter said the officials of the Corporation seemed to think they had a right to come to the Council every year for an increase of salary. Councillor TINNION : They are invited by the stand- ing orders. (Hear, hear.) He contended that, con- sidering the present position of trade in the borough, it was inopportune to increase salaries over £3OO a year. (Hear, hear.) That remark applied to all the leading</p>
        <p>The amendment that the minute be not confirmed so far as the proposed annual increment was concerned was put, 12 voting for it and 12 against. Alderman PIERPOINT : 1 voted under a misappre- hension. I thought I was voting in favour of the £25 being granted. Councillor PEICOCK : That is so. The MANOR : I think the minutes had better stand as they are. I give my casting vote in favour of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor A. PEACOCK moved that the question of the salary be rescinded altogether. Councillor P. PEACOCK seconded, and said they might not be in office next year, and why should they do the work of their successors ?</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTOIsi supported. The rates were pinching many people in the town. The amendment that the resolution be not confirmed at all, was lost. The result is that the minutes of the committee xere carried in their entirety, and Mr. Woodcock's salary will go on increasing in accordance with the recommendation of the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL, SEVERAL SALARY INCREASES NOT CONFIRMED,</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL, SEVERAL SALARY INCREASES NOT CONFIRMED,</p>
        <p>Lively Discussions. The recommended increases of salaries to Corpora- tion officials gave rise to considerable discussion at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Alderman Bolton) presided an i there were also present :—The Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Pairclough), Aldermen A. Bennett, John Evans, It. W. Francomb, T. Hutchinson, W. Pierpoint, and J. Smethurst ; Coun- cillors T. H. Ashcroft, T. Barton, W.Benson, W. Brough, T. Burton, W. Clegg, J. G. Craik, James Evans, L. Ellison, T. J. Hulse, J. Lord, J. Magian, A. Peacock, P. Peacock, J. Pemberton, J. Roberts,J.Sankey.J.Skelland F. Stott, D. Tinnion, J. Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson,and T. Wright ; Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), S. Wilson (deputy town cleric), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. Dens, (water engineer), C. Madeley (librarian),W.S.Haddock, (gas engineer) F. V. L. Matthias (electrical engineer), J. G. Gornall (medical officer), and J. M. Murray (director of education.)._ _</p>
        <p>THE LATE :MR. H. MILLING. The 111a.Yort said he had a sad announcement to make and that was that Mr. Milling, a former member of the !Council, had died since the last meeting. He moved: "That this Council receives with regret the announce- ment of the death of Mr. Henry Milling, formerly a ! member of this Council, which took place on the 23rd ! January, and requests the Town Clerk to convey to the widow and family of the late Mr. Milling this expres- sion of the condolence of the Council with them in their loss." His Worship said Mr. Milling served in the i Council from 1876 to 1879. It was perhaps unfortunate that he did not rem-tin in the Council longer as they ; needed men of his stamp. He was one of their most I respected citizens, and the town could ill afford to lose such a man. (Hear, hear.) Alderman Piefteom seconded, and the motion was agreed to. THE SA.LARIES QUESTION. !EDUCATION OFFCIALS AND THE MAXIMUM. Alderman BENNETT moved that the recommendations of the Education Committee, requiring the confirmation of the Council, beagreed to, and alluded to the proposed increase of salaries of three officials of the Education pommittee, namely Messrs. R. B. Roberts, assistant director, present salary £175, £lO per annum to a maximum of .£200; A. S. Elliot, stores clerk, present salary £125, £5 per annum to a maximum of £130; and E. Haigh, secondary clerk, present s Lry £7B, 2s. 6d. per week to a maximum of £lO9. A slight increase had been granted in each case, and the additions were in accordance with the scale.</p>
        <p>Councillor g LL-E-S-ON--se-c-o.nded. Councillor P. PEACOCK moved an amendment to the effect that the proposal so far as the annual increment was concerned be not agreed to. Last year the Council agreed thit there should ba no antom ',tic advances, and they ought not to allow the Education Committee to commence them. CollaCillOr PEMBERTON seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNE rr expressed surprise at the objection to the proposed increases, which had been well earned. More-over, the salaries were extremely moderate. The increases were in acc-rdance with the Council's practice of rewarding faithful and valuable service by some con- sideration.</p>
        <p>Councillor MA GINS remarked that he had nothing against the officials of the Education Committee nor against the officials of the Corporation, but the increase in salaries the Corporation had to pay this year to the Education Department was £1,027. They knew the state of trade in the town. The education rate last year was lid., and if they had the 'advances proposed, there would be an increase of 1 fd. He would like every official and teacher to be pail as much as they could afford to pay them, but as b usiness men they could only act as tradesmen wouldact. They should not pay more than they could afford. (Hear, hear.) Many children had to go to school without breakfast and some with part of a breakfast, and it was hard to think that such a state of things existed. The payments suggested might be according to the agreement, and they might be forced to pay them, but if they were it was a stupid arrange- ment. There seemed to be no end of increases to salaries by the Education Committee. If increases like this went on they vSould d epopulate Ithe borough. He appealed to the teachers and officials to allow this year to pass, and apply for an increase at some more favour- able time.</p>
        <p>The MA Yort said Councillor Magian had been out of order. because the scale of to tellers' salaries was passed at a previous meeting of the CourKul and they Nv,3re therefore committed to it.</p>
        <p>Councillor WRIGHT said that as a workingman he thought the system of increments was entirely wrong. It was wrong to be continually addin,,6 to what was already big, forgetting all the time about the poor men who were at the bottom of the ladler. More considera- tion should be given to the ill-paid servants of the Corporation, such as the dustmen and scavengers, who did ft very necessary work. . .</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD opposed annual incremnts on prin- ciple. He had been informed that there was likely to be a deficit of .£2,712 on the next year's estimate for the Education Committee. That was equal to a three- penny rate. Alderman BENNETT said that during all the years in which he had been a member of the Council one thing had struck him very forcibly, and that was that the one night when they all waxed eloquent and reached the height of virtuous indignation was when small increases of salary were proposed, while when they were chucking thousands about the matter was regarded with quies- cence. (Hear, hear.) These were all slight increases of salaries and were extremely well deserved. He was, however, prepared to withdraw the question of the maximum if the Council would agree to the first year's increase. Probably the Education Committee would agree to that (Hear, hear.) The MAYOR: I don't see how they can object. Alderman KENNETT: I know that the financial power is in our hands, but we want to act in harmony with the recommendations of the Education Co.nmittee.</p>
        <p>The increases were then granted on the understanding that no annual increment to a.maximuni. was involved.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT aakecl the Council not to be led away by Councillor Lord's alarmist report. He did bot know where he had got his information from to lead him to that conclusion. Councillor PEMBERTON: What will it be ? Alderman BENNETT : I am not in a nosition to tell you at present—Ca laugh)—but I don't think it will be as much a i was named by Councillor Lord. Don't run away with a cock-and-bull-story until you know the facts. The minutes were then confirmed on the understanding mentior ed.</p>
        <p>CORPORATION OFFCIALS AND THEIR SALARIES.</p>
        <p>EMEIT APPLICATIONS FOR AN INCREASE.</p>
        <p>i'iik-dontidit:lslli&amp; ECONONITCAL MOOD-.</p>
        <p>Various increases of salaries were recommended by different committees, and discussed as the minutes of those committees came up for consideration. We have, however, clubbed the lot together in order to keep the salaries question under one heading. The following is the list of the increases proposed. Finance 'and Estate Committee.—On the recommenda- tion of the Overseers that the sahry of Mr, W. H. Woodcock be increased by annual increments of £25 from £lOO to £2OO. As borough collector and assistant treasurer, Mr. Woodcock's present salary is £350. Health Committee.—That the salary of the Matron (Miss Lees) be increased from EGO to £7O per annum, and that an honorarium of £lO be voted to her for services renderer! during the outbreak of smallpox. Market Committees.—Mr. W. H. Wallington from £l2O to 2130 per annum. Rivers Committee.—lr. Schofield Jackson, river sur- veyor, from £lOO to £l2O per annum. Water Committee.—Mr..lames Gray,assistant engineer, from £2 to £2 10i. nor week: T. Lonashaw, clerk, from 30s. to 335. per week: E. P. 'Fuson, clerk. from £1 to £l3s. per week; C. Barlow, clerk, from 18s. to 21s. per week; the office boy, from 6s. to Bs. per week; and Harry Wardrasn, foremm pipelayer, 30s. to 335. per week.</p>
        <p>Electricity ani Tramways Committee.--11r. F. V. L. Matthias, electrical engineer, from £250 to £3OO per annum ; Mr. F. NV. Purse, the assistant electrical engineer, from £156 to £169 per curium; .vr. Tolfer, tramways manager, from £l2O to £l4O per annum; and Mr. NV. E. Rogers, mains engineer, from £l3O to £143 per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MR. W. It. WOODCOCK.</head>
        <p>MR. W. It. WOODCOCK.</p>
        <p>In submitted for confirmation the minutes of the Finance and Estate Committee, which proposed an increase of salary to Mr. Woodcock. The DEPUTY-IVIkYOR said that with reference to the salary of Mr. Woodcock, the assistant-overseer, he might say that the overseers had gone fully into the question of his duties, and the way in which he per- formed them, and they unanimously recommended that his salary be irfcreased in accordance with the resolu- intim]. The work he was doing was of a very important character, and every department was very well done. His work included the preparation of the voters' list, which everyone knew since it had been in his charge had been exceedingly well done. The result had been to save trouble and expense all round. The ratepayers in particular were saved much annoyance and loss of time, and he had been annually complimented On the results by both the representatives of the political parties and the Revising Barrister. The cost, he might also say. was very much less than it used to be. The collection of the rates was also very important work- needing great care and discretion, and the overseers had nothing but praise for the way in which he collected the large ennui which daily passed through his hands .He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Great News</head>
        <p>Great News</p>
