        <p>Half the labour of washing is saved by Fels-Naptha; wash-day is shorter and clothes last longer.</p>
        <p>Fels-Nap:ha 39 Wiison street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NIISS LEES.</head>
        <p>NIISS LEES.</p>
        <p>In proposing the increased salary and bonus to Nliss Lees, hospital matron, Councillor EVANS said that in her they had a matron of exceptional ability. On the very day she came they had the snoall pox outbreak, and as they knew, there was a very considerable number of cases. That, of course, caused much extra work, and the Health Committee therefore decided to grant the bonus of £lO. They had also decided to increase her salary, and he thought she thoroughly deserved it. Councillor WILKINSON seconded the proposition. Councillor NINGINN moved an amendment. He said he had no doubt that all Councillor Evans had said regarding Miss Lees was just and right, but he thought it was adding sugar to sugar to give her a bonus and an increase of salary. There were others in the hospital who worked vary hard during the outbreak, and who, on the same gronnds, were etititled to a present. The outbreak was a misfortune to the town and not a thing they made profit out of. If the committee would withdraw the bonus he would agree to the increase of salary. Councillor WRIGHT seconded the amendment. Councillor TtxNrox said Miss Lees rendered more than the services she WAS engaged to perform and was entitled to what the committee were giving. Councillor STOTT said it was rather unfortunate that the bonus and the increase of salary should come up at the same time. Recently, however, they agreed to pay other officials for extra work on the occasion of the Trading Inquiry and he thought they could not object to this grant for extra service; when they had established that principle. CollaCillOr WILKINSON observed that Miss Lees applied for an increase earlier on, but the committee advised her to leave it for a time and she did so. Although the Aikin-streez—hospital was only half the size in the time of the late matron, Miss Lees would not be getting the salary they were then paving even if she got her increase. She was justly entitled to what the com- mittee were giving her. . . . .</p>
        <p>The amendment wa,s then put awl lost and the increase and bonus were s.Lnotioned.</p>
        <p>MR. W. 11. WA.LLJNGTON.</p>
        <p>In proposing the adoption Of the 'minutes of the :Markers Committee. which included a recommendation that the salary of Mr. W. T. Wallington, market inspector, he increased from £l2O to £13011; Alderman HUTC Fr [NsoN pointed out that the official had held his position for eleven years, and was deserving of a slight increase. The recommendation was alonted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MR. SCHOFI ELI) JACKSON.</head>
        <p>MR. SCHOFI ELI) JACKSON.</p>
        <p>The MlYort moved the n.dontion of the minutes of the Rivers Committee. which contained a resolution that the salary of Mr. Schofield Jackson, river sm.- veyer, be increased from £lOO to £l2O per annum. He stated that Mr. Jackson had received no increase during the five years he had bym in the employ of the Council and devoted the whole of his time to the work. Aldorrnan FIIRCLOUGH se3onded the minutes, which were adopted. AIR. GRAY AND OTHERS. In moving the minutes of he Water Committee, 4lderman FRANCOMB referred to he proposed increases in the salaries of Messrs. James Gray. T. Longshaw, E. P. Tuson, and C. Barlow. and Henry Wardman. If there was a hard working official it was Mr. Gray, and he Coroughly deserved an increlse. The others were being very badly paid considering their ages, and it WAS proposed to give them an extra 3s. a week. Alderman EVANS seconded, and the minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>MESSRS. 111TTHI TETYER, PUrtiE, IND RIG-1;:ti.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. 111TTHI TETYER, PUrtiE, IND RIG-1;:ti.</p>
        <p>Alderman SVErfruftsT moved the confirmation of the rai4tes 4 the Electricity and Tramways Committee which contained the recommendations with reference to the salaries of 11,.. F. V. L. Matthias. electrical en.gineer, Mr. F. W. Parse, the assistant-electrical engineer, "Mr. J. Teller, tramways manager, and Mr. W. E. Rogers, mains engineer. The speaker said that he would much have preferred leaving the questions of increases aloue until the annual balance sheet waS brought out at the end of tile quarter. (Hear, hear.) As that could not be done, however, he was compelled Ito bring them up now. When the Council took over ! these works twelve months ago the committee were in an awkward position, and a great many things wanted putting on wh)t, the co-nmittee considereda business-like footing.. They had had a great deal to do, and he had asked the Council at that time for a grant for certain works and promised them that they would make a profit and that the works would pay for themselves in two years. The previous financial year of the electrical works saw the committee with a profit of £250. The Council had been kind enough to grant the committee what they wanted, and the committee made the best use they could of that assistance. He did not think that the Council would grumble when they had got a servant canable of bringing this profit of £250 up to £1,500 or £2,000 in a year's time. (Hear, hear). He did not want to make things appear too rosy but he thought that there was not a gentleman present who would not be willing to give an extra £5O a year, to a manager who was responsible for so large an in- crease of profit in such a time and way ag the electri- cal engineer had been. With reference to the sa!ary of Mr. Teller the latter was not satisfied with it, and he did not care whether he got the increase or not. Councillor NV/tic:Jur : Don't give it him then Alderman SMETHURST (continuing) said the same remarks applied to Mr. Telfer as to Mr. Mathias. There had been a great deal of alteration and a great saving in working expenses, and they must not expect to get much from the tramways. The committee had worked together very comfortably with.the officials and as the works were making much more this year than before the officials were worthy of the suggested increases. -</p>
        <p>Councillor IVELIctssoN seconded the passing of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor NIAGINN said he was sorry that he could not agree with Alderman S nethurst over the matter of raising of salaries. The princinle was wrong, in his opinion, lie maintained that it was wrong for committees to propose increases of salaries before the annnal balance sheet was produ3ed. Alderman Smethurst had given the prospects as very rosy, and the speaker hoped he was right. As far as the tramways were concerned, however, he was wrong. It wasan open secret that a great portion of the present. tramways needed relaying. This matter would have to come on next .year, so it was only deferring the black day. It had been stated that they would not have a loss on this work because thetrarnways were constantly being repaired. If the tramways had to be put in proper order, every tram in Warrington would have to be</p>
        <p>stopped. More conscientious and industrious servants than the gentlemen named for increases there could not be, but he objected on principle. A standing order which allowed proposals for the increase of salaries by a committee who had charge of a profit and loss account before the end of the financial year should be altered. (Hear, hear.) He would not move an amendment, but would enter his protest against the present standing order.</p>
        <p>Councillor PETER PEACOCK pointed out that Mr. Mathias commenced at a salary of £2 per week and in four years it was being raised to £6, a rate of increase which he considered too much. He proposed that the minute with regard to Mr. Mathias be referred back. Councillor WRIGHT seconded. He thought that Mr. Mathias and others had already had good advances, and there was the possibility of the system not paying to be considered. He was opposed to the increasing of salaries until something was done for the ill-paid work- ing man. -Councillor A. PEACOCK said the Coun,il bad given Mr. Mathias a post which would enable him to obtain a better position elsewhere, as well as the salary attached to his office. Four or five of the members of the com- mittee even did not agree to the advance. CouncillorEvass remarked that when the tram drivers and guards applied to be paid for time and a quarter on Sundays they were refused on the ground that the Com- mittee could not afford it. If the Committee could not afford to pay this to the most ill paid and hard worked men it would be both inopportune and improper to in- crease the big salaries as suggested. The Maitort said that when the last manager of the electrical department left'ani Mathias was appointed to the position at a salary of £250 it was understood that an increase would be forthcoming at the first oppor- tunity.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION supported the view of the Mayor. The engineer could easily get a position similar to this one at a higher salary somewhere else. The price of electricity per unit had been reduced during his manr.ige- meat. There was a very considerable reduction in a short time.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON did not remember any promise being made to Mr. Mathias as had been suggested. They had not only raised his salary materially already, but had placed him in a higher position in the labour market. The Council were entitled to a fair return for salary paid and could reasonably expect the best energy and brain power of their employees being untilized in their service. Then, all tha saving was not due to the engineer. They were now using slack at Be. instead of coal at 123. and he ilaimed some credit for that himself. (Laughter). The Chairman, in his rosy outlook, had made no reference to depreciation of plant. The amount of paving which would be required during the next two or three years would cost thousands of pounds. Councillor WILKINsom said it was pleasant to listen to gentlemen who had a tremendous idea of their own abilities. When Mr. Mathias was appointed Mr. G rims- dale was being paid £4OO a year. If the Council granted the advances to Messrs. 'Mathias and Teller the electrical and tram widths would still be run at a cost of £l6O less than they were two years ago. Councillor CRAIK was of opinion that when Mr. Mathias was appointed as Mr. Grimsdale's successor it was understood that the salary he commenced at would be increased.</p>
        <p>Councillor BARTON said they appeared to be losing sight of the fact that they were there as representatives of the ratepayers and should be careful about Increas- ing the expenditure of the town. A vote was then taken and the amendment was low; by 15 votes to 13 votes. Councillor STOTT then moved that the increase be £25 instead of £5O.</p>
        <p>The MA.YOR : You should send the minute back to the committee to alter it.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEACOCK moved that the minute with reference tolgr. Telfer's salary, be referred back. Councillor EVANS seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said that Mr. Telfer was appointed on the distinct understanding that his salary was to be £l2O, with a yearly rise of £lO. He was then perfectly satisfied and anxious to take the work in hand. The tramways undertaking should be in a better position before salaries were advanced. Councillor CaAn( said it was wrong for the com- mittee to bring this matter forward considering that Mr. Telfer had not been in Warrington a year yet. His was one out of 90 applications received for the post and he knew exactly what his position would be. He gave the committee itself credit for mnch of the economyFhich bad been exercised.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON said they had the distinction of paying the tramways manager the smallest salary in the country. _ _</p>
        <p>Ona vote being taken the amendment was carried by 15 votes to 13, and the ificrease thus refused. _</p>
        <p>The minute i with reference to the .;;laire; oi Messrs, Purse and Rogers were also referred back for father con- sideration, on the motion of Councillor PEACOCK, seconded by Councillor EVANS. A PHOTOGRAFIIIC RECORD OF OLD</p>
        <p>- BUILDINGS. In moving the minutes of the Museum Committee Alderman BENsErr siaid the Committee had armnhed to have photographs taken of old buildings which were being from time to time demolished. Only this week the old Warrington Blue Coat School behind Trinity Church was being pulled down. The minutes were confirmed. THE WATER WORKS.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN FifiTNDEliiii-"Arisigiti QUESTIONS. Councillor LORD asked the Chaldean of the Water ICommittee: (1) What was the amount of Lawson's contract, not taking into account any charges for extras? (2) What is the amount of Lawson's claim up-to-date, including extras?. (3) What is the amount already certified by the Water Engineer for work done? (4) What is the amount paid on account of same.? Alderman FRANCOMB replied as follows :(1,) £24,400 ; (2,)The contractor has sent in an account for work done amounting to £43,337 19s. 10d. The work is paid for according to a schedule of prices, and thew are no extras outside the schedule ;(3,)£37,640 ; (4), £33,684. Councillor PEMBERTON asked the Chairman: (1) Has he given instruction for any action to be taken against Mr. Lawson' the contractor for the work at Dolph- lane? (2) Has he received notice from Mr. Lawson that he intends to proceed against the Corporation? Alderman FRANCOMB replied : (1) I have no power to give any sath instructions. It an action has to be taken the 'm Itter will have to be decided by the com- mittee. (2) No. Ido not know but what 3fr. Lawson and the committee are on the best terms.</p>
        <p>Cosaacillor PEMBERTON : " dear,hear." MORE COVERED CARS.</p>
        <p>In proposin..g--th-e—MVin.ta—;-7,F the Electricity and Tramways Committee, Alderman SMETHURST said he expected the'new:cover-tops for four more tramcars this week. The tenders were again let at ,£BO for each cover- top. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>NO OVERCOATS FOR CENIECERZ; WORKAIEN</p>
        <p>Councillor WfcgoY moved :—"That the workmen employed st the Warringten Cemetery be supplied with overcoats."</p>
        <p>Councillor P. PEAC3OK seconded. This, he said, was another case in which a member of the Council who was not on that wirticular committee had been ignored and told he knew nothing about other people's committees. He understood Councillor Wilson had asked several times for the men to be atteaded to, and he had been ignored. Councillor Srarr said that when the matter was first brought before the committee, the discussion indicated that almost the whole of the members were in favour of overcoats being provided, but they desired the subject to be left over until autumn.</p>
        <p>Councillor CRAIK said that if the workmen themselves had not made application for overcoats be did not see why outsiders should play to the gallery by asking the council to provide them. Alderman FIYANs, in reply to a statement by Councillor WILSON. emphatically denied that any pro- mise had been made to provide the coats. On a vote being taken the motion was I sit, only two aoting for it. ADVERTISIN G WARRINGTON. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON moved : "That a committee of the Council be appointed with instructions to make known to Compani.s and others intending to build new works the suitability of Warrington as an industrial cent re."</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: We want Warrington to be known as a place where you can bring works and find employment for unemployed men. CO:11101110r SToTT : Can Vve spa(' money for the purpose? The MAYOR: We don't need to spqnd money yet. You are, only appointing a committee to consider ways and means.</p>
        <p>The resolution was uninimousl v agreed to. THE SALARY INCREASES.</p>
        <p>ALTERATION OF THE STANDIN6ORDER.</p>
        <p>Comic'llOr PEMBERTON moved "thai-n§t—anTlivn—g-o:der No. 39 providing that all applications for increases of salaries over £2 per week shall he considered at the February meeting of the Council be rescinded." He said it seemed to be the almost unanimous wish of the Council that such a resolution should be brought for- ward. Everyone knew how unfairly and awkwardly the present standing order worked out. Councillor A. PEACOCK seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION said he considered the amend- ment a wise one. He had always thought the present rule a sort of gratuitous invitation to the salaried officials to apply for increases once a year. The motion was agreed to. Councillor PEMBERTON moved : " That Standing Order No. 40 be amended to read as follows :—Every recommendation or report of a committee for an indite:lse of salaries shall be accompanied by a tabulated return, showing the length of service of each officer or servant receiving more than £2 per week in the department referred to in the recommendation or report." The TOWN CLERK said the meaning was that every application for an increase of salary must be accom- panied by a tabulated return showing the length of service and salary paid. The MAYOR explained that the old standing order that increases could only be considered at the February meeting was drawn up because heavy increases such, for instance, as those recommended by the Education Committee might necessitate the levying of a rate. Under the new arrangement they might find that increases had not been provided for in the estimates.</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried and the Council rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>LALL RIGHTS RESEIITED.3 THE ROMANCE. OF LAND MOVEMENTS,</head>
        <p>LALL RIGHTS RESEIITED.3 THE ROMANCE. OF LAND MOVEMENTS,</p>
        <p>In DR. ,ANDREW WILSON (Author " Science Storiec," dc.).</p>
        <p>The recent disasters in the West Indies have naturally excited widespread interest amongst geologists as well as iunon °O-st the general public. Scientifically speaking, both volcanoes and earthquakes rank themselves as part of a series of phenomena to which the term. "movements of the earth's crust" may be applied ; for it is an undoubted fact • that both actions are intimately associated with disturbances of that portion of the earth Which we are accustomed to term the habitable globe. Naturally also, earthquakes and volcanoes are closely related for the reason that both take their origin from the internal heat of the earth 'which, of course, is a remnant of the universal heat in which our planet .was born. There remains however, a certain series of phenomena to which the term sldw movements of the earths crust may be applied. These are sufficiently interesting to warrant a brief descliption of certain of the more prominent examples of such actions.</p>
        <p>Disco -Bay. The proof's of this sunmergence have been found in the fact that old building constructed on low island' have been submerged and the Greenlanders take care to build their huts some distance from the coast line. 'Another interesting proof of this subsidence in Green- land is afforded by • th'e fact that some of the earlier Moravian missionaries fixed strong poles in the sea beach close to the doors of their huts for the purpose of mooring their boats. To- day these poles are practically seen still to hold their places, but are far out at sea, the land meanwhile. having sunk and the sea flowed in upon it. The Italian coast, however, probably furnishes us with more numerous and more distinct exampes of these slow movements of land within a comparatively limited area, than possibly any other part of the earth's surface. There, as indeed are also found elsewhere in the world, marks of sea tett-ion on cliffs are met with high above the present , level of the sea. Also there are foetal in such situations the harscars' in the rock-. -mids le; shell fish whose the Ponza Islands. Ile de-citifies l'almarola as being then divided into two parts by a narrow canal or space of sea, which he states was of the dimensions sneh us w nhl Al= ñ barge to pass through it. This writer also constructed a map of these islands. lie shows the canal clearly enough. It lies in the position of the semi-circular depression already alluded to, this depression as we have seen being part of the island itself, and now above bight water mark. It is perfectly clear then that slow elevation of Palmarola must have taken place within rela- tively recent times. The extent of land rise represented in this locality is stuted by modem authorities to equal about 200 feet.</p>
        <p>.Next Week:</p>
        <p>"THE SERVICE OF WoMEN." BY H.M. THE</p>
        <p>QtEEN OF ROAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SLOW CHANGES OF THE EARTH'S SURFACE.</head>
        <p>THE SLOW CHANGES OF THE EARTH'S SURFACE.</p>
        <p>It their occureuce may be described as of much less obstrusive nature than is the earthquake or the volcano, their reality is nevertheless quite as strongly marked. Practically the real dif- ference between the earthquake awl the slow movements of the earth's crust is one not of kind but simply degree. An earthquake, in other words, will effect in a moment changes of . the earth's crust which, may require very lengthened periods of time bli the part of the slow movements to bring about. Nevertheless it is obvious that the three phenomena them- : saves, all indicate to the geological mind the great fact that the apparently stable crust of the earth is really in a conditien of more or less constant alteration and change. In some localities subsidence is the prevailing feature, whilst in others elevation of the strata is noted to take place. One rely important preliminary consideration may herebe noted. Where mans observation has long enough existed to con- vince him of apparent alteration in the relative levels of land and sea, a question might arise j regarding the exact seat of the phenomena he 1 has noted. Popularly spealdng, it would appear at first sight very much more reasonable to assert that either a retirement or an invasion of the ea would account for-11 e alteration per- ceived say on coast lands. A dttle reflection, however, will convince - us that the ocean may be considered in the Aunin to be it non-altering quantity. A simple conlidcration will easily convince us of the truth of this remark. The sea practically remains on the same level. There.' fore when an alteration is found to take Owe on a sea coast. unless .we are prepared to prove that on the opposite shore a similar alteoation has taken place, and to the same extent, we must set the burden of the Change on the shoulders of the land and not on those of the (Veal!. 'Suppose tor example what represents an actual event, namely, the case of submerged forest, common. enough round many of our own coasts, and also on those of other .countries. Here extending below low water mark we find the stumps of trees. It is obvious that these . must have grown on what was once dry land. If i.e assume that the forest was in reality sub- J merged by the ocean, this would necessitate our supposing that a rise of it may be 20 or 30 f of sea took place, this 'rise implying not merely a tidal wave but a pertuanent alteration in :lie sea level. Suppose that such au i event took p:ace, say on the north 'coast of the English Channel, then if the alteration were due to the sea's rise we ought to be able to show that a similar rise had taken place on the French side, seeing that water must always maintain its own level.</p>
        <p>THE EARTH CHANGES, NOT THE SEA.</p>
        <p>Apart from the impossibility of thus amount- ing for change in the apparent 'evil of land and sea, we have to reckon upon the immense additional body of water which would be re- quired to effect the change in question. There is not a shadow of evidence to lead us to suppose what any such material change in the bulk of our oceans is at all possible. Therefore we are left with , the reasonable theory that the change represented in the submerged forest has been due to a partial and local subsidence a a part of the coast, carrying the old forest gradually below the surface of the waves, an action con- tinued slowly and without much disturbance of the trunks of the trees themselves.</p>
        <p>Assuming that the land is the seat of these slow movements and not the sea, we may proceed now to note certain interesting examples of such actions. Curiously enough we find Celsius; the great Swedish naturalist, giving his opinion at the beginning of the eighteenth century that the Baltic Ocean and North, Sea together were gradually subsiding. He further insisted that as far as regards Sweden the rate of the depression of the sea amounted to about forty inches in a hundred years. Re- ferring to proofs in support of his contention, he added that rocks on the shores of the Baltic once existing in the state of sunken reefs and dangerous to navigators, had gradually ap- peared above the surface of the sea. Also it was known that certain towns which in former years had been seaports were Snow found islands also having been :joined to the mainland and fishing grounds rendered in this way Use- less. No doubt the observations of Celsius were correct as regards details, only he erred in crediting the sea as the seat of the changes in question. If to day we consult a map on which the coasts said to be rising and sinking in the world are figured forth, we discover that while the western coast of Norway and also the Swedish coast in the Gulf of Bothnia may be said to exhibit evidences of the rising mentioned by this author, the most southerly part of Sweden is actually proved to be at the present time sub- siding. It therefore must be clear that the changes in questiOn must be credited to the land and not to the sea, seeing that in coasts bordered by the same body of water we have the double movement represented. We find an example of a coasts which has been elevated in the case of Chili, this elevation following rapidly on earthguake action itself. The southern coast of Ehgland,4 as well as that of Ireland, and the north coast of France, are in a state of depres- sion. Tracing the North African coast, for example, we find evidence of rising extending from Gibraltar to Tripoli, but from the latter point onwards to the Red sea we have evidence of sinking. The south coast of Greenland is also an'area which is marked by depression.</p>
        <p>THE SEE-SAW OE CONTINENTS. Of this latter phenomena we find 'several in- teresting proofs. Danish investigators many years ago showed clearly that part of the west coast of Greenland extending for a length or six hundred miles from north to south, was gradually sinking. The evidence indeed points to the fact that for four huudrad years at least, and probably for a far longer period, the coast of Greenland haa been Ainkifie from lealiko to</p>
      </div>
