        <p>t 3(i.alker Opened the second half, and now the Warring- trans had the wind in their favour they soon made ofelr Presence felt in the Runcorn quarters. The loss a Man from the paok did not seem to hamper them ivatton saved splendidly a forward rush led by Boardman and then a series of penalties were succeeded, Fish taking a pass from Isherwood, and,tricking Harrison he tainted over Houghton's head. A desparate race was Witnessed, but to the surprise of all Flanagan had 1-4; ced across, and whilst Fish was in the act of ground- the ball was kicked from his grasp and Runcorn "Ills still held the lead. The home men rallied well, and smart forward play,in which Walker and Mesdltinger `lere always in the van, caused Hallam trouble. Clever eraearances were witnessed, and on one occasion Brooks t eed up, and flooring Houghton the game again took a jWarrington's favour. Hereabouts cross kicking Si(3n a 8 witnessed, and Fish, beating Harrison for posses- tricked Houghton and scored a cspital try, land- ing a goal from a somewhat difficult position. The Ii sure was maintained,and only the superb defence of ,cl7iughtoo saved the locals. The Warrington forwards it.o,eeted several scrimmages' but in each case daring es were effected. In one of these rushes.Jolley was An i and was temporarily knocked out, lint after th'uical attention he was able to resume. Meanwhile the Runcorn backs headed by Walker nearly broke ll),ugh. Give-and-take play was witnessed, and the •nl(,p4ases of the match were fought out at midfield, end arrived with Warrington winners by one oae try (five points) to Runcorn, one try (three P°ints.)</p>
