      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WORKHOUSE BOILER.</head>
        <p>THE WORKHOUSE BOILER.</p>
        <p>The Clerk rePOl:iecft-h;i'iiie-C-Oriiiniitre-i-s- appuinted to I consider the provision of a new boiler had now received the analyst's certificate of the quality of the water used at the House. It did not, however, give all the infor- mation they required, and the analyst had been asked to give a further report. -Dr. J. Carter, in his report, stated that the water was a first-class sample of great purity. It was rather hard from lime and magnesia salts. It was fit for domestic use. As to its fitness for boiler purposes he could not answer the question without making a special mineral analysis, the cost of which would be .£1 Is. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward reported payments in respect of out- relief :—To Mr. Cook, £80; Mr. Riley, £2l; Mr. de Prez, £33. The total payments for the day were £ll3 4s. At the last meeting there was a balance of £2,526165. id. so that without adding any receipts they had a balance of £2,383 2s. id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>A. GRANT TO THE MATRON.</head>
        <p>A. GRANT TO THE MATRON.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Visiting Committee it was decided that £2 14s. 10d. be granted to the matron for extra services rendered whilst the position of cook was vacant. The grant represented payment for forty days at the rate of £25 per annum. The recommendation was approved. THE INMATES ENTERTAINED. VOTE Or THANKS TO THE LADIES.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved that a vote of thanks be accord- ed to the Ladies' Committee for the treat and enter- tainment given at the house on Wednesday, January 18th. The treat he said, ,vas a lavish one and the ladies seemed as if they could not do too much to make the inmates happy. Mr. Spruce seconded and expressed regret that more members of the Board did not attend to partake of the ladies ungrudging hospitality. Mr. Morris supported and the vote was heartily passed. TO BRIGHTEN WORKHOUSE LIFE. ADMIRABLE SUGGESTION...</p>
        <p>----- Miss Bessie Dutton, of Moore, president of the Ladies' Visiting Committee, wrote to the Chairman on behalf of that committee asking the Board to grant permission to a few young ladies whom they would nominate and whose names would be submitted for the Board's approval, to teach knitting, fancy and technical work to inmates of the house not already engaged and willing to learn. This had been found most useful and was a movement which had proved an immense boon in almost all the unions of our country in as much as it greqtiv varied the monotony of Workhouse life and assistedmany(especially perhaps men)now leading listless and very dull lives to pleasantly and profitably occupy long dreary hours. If the Board would kindly encourage the Committee by giving permission, the approved ladies would visit once a week or fortnight or any day convenient to the matron and officials at a fixed hour, punctually adhering to the time appointed for the com- mencement and conclusion of their work. Thgy would find material for the fancy or technical work, and when some of it was finished, say in a year's time, they would dispose of it for money which would be used for the benefit of entertainments for the inmates. The Chairman said the House Committee bad decided unanimously that the permission should be given, perhaps temporarily to begin with, till they saw how I the scheme worked. It was very kind of the ladies to entertain such feelings towards the inmates and, on condition that they worked in conjunction with the master and matron and made arrangements to snit them in every way, the Board might grant the request. Mr. Wright moved and Mr. Spruce .seconded that permission be granted. The Board unanimously expressed their approval. THE SERVICES AT THE HOUSE. _ _ "</p>
        <p>In reply to Mr. Wright, the master said the new Workhouse chaplain had not yet commenced his duties and no provision had been made for the holding of the usual Sunday morning service at the house. The new chaplain would enter upon his work at Little Leigh in March. Mr. Wright moved that in the meantime arrangements should he made for some clergyman to conduct the services. Mr. Morris seconded. The resolution was agreed to and it was decided that the clergyman undertaking the work should be remuner- ated at the rate paid to the chaplain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TRANSFER REFUSED. Interesting Licensing Point at Warrington.</head>
        <p>A TRANSFER REFUSED. Interesting Licensing Point at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington transfer sessions, on Monday, Mr. IH. J. Longton, solicitor, applied on behalf of Adolphus Sheffield for the transfer of an oft wine and spirit licence from Edward Melia,of the firm of Daniel Melia und Co., Horsemarket-street. He explained that Mr. Melia was ceasing his active connection with the business and Mr. Sheffield made the application as secretary of the com- pany and a large shareholder. The course had been approved by a resolution of the company. In answer to Mr. Henry Roberts, Mr. Longton said Mr. Sheffield would not reside on the premises. Mr. Melia, who previously held the licence, did not live in Warrington. Mr. Roberts: The greatest safP7lard we have in granting a licence is that the holder shall reside on the premises. Mr. Longton: Do you think a man in the shop, a servant paid at so Tench per week or month, would be more responsible than a large shareholder in the com- pany—a gentleman oftmeans and position? Mr. Roberts: We have not to decide that question. We have to decide whether an applicant who resides a considerable distance away is the proper person to have the licence. Mr. Melia was called and said he lived in Manchester. Mr. Sheffield was a shareholder and held about thirty or forty licences in different parts of the country on behalf of the company. Since the changes in the concern he had applied for many transfers and they had all been granted. The Chief Constable said the objection mentioned by Mr. Roberts was the only one that could be urged, as far as he (Mr. Talbot) was concerned. Mr. Sheffield held sixteen or seventeen licences elsewhere, but of course that was no argument in that Court. Mr. Sheffield gave evidence in support of the applica- tion. Mr. Roberta: I take it your duties in regard to the licence are carried out by a subordinate who simply acts on your instructions. Mr. Sheffield replied in the affirmative. Mr. Roberts: You yourself hate no control what- ever other than what is placed by you on this paid ser- vant of the company. Mr. Sheffield : Yes, I hold the principal licence at the warehouse from which the off licences are supplied. Mr. Roberts: You have nothing to do personally with the selling of alcohol on these premises. Mr. Sheffield : No. Mr. Roberts : In other words, you simply farm these licences for the company. Mr. Sheffield : I hold them for the company and visit the branches from time to time.</p>
        <p>7-- After a consultation in private the magistrates refused the application.</p>
        <p>- TRANSFERS GRANTED. The following transfers were granted :—Soldier's Return, from Alban Stirrup to Ellen Stirrup 014 Ball from Peter White (deceased) to Fanny White; off licence, Allen street, from G. A. White to Edward Arthur Bradbury; King's Arms, from E. Plinston (deceased) to Thomas Brown Potter; off licence, Hoyle street from Robert Tickle(deceased)to Elizabeth Tickle. Henry Godwin Williams was granted temporary authority to sell at the Waggon and Horses, in lien of Thomas Massey, deceased.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL PAlLURES.—ACcording,to lierap's Mer- cantile Gazette, the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, Feb. 3rd, was 190, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 97 ; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 93. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 89 ; Deeds of Arrangements; 89—Total 178 ; being an increase of 12. The total number of Commercial Failures recorded during the 5 weeks of the present year is 955 ; the total number re- corded in the corresponding 5 weeks of last year was 1 34 showing an increase of 31,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>in Pack •ts</head>
