      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>FRENZIED FINANCE,</head>
        <p>FRENZIED FINANCE,</p>
        <p>Mc. H. Wurtn_or] It was in April 1004 that Ms. Lawson gave the first notice of his intentions with regard to Standard Oil. He said, "1 shall be busy for the next few weeks, months,or years, trying to induce the Standard Oil and the City Bank to return to amalgamated stockholders and original subscribers their 60,000,000d015. and `26,000,000d0ls." Immense sums of money are at stake and depending largely for their safety upon Lawson 's next move. chav,rms and names insurance societies with enter- ing largely into speculation, and puts the law on this matter before the public. Insurance is a great business and enters largely into the life and well- being of the people generally, consequently there is a vast amount of anxiety felt as to the outcome of Mr. Lawson" work. People say he is mad. If he is there is method in his madness, for he says, "If I am killed, or made away with in any fashion, this work will still go on. The story is completed and in the publishers: hands," His method is to keep the whole business as rnlch before the public as possible. To do this he has selected a cheap magazine as his medium and makes use of the daily press for full sheet advertisements. Since writing nay last article, another full sheet announceraent has appeared in which, addressing the Wall-street Frenzied Financiers, he says : " The valued structure which you have been erecting by fraud and chicanery during the past twenty years totters and sways, like a juggler's tent set upon the mountain top, with every breeze of truth. The results of my two previous adver- tisements, that contained only wisps of facts and myl intention to publish a third one, prove the instability of your card house." The peculiar thing about it is that Lawson remains in the business, but as an investor merely. He draws a sharp distinction between the manipulator, the speculator, and the investor. To those of us who are looking forward to the day when Socialism as a working and guiding principle will be the only accepted method W iereby the people can possibly five a happy and simple life, Wall-street, in all its phases, is but concentrated</p>
        <p>;elfishness.' It is not possible for any ordinary man, simply and honestly working his way through the world, to understand how Insurance, Bankincf, Railroading, Electricity, Coal and Gold Mining, and all the other industries into which large amounts of capital enter should so come under the control of one company. The Standard Oil—as to enable some half-dozen men to have and to hold, and to manipulate the investments and savings of the working men of a mighty nation. The Rev. John Yonge in his Peace Sunday sermon asks one or two pertirent questions : Whence came the bitterness, .the antagonisms, the jealousies, the hatreds, the strikes and lock-outs, the endless con- troversies between capital and labour? Did they come out of righteousness, of truth, of judgment, or of some stern, resentless, inevital4le Fate ? We knew per- fectly well that such was not their origin. They came of unequal and equitable relations, of the un- happy working cf unjust laws, of selfish and often , cruel disregard of the needs and rights of men, of ignoble and un-Christian greed of gain, of every man fighting for his own hand regardless of the claims and necessities of his fellows. Was wealth in the right, when it took advantage of the helplessness of poverty to exactlabour at even less than the barest living wage?" All the bitterness and strife comes of the fact that a very few men want to own the earth. It comes of the fact that workingmen will not get and keep them- selves in fit and proper condition, physically and mentally, to enable them to grapple with the evils of their environments. It comes again from the fact that the biggest scoundrels walking the earth, the Morgans, Carnegies, Rockefeller and so on, and raised above public opinion, England's Primate, the Arch- bishop of Canterbury comes to America, Morgan meets him, entertains him, and carries him about the country on a special Pullman. A crowd of the Bishops and other great shining lights of the church wish to come to California. Morgan orders his special out and the servants of the Christ ride in all the luxury gold can buy. Is Mr. Yonge surprised at the attitude work- ingmen are taking on the question of the day? Mr. Lawson tells a little story bearing upon this point. One day while on his way to a meeting of the "system," he stopped to exchange a word with Mr. and Mrs. Russell Sage, and for an instant, as I left them, my wandering tboughts took a new twist, for Mrs. Sage had informed me that Father and I are on the way to prayer meeting' early evening prayer meeting in New York ! For an instant I was in one of those tiny New Hampshire villages, a forgotten haven of rest and simplicity, innocent as yet of steam, machinery, or trolleys, for the sweet lady, and the angular man with the pained gait which spoke in loud tones of the unbroken store-shoe could belong in no other than a rural place. But the New Hampshire village flitted out, for my truant senses seized on a message over memory's telephone: Russell Sage has 100,000,000. One hundred million dollars, and I was back on earth again, but as I walked, the thought was buzzing in my brain Is it possible that my countryman has made one hundred million dollars, when the expert carpenter who started at the birth of Christ to trudge the world until from his honest labours he had accumulated 100,000,000 by. laying aside each day the greatest amount he was entitled to one dollar, had at the end of 1,900 years only more than half that sum ?" Let us enter one of the world's business palaces and watch the men who are buiiding up a great nation's financial structure. Taken from the "New York Journal," March 17, 1899,—" The frenzied brokers fought like madmen around the sugar post. The wildest sort of excitement prevailed throughout the day. The rest of the floor was practically abandoned, and brokers crowded, pushed. elbowed, and yelled frantically in their efforts to fill orders. There was no warning. The sudden jump of the stock almost threw brokers into a panic. Men became ferocious to fill orders. Those on the outside made wild rushes to get into the whirpool, men who are usually calm fell over each other in their excitement. Scores of arms whipped the air, and men yelled themselves hoarse. So great was the din and so compact the yelling crowd that those on one side of the post did not know the bidding on the other. At one point sugar was going at 159, and five feet away it was bringing 161. While almost at arm's length farther away it was going at 160, and farther around the post at 162. One minute the stock was away up, and the next it seemed to fall hopelessly. Then it would as suddenly soar up again. It reached 170, and in five minutes it was down to 150." This is stockbroking, or in plain English, it is gambling with the very life's blood of the people. And yet the question' is asked.—" Whence came the bitterness, the antagonisms, the jealousies, the hatreds, the strikes, and the lock-outs, the endless controversies be- tween capital and labour ? " To my mind this is not the worst feature of all this mad rush to get rich quick. This madness permeates through all ranks and sections of the social body. What do Lancashire factory workers think of the following? Towards the latter end of last December the prices of cottorr declined very much, and the planters and dealers determined to do something to force an advance. There had been a vast crop. Nature and the planters had been too successful in their efforts to make the earth give forth of her fulness. Big bonfires were started all over the State of Georgia. Bale after bale of cotton was thrown on, the fires were kept blazing until 2,000,000 had gone up in smoke. Much ceremony was observed at the first fire at Fort Gaines. ' Farmers came from all over the State and joined the merchants in the rites. More than 300 bales were rolled in the Court House Square A procession was formed and marched around the bonfire. Horns were blown, bells were rung and the populace cheered as the torch was applied. That's frenzied finance in another form. Now let us look at it still another way :—Chicago, December 6.—Out of 139 decoy prescriptions sent out by the State Board of Pharmacy to Chicago druggists to be filled, twenty-three contained no trace of the drug called for, sixty-five were SO per cent. impure, ten 20 per cent. impure, and only thirty-one were pure. I think this is almost the worst yet. It is selfish- ness, brutality, conspiracy and murder all in one. The druggist who will, knowingly and deliberately trifle for sordid gain with the physician's prescription is a cold blooded murderer. There needs no close scrutinity of the papers to find all these abominations. He who runs may read of them any day. I must close for the present with the following, and shall certainly make neremarks touching what must speak for itself. "Some time ago a child of one and a half years was brought to the New York Infirmary for treatment." said Dr. Daniel. " After some days the child's mother came for her and took her away. At that time the mother said that she needed the child's ser- vices in following her trade of passementerie making in her tenement home. She said the child's ser- vices were worth 50 cents a week to her. The little one rolled tiny balls in paste, which the mother attached to form a variety of passementerie for millinery and trimmings. She said that if the baby</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>did not help her she would have to do that detail of the work herself, and, of course, could earn but pro- portionately less."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>H. WHITH.I.II. January 14th, 1005. 775-11th Street, San Franciso, California. THE UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>H. WHITH.I.II. January 14th, 1005. 775-11th Street, San Franciso, California. THE UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>RELIEF MEASURES TO BE</p>
        <p>CONSIDERED BY A WARRINGTON COMMITTEE, The Feeding of School Children.</p>
        <p>The unemployed question continues to force itself upou public attention, and several resolutions dealing with the state of affairs locally were introduced at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening. 1 Councillor SKELLAND first moved :—" That a com- mittee be appointed to consider what steps can be taken for feeding poor children during the depression of I trade." He said that considering every child above five 1 years of age was compelled by law to attend an element tary school daily unless prevented by sickness, and that the power for enforcing such attendance was vested in the local authority, it was in his opinion the duty of the authority to assure itself that every child was provided with food before commencing the day's duties. He respectfully suggested that in view of the fact that some Warrington children were at times sent to school without breakfast, the Council should lend i 1 ts aid to provide food during periods of local distress for the children under its control. His house was often yisited by children and women begging for bread for little ones who, but for the assistance rendered by the Ragged school mission, would have gone hungry to school. Many hundreds of poor children had been fed clothed, and taught to be sober, honest, industrious and clean' and they were now respectable citizens. Many were blamed for having wasted their money on drink, but some were more to be pitied than blamed when 1 temptations were pat in their .vay. It was the duty of councillors and magistrates to make it easy to do right and hard to do wrong. He did not propose that the expense of providing the meals should come out of the rates. He would suggest that an official subscription list should be opened and he believed there would be a generous response. (Hear, hear). Councillor TINNION seconded. He thought it was a matter that should have the earnest consideration of the Council. He admitted he did not see a way out of the difficulty at present. He did not see how they could make use of Corporation funds for the purpose. He had always had the welfare of the children of the town par- ticulaely at heart, and if anything could be done to make their lot happier and brighter it would have his support. Conncillor PEMBERTON supported. There was not the slightest doubt that there was distress in the town at present. While the law provided compulsory education for children it did not make any provision to feed them. There were many hungry men and women in the town who were not drunkards. If the Council did not devote rates to the purpose they could, at any rate, open a sub- scription list. The resolution was carried unanimously. Councillor STOTT then brought forward the following motion " That in order to provide work for unemployed Warrington Citizens this Council instructs the respon- sible Committees to take in hand without delay all the work possible, such as the more frequent cileansing of back passages, the paving of streets and hack passages, the repair of roads and footpaths, the demolition of buildings, the laying out of open spaces and parks, and the planting of trees in suitable streets, &amp;c." The MAYOR thought the proposal would be unani- mously approved. Councillor SToTT : Will it be carried out ?</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Yes. Councillor A. PEACOCK seconded. Councillor TINNION said that while he was in favour of the spirit of the resolution he did not want his approval to involve supporting a vote of censure on himself. The committee were going on with the work of paving &amp;c. as quickly as possible. The delay was due to the fact that they had not obtained the sanction of the Local Government Board to the spending of the money. They would not send an inspector to hold an inquiry so some of the work was at a standstill. Councillor STorr said it was a disgraceful condition of things that the Council as representing the electorate should have to be dictated to by one or two men in London. It all arose because the working classes eSpecially had used their votes in the wrong direction. (Laughter.) It would not be money badly spent for the Corporation to appoint a temporary official to take charge of the quantities and specifications for the making of streets and back passages. The work could then be done more speedily. The MAYOR : We have got permanent officials to do the work.</p>
        <p>Councillor S-rorr : It has been given as an excuse that there was so much wofk attached to getting out specifications, Sm., that the Committee could not proceed more quickly. The resolution was unanimously agreed to. Councillor STorr then proposed :—" That in order to provide a means of ascertaining how many are unem- ployed in Warrington, an office be opened where men requiring work may register their names, and provide other necessary information." The MAYOR: You can't employ publics money for that purpose. Councillor STorr said it had been stated that in War- rington there were not many unemployed, and the dis- tress was not acute. The only way they could ascertain the extent of unemployment was by opening a bureau.lt was surprising that they could not spend money for the purpose in Warrington when very many county boroughs had opened similar offices during the past month. The 3Ltvou. said the money would have to be forth- coming by private subscription. The rates could not be used for the purpose. Councillor STorr said he simply wanted the Council to agree with the principle. Afterwards they could find ways and means of obtaining the money. Councillor ASHCROFT asked if Councillor Stott could mention a town in Lancashire where a bureau had not been a failure. In some towns the expense involved had been over £2OO and work had been found for only very few men. Recently a women's bureau was opened in Warrington. Over 300 applicants registered their names, but only three were provided with situations. Alderman BENNETT said a municipality was well within its moral rights in trying to see that its citizens should get work. The Council had readily discussed matters of infinitely less importance. Nine-tenths of the time of the meeting had been spent in mere puerilities and vuglar arguments about personal matters which were hardly worth considering. The matter now introduced affected the happiness and comfort of hundreds of men in Warrington and the Council was dismissing it in a perfunctory fashion, in fact almost refusing to deal with it at all. The Council he considered should apply in their next Bill for Parliamentary powers to spend money on the establishment of a bureau. Councillor TINNION said the Gymnasium Committee would be willing to provide a room and members of the Corporation would no doubt be willing to act as a com- mittee to look after the work of registration. It was quite possible that something could be evolved to help men who were out of work.</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT said the Town Clerk had to furnish returns to the Board of Trade as to the number employed in Warrington. It was uttelly impossible to get the smallest idea of the extent of unemployment from trade union officials. The great majority of workingmen had not the common sense to organize. The MAYOR: They have got too much common-sense sometimes. It is commonsense at times not to be led by agitators. Councillor STOTT said other re"istery offices had been failures because they had depended upon employers of labour making use of them. If they depended upon the employers the bureau would fail, because if an employer wanted a workman he had only to make it known, and he would have fifty applicants the next morning. He wanted a bureau to be opened in Warrington just to ascertain how many wanted work. The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>Councillor SfOTT then called attention to the follow- ing motion of which he had given notice :—" That if no other method is available a sufficient sum of money be granted to His Worship the Mayor for the purpose of providing the necessary expense required for the pro- vision of such office." He asked the Town Clerk whether there was any money which could be used for that pur- pose. The Tom: CLERK said the Council had not the power to spend money in that way. Councillor STOTT : Things are out of order in War- rington, but they are in order in other towns. The MAYOR: You cannot grant money to the Mayor for this purpose. Councillor STOTT : Can't we make a grant to the Mayor and let him do as he likes with it? The MAvoR : No. All money collected, from the ratepayers is to be accounted for and audited accordingly. Councillor Srorr asked if the Council had power to make any grant to the Mayor. The TOWN CLERK They have power to grant the Mayor a salaly, Councillor Szorr : Then can't we give the Mayor a salary? The Marort.: I will see that you don't. I won't be a paid servant. Councillor S ran. : You know the restrictions placed on us by the Local Government Board. When things are desperate we ought to take deperate measures. The MAYOR pointed out that no grant of salary had ever been made to a Mayor in Warrington. Councillor Sroi-r : You mean to say it is not customary. It is not a question of order. The MA.vou: The rule in Warrington has been that when you wanted anything from the Mayor you came and asked him and got it. Conncillor : That is one of the drawbacks of the office.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: It is one of the pleasures if you only knew it. (Laughter.) Councillor Srorr It makes it impossible for anyone who is not a wealthy man to accept the Mayoralty. Councillor TINNION : It has been done on the cheap for years. (Loud laughter.) I fail to see where the expense of a bureau would come in. The resolution was ruled out of order and withdrawn</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 11, 1905</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 11, 1905</p>
        <p>It is a great principle. (" Chair, chair.") The MAYOR: Will you allow the business to go on? Councillor PEMBERTON : I never felt so strongly on a matter in my life. (Further cries of "Chair, chair.") The resolution was properly proposed, seconded, and carried. The MAYOR: It is no use arguing. If you are deter- mined to go on, the meeting must close. CORRGiIIOT PEMBERTON: Then it must close. I feel very strongly on this matter. It is highly improper that any resolution— The MAYOR: It is no use arguing. Now that you have expressed your opinion will you sit down? Councillor PEMBERTON: It is highly improper and unconstitutional that any resolution passed cannot be discussed. The Standing Order says the chairman has the right to erase any minute he likes. I say it is abominable— The Mayon.: There is no minute book. You are all talking twaddle. Councillor PEMBERTON: If it is not on the written minutes it is one of the greatest jobs ever perpetrated in the Town Hall. It is a most serious thing that with regard to a minute proposed and seconded at a meeting of the General Purposes Committee there should be the power to stop an onen discussion. The MAYOR : You don't know what you are talking about. Councillor Pemberton : I know very well what lam talking about. I think if this was honest and straight- forward there would be no objection. At this point there were further cries of " Chair, chair," and Councillor Pemberton observed that the cries all came from the Aldermen. (Laughter). Councillor Pemberton added : This is a most serious business. That is not apersonal matter with me. It is serious that free discussion should be stifled. The MAYOR: If you don't sit down I shall— Councillor PEMBERTON : I propose that the minutes be referred back. Councillor MAGIVN : That is seconding my amendment The MAYOR: You can refer them all hack as far as. I am concerned. Councillor LORD : I shall support the amendment. Councillor MAGINN : That is my amendment. The MAYOR: You are jeopardizing the grant for the school in Orford-lane. (Cries of " No, no.") Councillor LORD : That is all a cock and bull story. Seven voted for the amendment and 12 against it. The MAYOR : The amendment is lost. The minntes were then confirmed in their entire ty and Councillor Maginn left the meeting.</p>
        <p>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE. [By Godfrey Dean.] The Philosophy of Phyllis. Though Phyllis's nature is so subtle, her tempera- ment so changing, I had believed—until yesterday— that with most of her moods I was acquainted. She had been trifling with philosophy. Why,l do I not know, except that she is Phyllis. It is sufficient ' for most of her sex that they should be their own I charming selves without disturbing the serenity of their surroundings with questions fitter for the I scholarly and scientific mind. I say "trifling " in the light of my conclusions, for at first her attitude deceived me. I found her abstracted. She was seated in a huge chair, before a glowing fire, with an open book in her lap, her shapely chin resting on the palm of her hand—lucky hand !—and her elbow supported by the chair arm, what time she looked steadfastly into the fire. Apparently she had not heard the knock that pre- ceded my entry. " I have come," I said, sententiously. She simulated surprise. "Is my call unexpected, then ?" "You did not ask my permission." If I had, Phyllis would have deferred it for a day ; acquaintance with her is a liberal education—chiefly with regard to herself and then of her sex. I repressed the gratified smile of knowledge. " Don't disturb the picture." She rebuked me with her eyes. Phyllis pretends not to like compliments—be they ever so delicate— but that wasn't a compliment. And I said so. " I am serious," she said. "So am I." "I have been reading this book," she said, "and one character in it is very philosophical. He is not the hero." "That, by the way," I remarked. " Do be serious.'" "I am—profoundly." " knd I am afraid," she continued, "he has ' infected me with his own spirit." I got a closer sight of the book, which I recognized as having read. I wondered what philosophy Phyllis's pretty head could contain. And, if any, why? It wasn't at all necesary. "Why bother with philosophy," I said. Phyllis's look told me it was an indiscretion, and I repented at once. "What is philosophy ? " she asked. I made a mental resolve to see how much phil- osophy there was in Phyllis. Philosophy is truth," I said, and gave her an audacious illustration. " The pretty girl is always on the other side of the street." ' She glanced towards the window. " This is a square," I said ; " besides, I spoke in metaphor." "Always ? " ' courage nearly failed me. I compromised. " Not always. My experience has been the excep- tion—perhaps--that proves the rule. Or else--" " What ? ' " Or else she does not remain there." Phyllis raised her head from the back of the chair, questioningly. "On the other side. I have crossed over to the other side." Her head sank back.. " To secure pearls one must dive deep." I believe,this time, Phyllis did not take me literally; though, as she did not speak,l cannot be sure. At least I have secured my pearl, but not yet brought it to the surface. When I achieve that feat I may be able—in a clearer light—to appreciate all its value. My thought, however, remained unspoken. There was a perceptible pause. "If people reasoned," I said at length, " there would be fewer marriages." " I agree." This was simply courting misforture. But Phyllis gave no sign as yet that she was vexed. I ventured further. "No, love does not want reason—and rightly," " Then you think love is unreasoning ?" I was on dangerous ground, and this looked peril- ously like a trap. " On the contrary," I hastened to say, at the risk of consistency, "it is reason—the reason—itself." My defence appearing sound, she relapsed into her former mood. "All the same," I resumed, " man is a strange mixture of sentimentality and animality." " Is he ?"—non-committally. " For instance, in this book (touching it) the hero is in one chapter making love in the tenderest man- ner, and in the next fighting like a Berserk." I "Yes."</p>
        <p>" But it isn't surprising. In fact, it is what one might expect. The qualities in conjunction are natural." Her brow puckered in thought. Philosophy was the last thing one expected from Phyllis. But I dared not smile. What is philosophy?" she asked again suddenly. "What a woman doesn't like." "And that?" "Oh, it applies to many things." "Define philosophy. I'm willing to learn." "Abstract truth, it should be." She seemed puzzled, and sighed over it. "If you'll give me an example I shall understand better." " Well"—I hesitated, was tempted and fell—" as an example of abstract truth. I think what I said answers, That the pretty girl is always on the other side of the street.' " "That's nasty and cynical," she said. "And you're simply horrid to-night." There was a little catch in her voice which stabbed me more than her words. And so this was Phyllis' philosophy ! But what else could I expect ? Phyllis was not meant for philosophy, nor philosophy for Phyllis. A woman is greater than all philosophy, for in five minutes she can pulverise all that a man may gather in a lifetime. Certainly philosophy would not enable me to make I my peace with Phyllis. Her face was turned from me, but I guessed her lip was quivering. I took the book—thanks to philo- sophy, at all events, for the opportunity it afforded of making up a quarrel—from beneath her unresisting hand and cast it away.</p>
        <p>The action coaxed a, smile to Phyllis's lips. "Philosophy is a delusion and a snare." I said. Philosophy is woman, the eternal truth, and I am the eternal lover. (To be continued.) (These articles originally appeared in the "London Echo.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>GEORGE HOTEL LICENCE. SCENE IN THE COUNCIL CHAMBER. Councillor Maginn and the Mayor.</head>
        <p>THE GEORGE HOTEL LICENCE. SCENE IN THE COUNCIL CHAMBER. Councillor Maginn and the Mayor.</p>
        <p>"MUNICIPAL DODGING."</p>
        <p>There was a stormy scene at Tuesday evening's meet- ing of the Warrington Town Councilwith respect to the vexed question of the George Hotel licence. The Mayor formally moved the adoption of the minutes of the General Purposes Committee in which no reference to a discussion on the licence at the last committee meeting appeared. Alderman BEN-NETT seconded.</p>
        <p>- Councillor MAGINN : I move the following amend- ment:—"That the minutes of the Committee be not con- firmed, but be sent back for further consideration and completion." The NI tYOR : That is only a negative vote. All you have to do is to say yes "or"no " to the minutes. Councillor MAG[xs : I suppose lam at liberty to give an explanation why I move the amendment. The MAYOR: If you don't like the minutes you can vote that they be referred back. It is not an amend- ment. You can speak with the minutes or if you don't like them you can vote against them. Councillor MAGrs); : Then I vote and speak against them. (Councillor PEMBF:RTON : Hear, hear.) On Monday, January 30th, a meet;ng of the General Pur- poses Committee was held, and amongst the matters on the agenda was that of the licence of the George Hotel. It is public property to-day that the matter was dis- cussed in this chamber and most emphatically discussed, Perhaps notes nicely or as well as some would have liked.</p>
        <p>The MaYou : You cannot discuss the matter now. It is not on the minutes. _</p>
        <p>- ---. Councillor MAGINN : lam going to explain why it is not on the minutes. ___</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: We don't want any explanation. We all know it. Councillor .MAGINN : This was voted on for and against, and carried. I proposed an amendment and it was lost, and yet, though the matter was debated for some considerable time, for some unknown reason to me it is not printed and it is not on the agenda paper. I know it is understood that even if a notice is not— The MAYOR: You talk to what is on the minutes. If you don't like what is on them you can send them back and have them altered or give notice of motion for the next meeting. I should like you to understand that matters of law, where value is concerned, loss or other- wise, are in the Town Clerk's hands, and they never come before the Council for discussion. You don't pay 700 !a year to a man who does not know what. the law is. Councillor MaGINN: It is not in accordance with the Standing Orders. It is not in the hands of the Town Clerk to say what shall be printed, but the chair- man of the committee, and you are the chairman, Mr. Mayor. I beg leave to tell you and this Council that it is for a selfish reason the matter has been left off the agenda. The MAYOR: I must rule you out of order; and I must ask the Council to support me. Councillor MAG INN : I have 120 doubt you will rule me out of order—(cries of "Chair")—but I wish it to be distinctly understood that this may be considered clever municipal management, but I could give it a better name—municipal dodging. (Hear, hear.) The M AYOR : Hadn't you better take the chair? Councillor MIGINN: If I want thechair I won't take it from you; and if I ever do I hope I shall conduct the business better and more honourably— The MAYOR: Now then; you must obey my ruling if von won't take the chair. Oouncillor MAGINN : I will obey your ruling. The M.avort: And I rule you out of order ; that's enough. Councillor MAGINN : I appeal to the Town Clerk for his ruling. The TOWN CLERK: I never rule, I only advise; and I say that the Mayor is quite in order. Councillor MAGENN: Why did you suggest that I should leave the matter to the March meeting, when it would be too late? Why is the minute put back a month?</p>
        <p>Councillor Tr:vmoN rose to a point of order. Councillor INlAonrs : I refuse to be a party to carry- ing the business of the Council any further to-night,and I leave the room as a protest against the ridiculous conditions carried on in the General Purposes Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Councillor Maginn rcse as if to carry out his threat, but on hearing the debate continued resumed his seat. Councillor Tnrslos : I don't wish to appear antagon- istic to any ruling of the chair, but as a matter of principle I must ask on what ground you have decided that Councillor Maginn is out of order? Ido so with all due respect, so that there shall be no misunderstand- ing in future. (Hear, hear.) Recently I presided over a sub-committee which had been considering the Standing Orders, and I don't understand the Town Clerk's ruling to-night. I claim that when the matter was discussed by the sub-committee that— The MAYOR : I don't want to rule you out of order but we are discussing the minutes of the General Pur- poses Committee. Councillor TINNtoN : I do this ma matter of principle. I am sorry to come into conflict with the Mayor, but it is very important that we should have an under- standing about the matter. The M &amp;TOR : We have these minutes before us.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION : I will deal with the matter as shortly as I can, but you Mr. Mayor, have already ruled Co mcillor Magmn out of order. I contend— The MAYOR: lam in the chair, and I am supposed to know what is in order and what is not. lam not here for you to lecture me, and tell me what I ought to do. .</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION : lam not attempting it. The MAYOR: You know the position of a chairman ? Councillor TENMON : I do. The MAYOR: I don't think I ought to argue with you. Councillor TINNION : I think we ought to have an explanation. It is better for it to come now than to have it out later. Councillor TINNION : I don't wish any misunder- standing to arise, but this is an important matter. The Town Clerk ruled the other day, or said when we were amending Standing Order No. 9, that we had a perfect right to discuss any matter that was brought forward by any committee in which a resolution was passed in the committee whether it was on the agendalpaper or not If you rule, and have already ruled,Councillor Maginn out of order' because the matter was not opportune to be discussed, I agree with you ; but I must protest if you have ruled that he is out of order because the matter is not on the agenda paper. Under what head does the Town Clerk rule? The TOWN CLERK : I repeat the advice I gave you. It is perfectly right for a member to discuss anything on the printed agenda paper or which appears on the minutes in the minute book. Councillor EVANS: Shall Ibe in order if I move the Standing Order of the Council be— The MAYOR: No, you must give notice. Councillor EVANS : That is another technical point on which some of us have been ruled out of order more than once. The MAYOR : You are not treating me fairly. You put me in the chair to conduct the business of the meet- ing, and you won't let me conduct it. Councillor EVANS I should be sorry to feel I was doing anything uniust to you. The MAYOR: You are not adding to my dignity in the position I occupy by squabbling over whether what I say is right or wrong. Councillor EVANS : Councillor Tinnion and myself are trying to uphold the honour and dignity of your position, and I think we are doing it in the best sense. I have had a great deal to say about Standing Order No. 9, and on one particular and important occasion I was ruled out of order. I put the interpretation on it that the Town Clerk has put on it. The MAYOR : This is all irregular. Councillor EVANS : And the decision we came to the other night was that if there was a minute discussed embodying a resolution and the minute was discussed in any committee— The MAYOR : He is out of order, but it seems you won't obey the chair. You want to conduct a meeting of your own. Councillor EVANS : I shall certainly give notice of motion about the matter. Councillor Pemberton then rose to speak. The MAYOR: Are you going to talk to the minutes? Councillor PgmnErgroN : lam going to talk to the minutes of the committee which were moved, seconded, and passed. The Mayon : Do you know what minute was passed. Councillor PEMBERTON : Yes. The MAYOR : That the matter be left in the hands of the Town Clerk. Suppose there is a piece of pro- perty to he purchased, or something in which the Cor- poration has to get a bargain, you don't go about with your mouth open all over the town, do you? Councillor MAGINN : This has been discussed three months, and is therefore public property. Councillor PEMBERTON : I want to stop improper bargaining. I think I have ELS much right, as represent- ing a certain section' of the ratepayers to know what is going on in regard to property as anyone sitting here. The MAYOR: When the matter comes on the agenda paper for confirmation is your time to object : Councillor PEMBERTON : The time to object is now, when you are passing the matter, The MAYOR : lam in the chair, and I rule you out of order, and this latter is not on for discussion; and I am not adding to the dignity of the chair by arguing with you. Councillor PEMBERTON : Perhaps that is so. The M.s.yort : Obey my ruling and have done with it lam not going to sit here all night, I have been hero long enough. (Cries of " Chair, chair."). Councillor PEMBERTON : I appeal to the Council whether the minute was passed or not. (Renewed cries. The MAYOR: I have ruled von out of order, and sit down. You know what the order of debate is. Councillor PEMBERTON rose amid cries of "Chair, chair." and said: I am very sorry, but this is a great principle—a great principle indeed. ("Chair, chair.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>—Rugby Football. (By " Ovalist.",</p>
        <p>Luck has certainly bean on the side of Warrington in this season's engagements with Runcorn, for they have triumphed by the narrow margin of a goal-kick on each occasion. There is some compensation here for the re- verses Warrington have sustained by failure to land a goal —as at Oldham and Bradford—but it is hard on the poor Linnets. 111-luck has relentlessly dogged them. throughout the season, and apparently nothing can save them from accompanying Batley in a descent to the second division.</p>
        <p>I am genuinely sorry for Runcorn in their adversity. They have always been a team playing football above the average, and for twenty years at least have they held an honoured position among the Rugby clubs of the North. But for the death of J. Richardson, the retirement of Price, and the illness of Houghton and Jolley they would probably have succeeded in keeping their heads above water. Fate has, however,been too strong for them. Possibly a spell in the second division may be a blessing in disguise. Expense will be less, and 1 with the promising juniors that the club possesses it should not be difficult to secure honours in seclnd- class company. And honours there are better than humiliation among the seniors.</p>
        <p>The game at Irwell-lane on Saturday was remark- able for the determination of the Linnets. They made a desparate effort to get points out of Warrington and were very near to accomplishing the feat—and a feat it would have been considering the strength of the visiting fifteen. Warrington weie practically at full strength, while the home team's ranks were further weakened by the defection of Butterworth, who was chosen to play wing and refused to turn out in any other position but centre. The disloyalty of " Jimmy " probably cost his side the match, for had he been marking Fish, it is hardly likely " the Irrepressible " would have obtained the winning point. There is consequently much soreness among the Linnets and their supporters.</p>
        <p>Runcorn were able to score first. By the activity of their forwards and a judicious nse of the peculiar slope they established a position near the line. Jolley got possession and made a determined attempt to cross.' He was promptly upset, but while on the ground he threw back to oue of his forwards. who scrambled over his prostrate comrade and scored. The try was the result of pure deterinialtion, and in missing the goal by only a few inches Jolley must be considered unlucky. However, the misfortune was not all one way.</p>
        <p>Warrington should have got ahead within five minutes, for Hockenhall and Isherwood quite beat the home defence and paved the way for a ay which Fish never got. The centre held tue ball too long and enabled Houghton to make a fine tackle of the wing man. An earlier pass would have enabled Fish to sprint right away and round the posts. In addition to the lost try, Warrington lost Hockenhall. The half back, in parting to Isherwood was bowled over, and falling unguardedly on his right shoulder he broke his collar-bone. This weakened Warrington's forces considerably,and the odds certainly seemed on the side of the home lot. At half-time Runcorn were leading by a try to nil.</p>
        <p>On turning round, Warrington had the avantage both of the slope and the breeze, and they very soon made it clear that they were far from being beaten. The seven forwards played grandly and very soon forced Runcorn to defend desperately. Fine kicking, at which the Linn3ts excelled, kept Warrington out for some time. Once, Fish got away and kicked past Houghton. A try seemed at his mercy when Flannagan came across from the opposte wing and sent the ball out of the ground, not a second in front of the Warringtoir man. The Linnets again breathed freely but they were destined to " die." The ball again came out on Fish's side, and the clever lef t- winger picked up wonderfully, dodged Harrison and Houghton, and scored a brilliant try. A goal was wanted to win, and Preston absent ! Hallam pre- pared to take the kick, when Fish suddenly decided to try his luck again, and the captain gladly gave way. The angle was difficult, but Fish sent the ball sailing through the middle of the goal amid the frantic cheers of the excited Warringtonians. Runcorn made great efforts towards the close, but were always kept in hand and Warrington ran out winners by 5 points to 3.</p>
        <p>The game could not be called a very good one. There was too much keeness displayed for open football to be indulged in, and a man no sooner got the ball than he was smothered. Warrington were certainly the stronger team but they have to thank the unequalled skill of Fish for the victory. His try was such an one as only he can obtain. Still, with Hockenhall in his place Warringtoa would probably have had a comfort able margin. They did very well indeed to obtain the lead when playing a man short, and too keep Runcorn generally on the 'defensive. Brooks played a very dashing ,rfame when he took the injured man's place? and Hallam was always reliable. Bate was not at his best on the attack, and suffered in comparison with Jolley. The latter played grandly, and sustained his usual injury. Houghton seemed as good as ever, and both in kicking and tackling was of immense service to his side. The home front ranli also deserve praise, for they were terribly overweightecl, but made up by speed and activity. They are an untiring lot, and at Irwell-lane the Linnets are still capable of extending the best teams in the competition.</p>
        <p>Oldham failed to beat Salford on Saturday, and have consequently lost further ground. Bradford now lead the way, with Broughton Rangers more than a good second. The latter indeed hold the best position with a match in hand. Warrington have risen to fifth place, and should get higher yet.</p>
        <p>The I;eague table up-to-date is as follows —For— —Against-- P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts Pts IBradford 24...17... 5... 2...29 51-220-20 25-115-36 Oldham 24...17... 6... 1...33 44-198...20 16— 88...35 Brou't'n R 23...17... 5... 1...28 51-209...29 18-112...35 Hunslet 24...15... 9... 0...46 28-176...18 27-117-30 Warr'gt'n 23...13... 8... 2...24 32-144...17 19— 91...28 Leeds Wigan 23...13... 9... L.. 22 33-143...25 21-113-2 T Hull K.R 23...12...11... 0-28 30-146-21 32-138-24 Hull 23...10... 9... 4...22 34-146...24 30-138-24 Salford Leigh. 23 9...12... 2...15 27-111...22 33-143...20 Swinton 24... 9...14... 1...14 27-109...30 31-153-19 Hal:fax Wakefield 24... 8...15... 1...29 12— 94-27 35-159-17 St. Helens 23... 8-14... L.. 28 26-131...32 51.-226-17 Widnes Runcorn Batley 23 5...17... 1...20 16— 88...34 38-182-11 To-day's match at Warrington is one of the mo'; important of the series, and it has a considerable bearing on the League Championship. Oldham can- not afford another defeat just now, but they ought to meet with one nevertheless. Warrington were very unfortunate in not gettimi the verdict at Oldham, for they fairly nveriolayed tEeir opponents, and they are very anxious to make amends to-day. It has happened on many occasions that Oldham's visit to Warring- ton has been big with fate. Once I remember them coming to Wilderspool with ten successive victories to their credit, but they went down before the locals. There is no doubt that Warrington ought to win to- day and-once more secure for themselves the honour of possessing the best de ensive record in the League. At present, Oldham have an advantage of three points.</p>
        <p>The home team should be a strong one, though llockenhall's loss is hound to be felt. He played a grand game in the earlier encounter with Oldham. D. Davies is fortunately well enough to fill the vacancy, and Naylor returns to the forwards in place of Harmer. ,The full team should be: Hallam, full back; Fish, Isherwood, Brookes and Kenyon, threequarters ; Davies and Bate, half backs ; Boardman, Thomas, Shugars, Belton, Naylor, Jolley, Swift, and Cook, , forwards.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night the committee of the Oldham F.C. selected the following team to oppose Warring- ton :—R. L. Thomas, full back ; Wilson, Civil, Maclean, and Cash, three-quarter backs Lees and Dixon, half backs; Ferguson, Frater, D. Thomas Vowles, Ellis, Owens, Jardine, Wright, or J. IT. Wilkinson forwards.</p>
        <p>A big effort to gain two points will also be made by the " A " team, and the strong side selected should</p>
        <p>The game itself was not so one-sided as the score of 3-0 would lead one to believe, and had the home side won by the odd goal it was about as much as they deserved. The Alderley forwards were very good, each man keeping his place, and with Tetlow included they must be a very strong quintette. The home backs were not sound in their defence, and had Stockton Heath had their usual forwards doing duty, I fancy the home team would have been defeated. The Stockton Heath forwards were i'ery disjointed, but wh Lt can be expected, when the club is forced to play three strange forwards, and get a substitute to play outside right, who although trying his best, would on form, not be tit for the Stockton Heath second team? The backs played their usual good game' Skelton shap- ing very well, while the other four J. Richardson, L. Faarclough, E. E. Woods and E. Pierpoint, were as , usual very safe, .Fairclough in particular playing a ' brilliant game, and keeping Milne well in hand. The third goal for Alderley might have been saved, but the goalkeeper running out, made the shot into a yorker.</p>
        <p>This week the club play Huyton Ist and 2nd, and the I following teams are chosen to represent Stockton Heath :—First (at home) : R. G. Pierpoint, ,goal ; E. ' E. Woods and E. Pierpoint, backs; L. Fahelough, J. Richardson, and J. G. Hatton, halves; C. H. Bond, S. Baxter, R. Pierpoint,W. Richardson, and R. Fairclough, forwards. Bully-off 2-45. Referee, N. W. Bellamy. Second (at Hnyton) : F. G. Kertland, goal ; E. Fair- dough and J. Oliff, backs; A. Hodgkinson, (captain) H. Smith and C. Mason, halves ; D. Skelton, W.Linnel, W. Sutton, W. Howard, and A. Ferguson, forwards. Train leaves Central 1-50 for Liverpool and leaves Lime-street 2.30.</p>
        <p>Huvton were the first to beat Stockton this season, and although they are sure to bring a good team down, yet I think the home side should win by a goal.</p>
        <p>With reference to the Brooklands ,v. Timperley match, which had such a sensational ending, at a meetm,g held on Wednesday night in Manchester, it was decided to prevent the Timperley centre forward Smith, from taking any further part in the game of hockey. It was ruled out of order, to deal with the play of the Timperley club in general, otherwise the club might have been asked some very nasty questions. Lotus hope that Timperley will now have learnt a lesson, and that we shall have no more of these occurrences in the game of Hockey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>THE INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS TO THE COUNCIL COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>" Covering Up the Faults of the Corporation.</p>
        <p>The concluding stage in the proceedings attendant upon the inquiry into the trading transactions of the Corporation was reached at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening. At the close of the ordinary business the MAYOR moved a voto of thanks to each of the following : Mr. Thomas Birchall, Mr. Limmus Greening, and Mr. Alfred J. Jolley for the velum ble services rendered by him to the Town Council and the ratepayers of Warrington, as one of the Com- missioners appointed by the Town Council to investi- gate the charges of illegal and immoral trading alleged, bylcertain persons calling themselves the Non-Trading Association, to have taken place between members of the Council and the Corporation and officials, and, for his part in preparing the satisfactory report of the find- ings of the Commissioners on such allegations." The Mayor also moved a vote of thanks to Councillors Evans, Wilkinson, and Arthur Peacock, "for the valuable ser- vices rendered by Ithem to the Town Council and the ratepayers of Warrington as the Commissioners appointed by the Town Council to continue the investi- tlon of the charges of illegal and immoral trading alleged, by certain persons calling themselves the Non- Trading Association, to have taken place between mem- bers of the Council and the Corporation and officials, and for the satisfactory report of their findings on such allegations." The motions, four in number, were taken separately, the Mayor remarking at the outset that all the members of the Jouncil knew the trouble the gentle- men named had taken, and bethought they were entitled to their best thanks. He moved the first resolution. Alderman HirrcHiNsoN seconded. Councillor Lon!) : On behalf of myself and others I wish to say that we do not object to you thanking the Commissioners appointed by the Corporation, but I do not think it is either business-like or usual to pro- pose three or four resolutions when one would suffice. No doubt the report of the three first Commissioners would be agreeable to those who had no sympathy with the inquiry, but was it satisfactory to the ratepayers ? For these three gentlemen not only closured the inquiry by takingladvanta,ge of the unavoidable absence of one of the Non-Trading Commissioners,hut declined to re-open and continue the inquiry when asked to do so by the Mayor and Corporation. These gentlemen sent a letter in reply to that request, Insulting to this Corporation and the Non-Trading Commissioners who carried on the inquiry, whose invincible courage and adherence to duty I brought to light the irregularities of the Corporation, I and rendered invaluable service to the town. Though these resoultions are rather belated,you may thank these gentlemen and praise them to your heart's content, if in doing so you do not endeavour to cover up tlie irregu- larities of the Corporation, for it is our duty to prevent such irregularities again occurring. It was these I irregularities which brought on the inquiry, and have made this Corporation unenvhibly known. If these resolutions had no tendency to cover up the faults of the Corporation, I should not have noticed them, but under the conditions I must vote against them. Councillor STorr: Would it be in order to delete part of the motion? The MAYOR: You cannot alter it. These gentle- men have done yeoman service, and surely they ought to be thanked. COURDMOY STOTT : I am anxious to support the thanks but I object to the framing of the motion. Therefore I shall vote against it. The vote of thanks to Mr. Birth:ill was then carriel seven voting against it. The votes of thanks to Mr. Greening and Mr. Jolley were accorded without comment, but not unanimously. The I‘I4YOR moved the resolution respecting Coun- cillors Evans, Wilkinson, and Arthur Peacock. Councillor STOTT inquired why there should be only one resolution for the three gentlemen, while the coun- cillors previously mentioned were thanked separately. The MAYOR: The first three were non-members of Council, and therefore should be thanked individually. The last three were members. Councillor Smorr : I think the commissioners deserve the thanks of the Council. The MA.vort : And still you won't give it. Councillor STOTT : I object to the framing of the motions and very much regret that they had been framed in this way. The MAYOR: What do you object to? Councillor LORD: The word satisfactory. You should have put agreeable. The MAYOR: I don't think it would make any difference. The resolution was passed, six voting against it.</p>
        <p>At Harrogate on Wednesday whilst two men wer e engaged in blasting at the sewage works northern out- fall, one of them, Richard Taylor, a ganger from a Liverpool firm, who have the contract, was literally blown to pieces. The other, named Slinger, was in jured, and two of his fingers have had to be amputate but last night he was reported to be doing well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Annual Congregational Tea Meeting.</head>
        <p>Annual Congregational Tea Meeting.</p>
        <p>K</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>The annual congregational social meeting of St. John's Presbyterian Church was held in the Church schoolroom, John-street, at 6.30 on Thursday. The attendance was :so much greater than that of previous years that the accommodation of the room was taxed to its utmost. After blessing had been asked by the Minister, the Rev. J. L. Milne, 8.D., an enjoyable tea was expeditiously served, the following ladies presiding at the tables, :—Mesdames J. Brown, Butterfield, Ditchfleld, Jowett, Miller, Milne, Smith and Walker, and Miss Hedge. After tea the chair was taken by Mr. J. Dodds, Penketh, who in his opening remarks said he was exceedingly pleased to be there that night, and was glad to see an attendance so much larger than there- was on a previous occasion when he had been with them. He hoped it meant that their church was mak- ing progress. The Presbyterian Church, as they now knew it, was started at the time of the Reformation, and it had grown into a mighty church, embodying, he supposed, a great deal of what had been best in Puritan- ism. The Presbyterian Church was a church of the people, and might be described as a kind of ecclesiastical republic, being a church in the management of which the people had a large share, and it always appealed to him as one which should be supported by the people. He had a great love for their church and he supposed that was because his gmndfather and great grandfather had been Presbyterians. When Scotsmen left home they liked to find their countrymen supporting a church that was associ- ated with their country. He did not know how many of those present were Scotch, but he thought Presby- terianism was quite as much adapted to the English as to the Scotch. In conclusion they had his very best wishes for their church and he would be very glad to assist them in any way he could. Mr. DODDS was unable to remain until the close of the meeting, and, on his leaving, his place in the chair was taken by Mr. J. Watson. Mrs. BrsoLoss was also present and briefly addressed the meeting. He said it was very pleasant to be there that night. and he hoped that sometime in the future these meetings might be held in a more commodious place. They wanted to make St. John's, and every other church, a centre of good and noble thought. The following is the programme of the evening's entertainment. It was arranged by the organist, Mr. J. W. Jewett. who acted most efficiently as accompanist: —lnstrumental selections, Mr. Scott's party; violin solo, "The Broken Melody," Mr. ligerton; song, "Goodbye," Miss Povey ; cornet solo, "Scottish airs," Mr. H. Shaw; "Fairy song," Miss Bather, who, as an encore, gave "The Mill-wheel"; song, "River of Dart," Mr. Warburton; song, "Entreat me not to leave thee,' Miss Woodland: song," A Tar of the King's," Mr. F. , Taylor. This concluded the first part of the entertain- ment after which there was a service of fruit. In the second part the ladies and gentlemen named again favoured with songs, &amp;c. and the concluding item, which was much appreciated, was an amusing sketch by- the members of the choir entitled "A nice quiet morning." The meeting concluded with the usual votes of thanks</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Sunday School Teachers' Meeting. The quarterly meeting of the Warrington and District Sunday School Teachers' Association was held in Holy Trinity schools on Tuesday evening, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton presiding over a large attend- nce. An interesting address on the subject of 'Awards" was delivered by the Hon. the Rev. C. F. Cross, Vicar of Stretton. He dealt with the s&amp;lection of books and adjustment of ptizes and urged that the best reward the teachers could have would be to see the children leading good lives and, in their turn, becoming Sunday school teachers. A discussion followed.</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and published by JO/IN WALKER &amp; CO., at The Examiner General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, and published by them at the same place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Victoria-road WIDNES : 32, George-street, ST. HELENS: 3, lqarket- street, EARLESTOWN; Church-street, RUNCORN, Fel&gt; 11th. 1905.</p>
        <p>t);y. , 1/4 4,4 .;s4l</p>
