      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S TEA PARTY. Presentation to Mr. A. H. Burgess.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S TEA PARTY. Presentation to Mr. A. H. Burgess.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was the scene of pleasant and success- ful gathering on Monday evening when the annual. congregational tea and en*.ertainment in connection with St. Paul's Church took place. Anticipated with keen pleasure, the event fully realized expectations and the members of the committee which had the arrange- ments in hand must be congratulated upon the admir- able way in which they had done their work. In this connection, a special word of praise is due to Mr. H. B. Houghton, who was the hon. secretary. About 350 persons sat down to an excellent tea, lady members of the congregation presiding at the tables. The subsequent entertainment was highly en- joyable, the programme, provided by well known artiste being interesting,and acceptably varied. In the absenc 3 through indisposition of Alderman Sutton, the Vicar° (Rev. IV. Bracecamp) presided. Before the music corn - menced there were several short speeches on the year'. work.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. EL SMETHURST, vicar's warden, presented a financial statement. Referring to the church expenses account, which he considered the most important, they had not quite so much money given to them, but the expenses had been cut down, with the result that although last year they were £9 to the bad, this year they were £4 to the good. (Applause.) With regard to the endowment fund, they had during the year sent to London £2OO in order that the Ecclestical Commis- sioners might meet that sum with an additional £2OO and he hoped the result would be that they would have increased the endowment by a further sum of £l2 in place of the £BO of the old days. In addition to that they had £2l to the good. (Applause.) Certain repairs had been carried out to the church doors, which had been paid for, and they had a sum £1 Ils. in hand. Referring to the penny bank, Mr. Smethurst said it WAR still going, and they had now started one at the mission, and it would not be long before they had 1,006 depositors in connection with the parish. The work of mission was proceeding in a very satisfactory manner, and the finances, thanks to Mr. Bowen's exertions, had greatly benefited. (Applause). The Vrca.lc cordially thanked the parishioners for the kind support which hid been extended to him during the past 12 months. One had a great deal to contend with in a large parish like his, but he had tried to do his level best, and the reason why they had been so suc- cessful was that he had been so well assisted by the con- gregation. No one regretted more than he did Mr. Burgess being called away to Birmingham, but they were fortunate in having rdecured the services of such an excellent man as Mr. John Rigby, who was a worthy successor to Mr. Burgess inthe office of people's warden. (Hear. hear, and applause.) Every branch of work in connection with St. Paul's was in a flourishing con- dition, and in this connection special reference was made to the men's Bible class. The number of members was increasing from month to month. (Applause.) The Vicar also thanked Mr. Smetlwrq for his valuable nscistincr,. The entertainment opened with a well-played selec- tion from "The Country Girl" by the ladies' orchestra consisting of the following:- The Misses V. K. and J. M. Finney, M. Leigh. H. Chesworth, L. Birchall, E. Greenough, Mary Chesworth, and H. Leigh. Miss Eckersley very effectively sang "Angus Macdonald" and "There's a land," while Miss Polly Houghton's sweet voice was heard to great advantage in "Star of the Twilight" and "The Nightingale's trill." Mr. J. Babbitt gave soulful interpretations of "Till death " and "River of Dart" while Mr. Poole acquitted himself with credit in "Oh ! Oh! hear the wild winds blow"and "The Bedouin love song." Great interest centered in the first public appearance of the Warrington Juvenile Harmonic Society under the conductorship of Mr. T. Brough. It was soon evident that the children had been splendidly trained the voices blending with fine effect. Their renderings of "Night bells" "Sister elves " and " Don't forget the old folks " called forth the highest encomiums. The committee are to be con- gratulated upon their choice of a humorist, Mr. J. Macdermott, of Liverpool, who was responsible for the lighter rein delighting the audience. His sketch " Various occupations " was most amusing and created great laughter. Encores were the order of the evening and the entertainment was much prolonged. During an interval a pleasing incident occurred. The VICAR said he had been asked to make a presentation to a friend they all loved and respected. Be alluded to Mr. Burgess. When he left St. Paul's he left his heart behind, and they owed a great deal to him and appreci- ated his valuable work. (Hear, hear.) They hoped to have the pleasure of seeing him at the next tea party. The Vicar then handed to Mr. Burgess a handsome silver cigarette box on the inside of which was the in- scription :--"Presented to A. H. Burgess, Esq., by the Tea Party Committee of St. Paul's, Warrington, as a small recognition of his valuable services as secretary," (Applause.) Mr. BURGESS briefly returned thanks. What he had done for St. Paul's he had done out of love for the dear old church. (Applause.) Although he was separated from it by a certain number of miles, the church and people would never be forgotten by him, and he hoped as long as he lived that he would never be absent on these occasions (Applause.) On Tuesday evening the annual dance was held in the Parr Hall, and passed off most successfully. Mr. Ander- ton's band supplied excellent music, and the duties of M.C.'s were discharged by Messrs. W. T. Flood, A. C, Binks, J. Robinson, J. Houghton, J S. Bate, R. W. Wilson, S. Raney, and W. Thompson.</p>
      </div>
