        <p>WOMAN'S REALM. DRESS-FASHION-HOME. LONG GLOVES AND BANGLES. The re-introduction of elbow sleeves means the wearing of long gloves. Suode in champagne, souris, fawn, and gazelle colours are at present in favour, and very nice they look with velvet, crêpe- de-chine, and panne dresses. Bangles once more become of importance in the daytime, as once again they can be displayed, and are valuable arm- furnishings when a woman is dressed out all in her best, and anxious to look fitted at all points just as she should be. They have never been out of fashion, because there were always the evenings in which to wear them. Now they can do double duty. AN AMERICAN DEBUTANTE. I hear much, says a writer in the Onlooker, of a future American debutante, the daughter of Mrs. Drexel, who was so honoured by the King and Queen last season when at Ascot. She is said to be a very lovely girl, and is now going to Dresden with her mother (who has taken a'house over there for two or three months), for a course of drawing and music lessons preliminary to her "coming out. Mrs. Drexel has very pronounced opinions on the Subject of girls' education, and wishes her daughter, of whom she is very proud, to make the most of her time while in Europe. BLOUSE BODICE. The blouse bodice shewn in our sketch is made up over a fitted and boned lining of silk, the fastenings being effected up the centre of the back. The materials required for its make up are cloth and soie sou pie in two shades of reseda, the cloth being of the lighter shade. The bodice is quite plain, and is overlaid with a yoke, which in front is continued right down to the waist;_ this is formed of the cloth,aud is tabbed at the edges, and</p>
