        <p>rtgrat moment can, or course, °my come witn long practice. Many players may find the stroke lees difficult if they hold the cue rather tightly between the first finger and thumb of the bridge hand, so that the cue as it is slid backward and forward meets with a pretty firm resistance. If juSt at the moment of striking the pressure of the thumb and first finger of the bridge hand be increased the cue will be arrested, and thus prevented from following on after the ball. In diagram 59 the cue ball and first object ball in the closest position are intended- to be two inches apart It Ls, however, quite possible for a first-claw player to get a run-through cannon with the cue ball only an inch and even less from the object ball. DIAGRAM F•N</p>
