        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 1 151.905. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS, Ladles' Admirable Suggestion. This Board held its fortnightly meeting at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. A. R. Norman presiding. There were also present: Messrs. S Woodwood, T. Wright, J. L. Jones, P. Mounfield, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. Stokes, A. G. Guest, J. H. Davies, H. M. Davies, G. Pilling C. E. Linaker, J. Mounfleld, C. E. Linaker, jun., J. Hallows, W. M. Morris, J. Douglas, J. Whitley, 3. Spruce, J. Smith, A. Broome, J. Bolshaw, W. Harrison, J. Ratter H. Eyes G. F'. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), T. Cook and W. R. dePrez (relieving officers) and J. Cunnsh (master.) Mr. T. Riley, relieving officer Jar the Frodsham district, wrote regretting his inablity to attend owing to indisposition. THE POOR LAW CONFERENCE. Mr. Woodward moved that Mr. Antwis and Mr. Mawdsley along with the clerk, should represent the Board at the Central Poor Law Conference to be held in London. In the event of either of the delegates being unable to attend his place should be filled by Mr. J. L Jones. Mr. Stokes seconded. Mr. Morris moved an amendment that only one member and the clerk should attend. He thought that number should be sufficient for the amount of good received. Mr. Spruce seconded. He failed to discover anything to justify so costly a deputation as bad been proposed. The Chairman said he thought they would all agree as to the good of the conference. Unless all the unions sent representatives there could be no conference. It 'had always been the custom to send two members ad well as the clerk. The clerk said it was the rule throughout the country to send two. Mr. Hallows would not like the Board to set a bad example. They ought to send as many representatives as other unions. The 9,:nendment was defeated, only four voting in favour of it.</p>
