        <p>PENKETH. POOR CHILDREN'S TREAT.—Last Friday evening Master Tom Barton's orchestra gave a concert in the Wesleyan Schoolroom before a ]urge audience, the proceeds being in aid of the Poor Children's Summer Camp. The Chairman, Mr. Dodds, pointed out the object of the concert and mentioned that they showed their sympathy with the movement by their very presence. After a few items on the pro- gramme had been given Mr. Dodds called upon Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins to address the meeting. This gentleman is the hon. secretary of the Camp and he stated that poor children, .if brought up to use their faculties in the right direction would be the backbone of the country. The programme was then continued, the items being selections by the Orchestra, pianoforte solo by Miss Blair of Glazebroolt, who was also the accompanist. Songs by Masters Wrigley and W. Fryer of Manchester Cathedral Choir were loudly applauded and received encores. Songs by Master Dennott were also well received. Humorous songs by Master B. McCollum were rendered in excellent style and encored. Two solos on the violin by Master Tom Barton were beauti- fully played and there is no doubt that this talented young musician is becoming quite a favourite with the musical people of the town and district. Mr. Dodds in his closing remarks said all the entertainers deserved great praise and he would notl attempt to pick them out'individually. Together with these he said he must also:include Miss Wood, the prometer of the concert, I who had spared no effort to make it a ;success. The orchestra concluded the meeting by'playmg "God save the King."</p>
